      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>POULTRY FOR PROFIT.</head>
        <p>POULTRY FOR PROFIT.</p>
        <p>A BREED FOR THE BACKYARD.</p>
        <p>One of the few, breeds which retains all its old- time popularity- is the Black Minorca, says "The Roultry. World." Some time ago the white wiriety: somewhat boomed, but never rieppeared -to "'catch on," and to-day it is but rarely seen. This is to be regretted, since the birds are extremely beautiful and are suited to anyone who can allow a free range. With the black it is different, and probably at no time has the breed been so extensively kept as now. The great increase is largely due to the wide circle of admirers the fowl has gained as a " Backyarder's " breed. It is undoubtedly one of the best breeds for this purpose, looking neat and clean in even the most smoky town run. Its presentable appearance has much to do with its popularity, for the backyard poultry-keeper naturally desires a fowl that will always show to advantage. Minorcas readily lend themselves to confinement, and given ordinary treatment will thrive in the most limited quarters. Green- food is particularly necessary to Minorcas if it is desired to preserve the pure white lobe. Although regarded as a laying type of bird, the Minorca is by no means to be despised as a table bird, the chickens are fast growers and reach a suitable weight at an early age. Though a dark-skinned fowl the flesh is tender and of good flavour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>FINE ALL-ROUND QUALITIES.</head>
        <p>FINE ALL-ROUND QUALITIES.</p>
        <p>• It is as a fine, all-round fowl that the Minorca, • has most to recommend it. , It meets the re- -quirement of all classes of poultry-keepers, and will answer splendidly in the limited range of the suburban poultry-keeper or on the free range of the poultry-farmer. In some quarters the Minorca is regarded as rather delicate; maybe this. is so with the highly-bred exhibition stock, but the utility bird is one of the hardiest breeds we have. The eggs are probably the largest laid, and the number produced Lwill compare favour- ably with any breed. It is very often stated that brown or tinted eggs are preferred by con- sumers, but the customer who will prefer a •down tinted eggs to a dozen white of the Minorca will be hard to find. In most breeds strains will be found which lay eggs of various sizes, and the uniformly large-sized egg laid by the Minorca gives it a great advantage. When eggs are sold by weight, as some day they will be, the size of the eggs laid by. these birds will more than equalise the extra 'output of the record-breakers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>GOOD COOP.</head>
        <p>A GOOD COOP.</p>
        <p>There is a great number of different kinds of coop in common use, but the best type for rear- ing in the early part of the year, or in an ex- posed situation later in the season, is that known as the double coop. Convenient measure- raents for such a coop are 4 feet long by 21 inches deep, 18 inches high in front, and 15 inches high at the back, divided into two compartments, one 21 inches long and the other 27 inches. The ends, back, top, and front of the smaller com- partment are made solid, the top, which is made in two parts, and the door in front, being hinged to facilitate cleaning. The partition, and the front of the larger compartments, are fitted with laths of wood 2 inches wide and 2 inches apart, the centre one in each case being made mov- able to form a door. A movable wooden floor is fitted in each section. If made of finch timber the cost should not exceed 3s. 9d. or 4s. Such a seep, placed with its back to wind and rains affords sufficient shelter for the hen and her brood, as well as allowing for a scratching shed for the chickens apart from the hen.— Expert, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LITTLE THINGS THAT COUNT.</head>
        <p>LITTLE THINGS THAT COUNT.</p>
        <p>Grit and Shell.—A plentiful supply of grit and shell-forming material should always be within reach of fowls, as these are most essential for their well-being. Grit constitutes the,hen's teeth, and without it the gizzard cannot per- form its function of grinding up' the food. Cleaning the Food Troughs.—Feeding troughs should be used for giving of soft.food, as by so 'doing the food will be sweeter and more enjoy- able to the birds. The troughs should be kept meet and clean by now and again giving thern a good wash with hot water and soda. Household or Table Scraps.—Save all the scraps from the household table and mix with the morning or evening meal, and this will tend somewhat to lessen the cost of feeding the birds.</p>
        <p>Laying Away from Home.—Poultry-keepers are sometimes troubled with the fowls laying away, and this not only causes annoyance, but a loss of profit. To prevent this, procure some -oil of aniseed and sprinkle a few drops on the nests. This has a peculiar influence on birds, the aroma attracting them, and is in no way injurious.</p>
        <p>Number of Meals per Day.—Fowls never re-</p>
        <p>Eggs, poultry, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHICKEN REARING.—Mt. Wm. Coombes, of the " Pleck Gate Poultry Farm," winner of the let Prize in the Blackburn and District Lay- ing Competition for four months ending the 4th February. 1916. writes:— " I have always made a practice of using the best good, which I am convinced is the cheapest in the end. My pen of four Buff Orpington Pullets, which laid 316 EGGS in the above competition were reared from the shell on ENTWISTLE BROS.' DRY CHICK FOOD AND CHICKEN MEAL, which I consider to be the best and most reliable on the market. The Pen of Birds from which the winners were bred. were also fed on this firm's various lines, and I have no hesitation in recommending them to others." You can get it from your Grocer or Corn Dealer in 311 b. and 71b. bags. Ask for it to-day. Sole manu- facturers: ENTWISTLE BROS.. River-st. Bolton. Agents: Carter &amp; Son, Ltd. Barlow '45; Sons, Warrington: T. Gregory, Earleetown.</p>
        <p>• EGGS.—White Leghorn, White Wyandotte, Norman Hunt's competition laying strain. 4/- sitting. Buff Ornington, Golding's direct, 5/- tin. Best Pen all winners, 10/- sitting. Guaran- teed fertile or replaced.—Bennett. Pelham-road. Retford.</p>
        <p>PUBE-BRED White Leghorns, Barron's 282- egg strain, is. 641. dozen.—Miss Bromley, Rem- ington Hall, Derby.</p>
        <p>ROSE Comb Black Leghorn Eggs, 4s. dozen carriage paid; unlimited greets runs.—J. W. hfoorhouse, Cononley, Keighley.</p>
        <p>WHITE Wyandottes, first prize laying strains; eggs, 6s. per doz.; chicks double; table crosses, '7B. 6d.—Ellis, Holly-hill, Blackwell, Brorasgrove. .</p>
        <p>50 PULLETS, just started laying, 5/6 each, 6 for 30/-, or 12 and unrelated male bird £3, delivered. Our best birds are marked with rings, bearing our name and numbered, so customers can prove for themselves the quality of our laying ..strains. Approval with pleasure. EGGS for sit- ting, 3/6 dozen,_. CHICKS, 8/- dozen. Approval with pleasure. List sent.—Brown's Poultry Farm, Woolton, Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Carter &amp; Son Li</head>
        <p>Carter &amp; Son Li</p>
        <p>pOULTRY CHICK FEED Clover Meal, Fish Meal.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>FOR YOUR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>tarter &amp; Son Id.</head>
        <p>tarter &amp; Son Id.</p>
        <p>BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON. Corn, Flour and Provender Stores, CONN MILLERS.</p>
        <p>quire more than three meals a day, and are often better with two. Birds on a free range will manage well enough on two meals, but those in small, confined runs are no worse if, in addition to the morning and evening feeds, a little of something, either grain or animal food, is given as a noonday feed. Chicks Unable to Extricate Themselves from ' the She 11.—Much disappointment is very often experienced through young chicks being unable, owing to the toughness of the shell, to extricate themselves, and they become exhausted and die. A simple remedy is when setting the eggs or a few days before hatching time to dip' the finger in vinegar and pass it round the centre of the shell of each egg. When hatching is due the shells will easily drop apart with very little exertion on the part of the chicks. Charcoal.—Charcoal powder is of the utmost value in the poultry "yard, and yet there are many who do not recognise the importance of its use. Many of the intestinal ailments have their origin or are brought about by the use of polluted drinking water, stale or sour foods, and exposure to colds, which sets up a form of fer- mentation. One of the finest aids in combating such fermentation is charcoal, which acts as a splendid disinfectant. In the case of fully- matured birds it is best to place the granulated charcoal in a box where the grit or shell is kept, but for young chickens it can be mixed., with the soft food. , , • .</p>
        <p>Bread Crusts.—These can be used to advantage and a saving effected in the food bill by steeping them in skimmed milk for an hour or two, then giving the crusts to the chicks, and there is no better food for promoting size and pushing for- ward the chicks than bread and milk.</p>
        <p>Change the Coops to Fresh Ground.—Move the chicken coops onto fre.sh ground as often as pos- sible, and as the chickens get older allow them a wider range. Chickens always do better on fresh ground, and this should be kept clear of adult birds. Small Runs.—lt is a bad plan to overstock a small enclosed run. Keep it scrupulously clean. and provide a good dust bath, and plenty of grit and shell material. Fresh water should be given every day, and the drinking vessels kept sweet and clean. Serve the food in troughs, and see that a plentiful supply of greens of some kind is' always within reach.—H. Inman, in "The Feathered World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>AGE OF LAYING STOCK.</head>
        <p>THE AGE OF LAYING STOCK.</p>
        <p>Old prejudices die hard, it is said. Certainly this is so in many phases of poultry-keeping. A hundred and one things are done merely because it has been customary to do them for years past. It is the hardest thing in the world to get people out of old Tuts. There is one point in poultry-keeping which illustrates this admir- ably. Although experiment upon experiment has demonstrated the fact that a hen is in her prime during the first and second seasons, after which she rarely pays for the food she con- sumes, the space she occupies, or the labour she entails, yet there are comparatively few poultry. keepers who can be persuaded to dispose of their hens when they are two years old. I have many and many a time visited a poultry farm where the bulk of the laying hens have been three and even four years old. There is small wonder that upon such places the complaint is general that eggs are scarce. If a pullet be hatched in March, she should be disposed of two years the , following June or July, that is when she is about I twenty-seven months old, or just before she enters her second moult. In this country there is a big demand for old hens during June, and the prices realised average about 2s. 6d. to 2s. 9d. each. When I recommend readers to dis- pose of their hens when they are two years old, I am referring especially to purely utility stock, for I realise that there are many valuable show specimens that pay to retain much longer, than this. There are plenty of fancy hens that would pay well were they only to produce a dozen eggs in a season. But this does no apply ,to the ordinary laying hen whose eggs are sold for edible purposes.—Middlesex, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>POSTAL SERVICE TO THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>THE POSTAL SERVICE TO THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>Wonderful Organisation That Keeps •"Tommy " In Touch With Home.</p>
        <p>An interesting account is given in the May "Windsor Magazine" of the way in which -the' Army's mail is handled in the war, and many details as to the wide ramifications of this unique postal service are recorded, with a re- markably complete set of photographs as illus-' trations. In the course of his theme the writer, W. G. Fitz Gerald, says "Of all war's social phases, I think this should have first place, so nearly does it touch every man in the firing-line, and—more or less directly—every home in the Empire. Our armies already run into millions, with Over- seas contingents which are armies in them- selves. The matter of letters and parcels is, therefore, a prime consideration to which Gov- ernment is keenly alive. "So, after food and munition, the roads of the war zone know no more precious freight than the postal lorries, thumping and splashing through seas of mire in a foreign land, losing their way at night, with all lights out, and ghastly rockets aloft—those merciless eyes of hostile guns. These postal lorries are often ditched, sometimes under fierce shell-fire, too, with their sacks ablaze and drivers crouching at the wheel, racing the cumbrous vehicle to safety and the common rendezvous. Heroics ' are not confined to the firing-line. The whole system Is one of subdivision, down to the smal- lest unit of our Army. Then the trench post- man goes his rounds. Try to visualise that round. It is usually done at night. The whole system is a marvel of smooth, efficient working, from the home collection to the tactful censor- ing of "Tommy's" prompt and grateful reply. "What was the origin of this admirable field service? It is worth more than a moment's retrospect. In 1882 the 24th Middlesex—the old Post Office Rifles, a corps made up entirely of Post Office servants—sent out a contingent to look after the Army's mail in the Nile cam- paign. Again, in the Sudan, we find the Army Postal Corps gladdening the troops in those endless sands.</p>
        <p>"Lord Haldane's Army Act brought into being the Postal Section of the Royal Engineers, who now handle parcels by the hundred tons and letters that run into millions for our scat- tered forces by sea and land."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LAND FOR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>LAND FOR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Australian Land To Be Reserved For Men And Dependents.</p>
        <p>The Minister for Lands of New South Wales recently state that he hoped to have 4,000 homestead farms made available for soldiers within the next two years without interfering with oidinary settlement. The Government has decided that advances shall be made up to £5OO i:pon improvements, and for the purchase of stock and implements. It iS hoped to'open up extensive new country by means of addi- tional stalk routes and new railways, and the efturts of the Government will undoubtedly be extensively supplemented by. private entar- plises such as that organised by Dr. Artlint., at French's Forest, where 27 farms for returned soldiers have been allotted. In eon- nectien with this latter scheme, cottages I.re being specially constructed and the occupiers will only repay £lOO (at the rate of ss. per .week) out of the cost price of £l5O. At the last meeting of the Board of the British Immi- gration League, the following motion was un- animously carried "That inasmuch as the Empire's forces have been and are fighting for all the Empire pos.: sesses, the men and their dependents, including war widows, are, in our opinion, entitled'. to have the beat of our lands reserved, upon which they may be provided with homes and occupa- tion. Further, that attempts to induce widows and maimed soldiers or others to settle on lands of an inferior or doubtful quality Should be strongly resented." .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>New Leather.</head>
        <p>A New Leather.</p>
        <p>Owing to the scarcity of leather, coupled with agrowing demand, the hunting 9f sea-lions (a large species of seal found in, the Pacific) is suggested as a new industry for the Canatlian Pacific coast. It is said that the leather 'pro- duced is of an excellent type for the manufac- ture of light articles, such as gloves, belts, &amp;c., and a factory in British Columbia has already turned out a quantity of such goods, the qual- ity and style of which are favourably co'm- mented upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
