      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY .6, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY .6, 1916.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>COUNTIES AND ALLIED FARMERS.</p>
        <p>The Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund—the fumi which the farmers of England are raising for the re-establishment of the war-ruined peasants in allied countries—amounts to £65,000. In addition stock, seeds and implements of the value of about £5,000 have already been dis- tributed in relief in France. The counties whose contributions, raised or in sight, reach four figures are :—Lancashire and Cheshire £9,947, Middlesex £8,012, Essex £6,545, Kent £4,926, Notts £3,800, Hunts £3,267, Cam- bridge £2,660, Hants £2,566, Sussex £1,977, Northants £1,842, Yorks. £1,754, Warwickshire £1,505, Derbyshire £1,375, Berkshire £1,354„ Rut- land £1,231, Oxfordshire £1,071, Northumberland and Durham £1,039, and, Lincolnshire £1,009. Then follow Hereford £737, Monmouth £727, Bedfordshire £584, Bucks £570, WestmorWnd £527, 'Somerset £517, Suffolk 5:477, Gloucester- shire £444, Cornwall £4OO, Herts £314, Devonshire £307, Shropshire £286, Surrey £209, Wiltshire £lB9, Worcester £179, Cumberland £173, Norfolk £144, Dorset £lO5, Leicestershire £.36, and Staffordshiree£l6: The contribution of Wales stands at £954.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WOMEN FOR FARM WORK.</head>
        <p>WOMEN FOR FARM WORK.</p>
        <p>• The response to the Board of Agriculture's appeal for women to work on the land has, says the "Times," been "extraordinarily good." The official figures available are. 30,000 from the registration forms already sent in by the village registrars; the real figures are said to be more like 50,000. This is due •to the fact that many women on the town registers are asking for work on the land, and also that only 29 out of the 37 county women's agricultural committees have as yet sent in their returns. As far as can be judged Norfolk and Suffolk have been specially good; Cambridge, Bucks, Devon and Dorset have also been excellent. Typical figures are Norfolk, 4,000; Suffolk, 4,000; Hants, 2,378; , Cambridge, 2,278; Bucks, 2,000; Devon, 1,800; Dorset, 1,736; Wilts, 1,557; Essex, 1,535. But these figures are constantly changing as new reports are coming in from many of the 2,000 registrars who have not already sent in returns. Cornwall, which shows only 132, is better than it looks, groups of women not included in the return cultivating every spare bit of land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DANGER IN BURNING RUBBISH.</head>
        <p>DANGER IN BURNING RUBBISH.</p>
        <p>Before the Newcastle (Staffs.) County Magis- trates, James Barnes, farmer, of Mucclestone, pleaded not guilty to a charge of making use of a certain fire in such a manner as could serve as a signal, guide or landmark, on March 31st, without permission of the competent naval or military authority, and contrary to the Defence of the Realm Regulations Consolidation Act.— P.C. Seaton gave evidence to the effect that at 7.50 p.m. on the 31st ult., he saw a fire in a field occupied by the defendant. When he ques- tioned the defendant he replied that he was only burning some rubbish and hedge bushings. The fire could be seen from a distance of two , miles. Defendant told the Bench that he was getting ready for corn sowing, and he was burn- ing some rubbish and hedge bushings, so that he could get on with his work. He started the fire at 7.30 p.m., nnd half an hour later it was completely out. The Bench ordered defendant to pay the costs and cautioned him.</p>
        <p>DUTCH GOVERNMENT BLOCK THE WAY.</p>
        <p>I regret to announce, says a writer of "Farm and Home," that the English Beet Sugar Cor- poration, Ltd. (formerly the .Anglo-Netherland Sugar Corporation, Ltd.), which lately took over the Cantley Factory, has been compelled to issue a letter to growers of beet, stating that owing to• war conditions and the refusal of• the Dutch Government to permit exportation of beet seed, the Corporation is unable to supply the seed it has purchased, although that seed is now lying at Rotterdam all ready for shipment. The natural inference therefore is that all con- tracts between the Corporation and the growers of Sugar beet are cancelled for 1916, a serious check to the infant industry which is regret- table from every point of view, particularly from the national standpoint, when we have thrust upon us from every side the desira:bility of our producing home-grown foodstuffs during the present crisis—this most. emphatically so in the case of sugar, geeing that we are officially in- formed of the necessity to prepare for a reduc- tion of 25 per cent, in the 1916 supplies as com- pared with 1915. At the same time the news is passed along from Germany that the Imperial Government has taken measures to guarantee the planting of a much larger acreage of beets in 1916 in the Fatherland, so great have been the advantages accruing from the cultivation of the root during the tremendous crisis .through which the Central Empires are passing, for in addition to providing a most valuable food pro- duct, sugar beets have been invaluable as a source of alcohol, as well as other most im- portant by-products; it is even stated, too, that those much-vaunted German chemists have been able to convert beet sugar, &amp;c., into tabloid forms of food which go a long way to- wards constituting the diet of the Hunnish armies! (Let us hope the men have a grain of salt served up with these "confections.") , In the year which has just closed, the Cantley Factory dealt with the produce of some 2,150 acres, which on the average gave the fairly satisfactory yield of rather more than 9 tons of washed roots to the acre, with a sugar content of 17.36 per cent. (quite good!), and at the price per ton of washed roots, free on rail, of £1 10s. Bd., yielded to the growers an average return of some £l4 per acre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FEW HINTS TO HORSE OWNERS.</head>
        <p>A FEW HINTS TO HORSE OWNERS.</p>
        <p>" Colds" are more often contracted when the horses are brought in from grass than when they are turned out. The change from the pure air of the field to the atmosphere of a stable that may be badly ventilated, dark and impure, is responsible for more injury than exposure. See that the harness fits well. It may cost some trouble and even expense, but this will be repaid by better work and by the saving of days of idleness that may follow in the use of a badly-fitting collar or other gear. And after all, sooner or later the expense and trouble will have to be incurred.</p>
        <p>"Tugging at the reins" is one of the com- monest indications that mark an inexperienced or clumsy driver. The horse's lips are sensitive, and the aim of the driver should be to keep them so, and not to toughen and harden them by ill-treatment. The tandem-driving at the Agricultural Hall during the show season is instructive. We remember seeing one " whip " going through most complicated evolutions; it was almost impossible to detect his hands mov- ing, yet his control of the animals was perfect. Compare this with the tug, tug of the streets, and you will see what a wide chasm separates even some men who think they can drive from the real expert. I The horse that always has water to drink in his stable is less likely to suffer from drinking ! too much than the animal that is supplied at I irregular times. The latter takes a large quan- tity hurriedly because he is never certain when he will get the next opportunity; the other takes just as much as he wants, and no more.—Carm, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>INDIAN AND JAPANESE PROSPECTS.</head>
        <p>INDIAN AND JAPANESE PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries have received the following information from the International Agricultural Institute:—</p>
        <p>The area of Wheat in British India is esti- mated at 30,214,000 acres, or 5.6 per cent. below last year's acreage and 3.5 per cent, above the average of the preceding five years. The acre- age Of wheat in Japan is estimated alt 1,240,000 acres, or 11 per cent. above last year's area and 46 Per cent. above the average Of the Preceding five years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>CUTTING IVY.</p>
        <p>The ivy is no doubt the best of all evergreen wall climbers, giving a snug, warm look to any building that is covered with it. Like all other strong growing creepers it needs frequent atten- tion to prevent it choking the gutters and over- growing the windows. It is well to trim it in once a year, the present month being the best for doing this; the ,season of active growth. is at hand, the young leafage quickly develops and tAkes off the bare. appearance inseparable from the cutting. We find a sharp hedge shears the best for the purpose. Care should always be taken that the foliage and young growing shoots are entirely cleared off the wall below the gutters for at least one foot, otherwise they</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>RURAL HEALTH.</head>
        <p>RURAL HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Nine Deaths At The Lord! Derby Hospital In 1915.</p>
        <p>Dr. T. A. Murray, medical officer of health, in his report for 1915 to the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Councils says that owing to the war esti- mates of local populations have been made on unusual methods. The Registrar-General esti- mates the civilian population of the district to be 10,903, which is adopted for the purpose of estimating the death-rate; while the estimated population for the year 1914 (12,759) is used for calculating the birth-rate. . There were 242 deaths in the district, of which 103 were of non-residents. Persons with ft fixed residence who died away numbered 2O leaving the net death-rate 159. Births numbertd 247.</p>
        <p>Birth-rate, 19.3; average for last six years, 9.9. -- . . .</p>
        <p>Death-rate of persons belonging te district,</p>
        <p>Deaths at the War Hospital.</p>
        <p>In the three months of the year berore Win- wick Asylum was taken over for military pur- poses there were 66 deaths among the inmates. During the remainder of the year nine soldier patients died, one from each of the following cause:, Cancer, nephritis, eOlepay,, gunshot wound of skull and abscess of brain, gunshot wound of chest and secondary hemorrhage, gun- shot wound of left leg and tetanus, gunshot wound of right leg and tetanus, shrapnel wound of right shoulder and tetanus, gunshot wound of abdomen and volvulus of small intestine, opera- tion shock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Physical Characters of the District.</head>
        <p>Physical Characters of the District.</p>
        <p>TheY district is situated on the north side of the river Mersey, and extends from the bound- ary of Widnes to that from Cadishead, a dis- tance of twelve miles. It is mostly low lying and flat, with very little natural drainage, con- sequently it is cold and wet. The subsoil on the west side and .centre portion is clay, while on the east side there is moss land. There are plenty of workmen's houses through- out the district, those in the country being old but habitable, whilst.others in the villages are mostly new. The occupation of the inhabitants is mostly agricultural; but in the west there are coal miners. Milk supply is abundant and wholesome. There are 59 purveyors of milk on the register. The dairies, milkshops, and cow- sheds are frequently visited and found pa the whole satisfactory, clean and sanitary‘ as re- gards space, ventilation and drainage, but the grooming of the cows is sadly neglected.</p>
        <p>Other feints. , Diseases ,--specially prevalent. Measles, mumps,v and' chicken pox during January, May and December. School closure adopted three times for measles and whooping cough. Dwelling-houses inspected in 1.914 under Sec- tion 17 of the Act of 1909.-288. • Dwelling-houses considered unfit for human, habitation.—None. Representations to authority with a view to, making closing orders.—None. Dwelling-houses in which defects were reme- died without malfing closing orders.-139. General character of defects found to exist.— Want of ventilation and dampness. There is not a deficiency of housing accommo- dation. Source of water,supply.—Virarrington Corpora- tion. mostly. Very good. No possibility of con- tamination. \ No insufficiency. • • Scavenging and removal of house refuse is car- ried out satisfactorily for whole ,of district. Condition of slaughter-houses satisfactory. A public abattoir has not been provided. 'Lodging-houses.—None left. in the district. , Sanitary condition of the: schools.—Good. Dairies, cowsheds and • milkshops.—Condition fairly good. THE INSPECTOR'S REPORT. , Mr. Frank W. Kerfoot, inspector, reported 'that 776 inspections were made, which includes dwelling-houses, farms, cowsheds, slaughter- houses, factories and workshops, &amp;c. The num- ber of statutory notices served were 66, informal notices 47; total 113. During the year 288 houses have been inspected under the Housing Town Planning Act, 1909. The number of houses in which defects were 'found hunibered 182. It was not fOund nedessary to make any closing orders. The general character of the defects found were the want of free circulation of air, damp and defective walls for the want of damp courses and pointing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CONSCIENCE.</head>
        <p>CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>Judge And A Noble'Word,Put To Contemptuous Use:</p>
        <p>Last week, in giving judgment in an appeal by a conscientious objector, Mr. Justice Law- rence said : The only exemption in the Act—which is weak enough, in Heaven's name—is exemption from combatant service. These cases are lamentably rumerous, and there are agitators who support one another in their objections. This applicant succeeded in evading combatant service, and he wants to show, he has a con- scientious objection to serving the State in any other capacity. It is almost a contradiction in terms to use the noble word "conscience" in reference to such a state of mind. He wants to take all the benefits of citizenship and per- form none of its duties. A more unconscien- tious claim it is almost impossible to conceive. He says he has a conscience that is too tender to do anything for the State in war. Such a conscience is only worthy of the utmost con- tempt. I cannot get my mind to regard it as conscience. Mr. Justice Avery concurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FARM AND CARDEN.—Continued.</head>
        <p>FARM AND CARDEN.—Continued.</p>
        <p>will very soon choke them and cause a lot of trouble.</p>
        <p>PEAS AND DR' WEATHER.</p>
        <p>Very few vegetables suffer more in a dry season than peas, and those who have light soil to deal with should guard against injury by sowing in trenches. A good way is to take out the soil to about a foot in depth, placing some well-rotted manure in the bottom and filling in with the soil that was thrown out. Do not quite fill in the trench, but all9w. of sufficient space so that frequent soakings of water can be applied, which if allowed to percolate through a mulch of manure will be all the more efficacious. The feeding from the surface and the rich larder into which the roots will even- tually penetrate will go a long way towards preventing the peas failing at a critical time, just when the pods are forming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>KEEP THE HOE GOING.</head>
        <p>KEEP THE HOE GOING.</p>
        <p>Very few people seem to realise the benefit that accrues to growing crops from stirring the surface of the soil. Ido not mean merely scratching the surface, but a deep, honest moving of the ground with the hoe, thus break- ing up the surface and admitting light and air, so beneficial to root action. Plants growing in a hard-baked soil very soon show signs of dis- tress. All too often these signs of flagging are misunderstood by the inexperienced grower, who at once rushes off for his water-pa insread of employing the hoe to break up the surface, thus , filling up the cracks, so often seen in clayey soil and through which the moisture is rapidly , escaping. There is quite a number of ways of hoeing. Too often does it happen that the operator only runs the hoe along the surface, merely :raising a dust, but neither killing the weeds nor filling up the outlets through which the precious moisture is escaping. If water must be given, then when the hoeing if com- pleted a good 'soaking may be 40Plied,, this owing to the loose surface finding its' way to the roots of the various crops. A goodplan when the ground has been thus Well soaked is next day to run the hoe over the ground and then if obtainable to give a good mulch of rotten' manure, which will help to retain ,the moisture. —" Wick," in "Farm and Home.'" •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>TURNIP ROOTED BEET.</head>
        <p>TURNIP ROOTED BEET.</p>
        <p>Egyptian turnip which is great rho se whovee variety ent lioknce r the hmh ees otonur turnip Globe, rooted beet should itmryTip rooted variety. In Crimson lobe the flesh is very dark and of fine texture; in fact, if well grown—that is, in soil that has been well manured for a previous crop—it is equal to the best of the long-rooted beets. If any reader has n a nice warm border and will-Bow a 6w lines, thinning g will htalvi the .f aseedlingslgoewshwenel, a ice lot of roots to 6 inches apart, from the middle to the end of July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Lengthy Public Service. I should like to join the Chorus of congratula- tion and praise of Mr. W. Mullard, who has had not far short of 30 years' continuous clerk- ship successively of the old Local Board and the present District Council of Lymm. Apart from a few judges and magistrates' clerks, who are immortal and no more liable to change than the old Medo-Persian laws, I have personally linown of only one who has "stuck it" so long, and he was an official In miiiewhat similar cir- cumstances. On the formation of an extensive School Board area in which I worked in the early 'seventies, he was appointed clerk, and, when I last saw hint two or three months ago, he was still hard at it, rejecting with disdain the assistance of all but one young clerk. My old friend is one of the last of a rapidly dis- appearing generation. "Tha' Knows.'!</p>
        <p>Thus "Critic" in his last week's Lymm "Local facts and fancies." I don't know why, but it brought to my recollection a -rather laughable incident that occurred in one of my districts when I was quite a young ,fellow. A very strict father allowed his son for the first time to viiit his tailor unaccompanied, and the young hopeful ordered the astonished trades- man to make the suit entirely without pockets. On the delivery of the strange goods, his scan- dalised dad called for his explanation. "Well," was the bold if unfilial retort, " pock.sts are no good to me,; you never give me, anything to put in 'em." He had a regular allovr4ucl from that date.</p>
        <p>Why? The contributor just cited wants to know why a Lymm audience always insists on Mr. John Brazendale singing "The water melon." I have often been puzzled in like manner. For many years Mr. H. Rickard, a noted bass ,singer in his day, periodically came my way, and 1 can- not recall - his ever being permitted to evape without wasting some. of his rare sweetng,s on the desert air of an absurdity, entitled "Camomile. tea." In one locality in which I spent some years there was a tenor who, what- ever else he gave, was invariably obliged to add "The old stable jacket," of all things! One who entertained in semi-private circles was con- strained to sing "Brigham Young" on such occasions down to my leaving the neighbour- hood—a 'song •which had a certain vogße,some forty and odd years ago.</p>
        <p>Another Example. For a more striking instance of a different character I fftll back on a little 9f ray:, promised experience with the old Rifle Volunteers, whose dinners I had the rare fortune to attend-over a considerable succession of years. One item on the evening's programme, indispensable as the menu itself, was a. song which comprised the sole repertoire of one of the non-commissioned officers. It was about a charming specimen of femininity, who wor,ked a sewing machine. I first made this young lady's acquaintance at the old Middlesex Music Hall 52 years ago, but the professional version of the song wasn't "a patch" on that of the amateur in Volunteer uniform who was wont therewith to convulse with mirth all who., sat with him at meat. The promoters of the feast always took care to, re- tain the services of a competent pianist—one of those convenient musicians who sit, at the piano what time the impromptu vocalist softly hums over his more Or less strange and complicated melody, and forthwith improvise an accompani- ment, elaborating and improving upon the theme with , growing familiarity—the reader knows the useful sort of functionary I refer to; I believe the-sp4rformance 'is popularly if, not technically known as "'vamping." To his great credit be it recorded that he was never com- pleted- baffled by the formidable tasks- set him by this particular singer, but I think they must have tried his rare talents to the utmost, and kept him in constant fear for his repata- tion.</p>
        <p>"Acting the Goat." The trouble so far as, the pianist was con- cerned, and the fun for the company generally. arose out of the utter inability of IlievOcalist to start each verse on the same key' or even- to begin on the bne which he had given the sorely- tried accompanist. Having arranged matters with the poor chap on the stool, apparently to their mutual satisfaction, he would startle and puzzle the sensitive player and delight ,every- body else by "banging off" on On entirely different key. Then would the manipulator of thiekeyboard instrument cast upon him a half- reproachful, half-despairing glance, and the two would put their heads together for a second course of instruction. After this further re- hearsal, they would straighten things out pretty well and eventually try again—with disastrous results as before. The humorist, however, was a believer in.the doctrine of final perseverance; he would aflternately.,scratch his head and wring his hands in serio-comic fashion, and then—heroically perpetrate another and yet an- other false start before he was able to repeat in song the hackneyed story of the maiden of sewing machine renown. Once the preliminary obstacles were fairly overcome, interest in the performance rapidly waned, but as long as the initial difficulties lasted the contribution was tremendously funny. Especially noteworthy was the unruffled demeanour of the principal, was was plainly unconscious of his limitations. All this, of course, has nothing whatever 'to do with Mr. J. B. and "The water melon." I must leave others to deal with the interesting question raised by "Critic"; it has served my turn, at all events. Grossmith the First. ,</p>
        <p>Those above referred to were the days when the late Mr. George Grossmith was wont to tour the provinces with little more luggage than Artemus Ward comprehensively summed up in the significant phrase, "A clean biled rag." The inimitable entertainer's excellent two hours' programme was wholly sustained by the spare figure in evening dress with the aid of a piano. But he was not " Grossmith the First." I wonder how many of my readers recollect, as I do very clearly, when the lamented George's father was also able to keep a big audience rocking with laughter for a couple of hours or so. When last I heard the elder Grossmith, grandfather of the present worthy bearer of the honoured name, he was going the rounds with just such a humorous "Lecture on lecturing" as Max O'Rell turned to good account. I reckon that to be 47 years ago, but to this day I distinctly remember leading points of the address and the main incidents of that capital entertainment—for that most emphatically, in the hest sense of the term, it was. The fine old man, who was a capital mimic, died of apoplexy while in the act of delighting his fellow guests at a dinner party with one of his favourite representations.</p>
        <p>Endings of Letters. "Uncle Ned's" -suggested titles for meritorious members of the "Children's Corner" set me rummaging vainly for something I imagined I possessed in relation to the subject, but I turned up some other matter which will doubtless in- terest a section of readers. There is a remark- able choice for endings of letters. James Howell, the historian (1720), used a quaint variety, such as "Yours inviolably," "Yours verily," "Yours really," "Yours in no vulgar way of friendshii)," "Yours, yours, yours!" Walpole wrote "Yours very much," and to Hannah More, in 1789, "Yours more and more." John Bright (whom one gratefully remembers as ever extremely thoughtful for the harassed reporter) once had a controversy by letter, and closed with "I am, sir, with whatever respect is due to you." The old Board of Commissioners of the Royal Navy used to subscribe their letters to officers "Your affectionate friends." Eventually a certain captain replied to them in the like terms. He was promptly called upon to cease using the expression, so in his next communication he wrote, " I am, gentlemen, no longer your affectionate friend." After that the habit was discontinued. Sarcasm.</p>
        <p>Thus far have I written without mentioning tno you either the war or the Shakespeare tercentenary festival—though both figured so prominently in last week's issue as to give excuse for a u here. For fairly obvious reasons It;o,uqcuhesmeornel,y. upon the latter. And, curiously enough, he bdeitsiinrge in laexamplest of Saturday'ssarcasm pha headed sentd '` A nni e n o "As ti like it," where Orlando says to Jacques Ido yetonbetstaeird nsftrcaenrgtnersi:so.c a notice sarcasm may r poetry to which his attention had been directed,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>A Saturday Afternoon In</p>
        <p>Pastoral Cheshire.</p>
        <p>Twelve miles out of a total of some 34 of good and picturesque' roads, little traversed by motor car or other traffic, may be found by the Saturday afternoon cyclist in the following route through a part of Cheshire. It takes the rider through a great diversity of scenery, the middle portion being through a patch of country which, while remote from the beaten track, is yet typical of the county'ssylvan characteristics. Let the cycle enthusiast betake. himself then up Stretton to Acton Bridge •and onward throdgh the quaint village of Weaverham. About two miles beyond, in the direction of Northwich, he will turn to the right at a pointer marked " Chester." Crossing the Manchester to Chester highway, in a. further quarter mile, proceed past the stately front of Hartford Hall to Daven- ham. En route the road will dip into the pleasant valley of the navigable Weaver, and to the right, I?efore crossing the bridge, will be seen the entrance to the famous Vale ROM Park, the further gates being situated in the sequestered village of Whitegate. Still onwards through Davenham, the less-pronounced though pretty .)vale' of the river l/ane will,be similarly crossed. 'Keeping an eye oji all sign-posts indicat-, ing Snutsford, the cyclist will make acquaintance with the somewhat straggling village of Lath Dennis, and commence to run through that strangely quiet length of the ride to which allu- sion has been made. Winding through an agri- cultural tract of great fertility, the read takes in Nether Peover, and, later, Lower Peover with its venerable church and umbrageous old trees. Gaining at length the -ICnutsford to Holmes Chapel main road, the rider will make for the first-mentioned' of these places. With Toft Hall and its fine park on his left band, he enters linutaford by way of its proudest and most picturesque thoroughfare. Refreshment for the inner man, and safe stabling for his quiet and uncomplaining steed being found at some suitable hostel, the homeward journey can be chosen between the rival routes of High Legh and Bucklow Hill.</p>
        <p>An excellent map for guidance in those parts of Cheshire south of a line drawn from Frod- sham through Knutsford to Macclesfield is Bartholomew's half-inch to the mile Sheet No. 12. It can be obtained from Percival Pearse, Warrington, or from any map seller, at a cost of 2s:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HOW TO STRAIGHTEN A WHEEL.</head>
        <p>HOW TO STRAIGHTEN A WHEEL.</p>
        <p>While the job of trueing a badly buckled wheel may be a task beyond the amateur who is not accustomed to tackle important repairs, no rider should shirk the straightening of a wheel which, having suffered a bump at a curb or against n cart, is a little out of truth. It is a simple task. First, find out at what points the rim has gone askew. To do this suspend the machine so that the damaged wheel revolves freely. Then take a long piece of chalk and hold one end firmly against a fork so that the other just misses the rim by the barest frac- tion of an inch. Give the wheel a turn and then that part which is out of truth will come into contact with the chalk, leaving a white mark wherever it touches. If the spokes feel very tight near the chalked parts and on the same side, you will probably find that if you slacken them slightly and tighten those spokes on the side opposite to them the wheel will come true. After you have slackened or tight- ened the spokes a half turn, test the wheel for truth again, and if necessary give another quarter or half turn. Aim at having all the spokes of equal tension; over-tight or slightly loose spokes will soon throw a wheel out of truth. Nipple-keys should be used for tighten- ing the spokes. They cost only a few pence. If you have to screw up any spokes more than a turn you must remove the tyre and tuba to make sure that the spoke end is not protruding through the inner side of the rim sufficiently to endanger the tube.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CYCLING FOR YOUNG PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>CYCLING FOR YOUNG PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>The advance that cycling has made among young folks during the last few years is very great indeed. There is no commoner sight nowadays on almost any road than boys and girls from ten years of age and upwards cycling merrily along. Children younger than this are often seen and in most cases are quite adepts at this form of exercise. But it is most im- portant that a child's cycle shall fit properly. The machine must not be too heavy nor yet must it be the large for the rider. I am in- clined to the opinion that a child is better not allowed to cycle at all .unless the machine is suitable in every war. Many people who buy juvenile cycles, excellent though they may he, allow their children to ride .on them when they have outgrown them, being naturally averse from buying a new one whilst the old machine is in perfect running order. An experienced cyclist should personally see to the adjustments of the machine and take care that the child does not either have to stretch too much to reach the pedals, or, on the other hand, crouch</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>"This will be read with admiration when the names of Homer and Shakespeare are forgotten —but not till then." Which in turn recalls a very short but sharply-pointed fable. "Now I'm off," said the fly to the cow, as the swish of her tail came dangerously near. "Really," said the cow, "I did not know you were there." However, I was alluding to the "Sweet Swann of Avon "—as Anne Hathaway- wrote of him whose memory we now specially' delight to honour—and to close with that little story would never do. But after all, as Milton puts it in the epitaph, "What needs my Shakespeare for his honoured bones?" This "Dear son of memory, great heir of fame," has built himself "a livelong monument," and there is here at least one poor scribe who is incapable of deal- ing with this theme. To close with a couple of lines from Ashworst's acrostic on Shakespeare— " Rest thou in peace near Avon's tranquil ware: Eternal is thy name, that ne'er can find a grave!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
