      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>MOTOR CYCLE TYRE.</head>
        <p>THE MOTOR CYCLE TYRE.</p>
        <p>The new taxes and the unreasonable price of petrol can be counter-balanced to some extent by taking every possible care of the tyres. A very appreciable reduction can be made in the running costs if they are looked after properly. Renewals are costly. Therefore give constant attention to the 'covers. Examine each tyre thoroughly after any long run, so that any small repairs may be affected. Grit is a con- stant source of trouble. If a minute piece gets into the rubber and is not extracted, it will. rapidly work its way into the canvas and finally into the tube. During its passage, moreover, it allows water to penetrate, and this has a very deleterous effect on the fabric. Each tyre should be gone over and any pieces of foreign matter should be eased out with the point of a knife. A little solution worked into the cut with the knife edge will heal the wound. An- other point is, look after the spare tubes, Don't carry them anyhow in thp tool box, but see that they are protected from injury. Rubber perishes if not cared for properly. Sunlight and oil are its greatest enemies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HOW TO GET A CAR OUT OF SAND.</head>
        <p>HOW TO GET A CAR OUT OF SAND.</p>
        <p>It is not very often that a.inoterist gets stuck in and, .not in this country at 'any rate. In America and in the Colonies it is ,quite a com- mon experience. A few years ago, when I was motoring in Ireland, I did get 'stuck fast in sand, and great trouble it was to get out. I wish I had known of the following tips, milled from an American eontemporary. "When un- able to move in sand do .not continue to apply the power to the rear wheels, as it only results in the wheels •bedding in. A Simple method, and one not generally known, is to let the air out of the rear ,tyres. This will afford the re- quired traction; as the •width Of the" tyre is greatly- increased. It is much easier ta inflate tyres than to dig. ,and use boards, • rails, &amp;c. If the power is applied gradually neither the - tyre nor the tube will be injured." The North British Rubber Co., Ltd., of Castle Mills, Edinburgh, have been made by special appointment suppliers of pneumatic and other tyres and general rubber goods to his Majesty King Alphonso XIII. of Spain. The popularity: of "Clincher" tyres and other pro- ductions of this huge concern, not only in thi3 country, but in all parts of the world, is such that no one will be surprised that the company has secured this well-deserved royal appoint- ment.</p>
        <p>SPOKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending May 12th,</p>
        <p>Parades.</p>
        <p>Sunday (To-morrow).—Church Parade, Christ Church Latchford. Fall in at Headquarters at 10 a.m. prompt. Thursday.—Class firing, 7 p.m. prompt. Range Officer, Quartermaster G. B. Starkey. Friday.—Fall in at 7.30 prompt. Uniform must be worn. Company drill, &amp;e. Ban.: prac- tice at 7.30. _</p>
        <p>Orderlies of the week, Corporal P. Nelson an Lance-Corporal H. Smith. By Order, _ _</p>
        <p>J. B. STARKEY, C.O.</p>
        <p>S</p>
