      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ADMIRAL'S WIFE'S APPEAL FOR , OUR SAILOR LADS.</head>
        <p>ADMIRAL'S WIFE'S APPEAL FOR , OUR SAILOR LADS.</p>
        <p>`Sir,—l am asking for gifts towards the ex- cellent work of the British and Foreign Bailors' Society to be announced at their 98th annual gathering at the Mansion House, Lon- don, on May Bth. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>The Society is both Internatimial and Inter- denominational and is the oliiest organisation looking after the all-round interests of our sailor lads. It has long enjoyed the patronage of the Royal House and continues to receive the regular support of all the Churches, as well as the leading members of the naval, shipping, and commercial circles. Even' more important than all, I am con- vinced that the society has a real place in the hearts and lives of our brave sailors, and as a small reward for their splendid courage and endurance I hope that you will generously assist this glorious enterprise. I am sure you will agree with me that it is not only necessary to maintain this work at its present high level, but also to extend its activities in other centres where our sailors are in urgent need of institutes ashore for the effective supply of their social and spiritual wants.</p>
        <p>_ ETHEL BEATTY. ilanotver Lodge, Regent's Park, N.W., 28th April, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LIQUOR RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE LIQUOR RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Parkes must indeed be very hard 4IP for argument when he actually has the .audacity to tell us "Dr. Addison testified in the Rouse of Commons to the good result of the restrictions." He must think your,. readers either very ill-informed or very stupid if he thinks that any amount of " testifying " by the official apologist for the Control Board Would bear any weight with them. He might ae well inform 116 that the Kaiser " testifies " •to the benefits of German kultur !</p>
        <p>The restrictions are rapidly becoming not 'Only a confounded nuisance but a positive dan- .ger to the public. "Brandy might have saved the old man's life," remarked the Westminster 'Coroner at an inquest held on April 25th on a ,case .of death by drowning. The rescuer lAad endeavoured to purchase spirits, but in vain, owing to the restricted hours. The Coroner commented strongly on this "dreadful omission in the regulations." This is only one case that happens to have come to light. Goodness knows how many lives have been sacrificed to the teetotalish restriction fad!</p>
        <p>J. E. SIMPSON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—With reference to the discussion in your tolumns on the drink traffic, may 1 suggest that an acceptable solution Would be found by the adoption and the adaptation of the Gothenburg System of Dis- interested Management. One great merit of this Proposal is that it finds very influential support among members of all the parties in Parlia- Went.</p>
        <p>Speaking on behalf of the then Liberal Govern- luent, Earl Beauchamp, who had charge of the Government Scotch Bill in the House of Lords, said on November 2,5 th, 1912</p>
        <p>_ " I can only end_ by repeating that there_ is really little except the practical difficulty of a good scheme being formed which prevents Mis Majesty's Government from agreeing to an amendment being put into the Bill dealing, with this subject." (Of disinterested 'management.) Mr. Bonar Law, speaking in the House of Commons on. May 15th, 1915, declared that he had "always been of the opinion that the only real chance of temperance reform in this country is some system of disinterested manage- ment."</p>
        <p>Mr. Austen Chamberlain on the same occasion 13aid, "I have a certain hereditary interest, if I may say so, in any proposal of public control - ,over the retail sale of drink. Had the proposals which my father earnestly advocated in the early part of his Parliamentary career, and to which he secured the assent of the Corporation of Birmingham, approved themselves to Parlia- ment at that time, when there was not a tied house in the country, the solution of the prob- lem might have been advanced very considerably beyond the point we have reached to-day. Therefore, ,I do not speak as one hostile to public 'management, still less as one hostile to dis- interested management." I Sir T. P. Whittaker, speaking in the House oaf Commons debate on the subject of disinter- ested management on February 6th, 1913, gave !utterance during his speech to these weighty -opinions: "Disinterested management largely !Bets rid of the trade interest I feel 'very strosgly that it would do much to render the carrying out and putting into effect of reforms much more easy I also am con- vinced that the system of disinterested manage- Ment would enormously hasten and 'facilitate the adoption of prohibition, which I ultimately look for "I also, after having studied this question for some years, and given such attention to it Q s I could, am satisfied that the result of the .sxPerience in Norway and Sweden, limited,- as it is there, to the sale of Spirits, is not so ,Perfect as it could be made, but such as it is it has demonstrated the enormous advantage of that method of dealing with the liquor traffic 'ever the ordinary system of licensing." Mr. George N. Barnes, M.P., speaking in the .eliate on the Scotch Temperance Bill, said : " For my own part I am in favour of tiunicipalising ,this trade , But .I realise that municipalisation, is, after all, not in prac- tical politics. There is no reason, however, why aliOther principle which, I think is a step to- iards that, should not be grafted upon this Bill, 'Whereby we might, at all events, eliminate this element of private profit to the individual who runs the public-house. I see no reason why filo Principle of disinterested management should ot be given as an additional option under the )3111 I hope that when the Bill goes up- stairs to Committee, something will; be done Whereby this additional option will 'be given, and if it is done in Committee I hope the Government will see to it that it is accepted When the Bill comes back into the House." These quotations prove that the proposal I Iggest is supported by Liberals, Conservatives, eraperance Reformers, and by members of the J'abour party.—Yours, .</p>
        <p>PROHIBITIONIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Quail Adm.</head>
        <p>Quail Adm.</p>
        <p>SPIRITUAL GROWTH.</p>
        <p>Striking Sermon At Wycliffe Sunday School Anniversary.</p>
        <p>As usual, there were large congregations, ex- cellent singing and striking,sermons at Wycliffe Congregational Sunday school anniversary, held on Sunday. : During the day, Mrs. Smallshaw sang "The Motherland," and the choir sang "He Watching Over Israel" and "Hear My Prayer," the solo part in the latter being rendered by Miss Elsie Hewitt. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. In the afternoon Mr. Eric Jeffery ably recited " Rooia for the Leper." The Rev. T. S. Taylor, of Eccles, was the special preacher, and in the morning, taking as his text "Go on to perfection," he said true disciples of Christ should be able to leave be- hind the first principles of Christian life and to advance to something better. In order to demonstrate the serious need of the growth in the Christian life Paul, in iiebrews, wrote the most perplexing exhortation to be found in the Scriptures. Here it was stated that it was im- possible for tips° who were once enlightened and had tasted of the heavenly gift and been made partakers of the Holy Ghost, to renew them again unto repentance if they should fall away, seeing they crucify to themselves the Son of God afresh and put Him to an open shame. It might be urged, he said, that the parable onlys referred to the fall of one who had once been innocent and ,not to the fall of one who had lived in a state of grace. A newer trans- lation had removed the difficulty and put it as follows: It was impossible to keep on bringing back to new repentance those who, by their own undoing were repeatedly crucified. Therefore, he continued, it was high time they had mastered the first principles of the Gospel. It seemed perfectly natural to him to, point out that it was impossible to keep on restoring in- definitely those who never got beyond the elementary principles of their faith, and who were continually sliding back into a state of sin. ,It was looked upon in everyday life as necessary to try to improve anything they took up, but when they came to improve their -lives as Christians they began to act as though they had never heard anything at, all, or that such a principle existed. Life was not a rigid thing. Their standard of morality was not a set -amount like a bank balance. It was suddenly changing, either increasing or decreasing, keep- ing better or getting worse. To some people the Christian life was a series of fresh starts. To such he would give a solemn warning. It was impossible for that kind of thing to go on in- definitely; it was impossible to keep on bringing them back to new repentance since they were continually crucifying the Son of Gad afresh and putting Him to open shame. That was the meaning of those terrible words; terrible to those who were deliberately trifling with the Christian life. It was a serious blemish on much of their preaching that they heard any- amount of direction as to haw to begin the Christian life, but there was very little prac- tical guidance as to how they were to continue in it and grow in it. There was,, however, one rule which could be insisted upon, and that was that they should grow in the Christian life so as to walk in the way of Christ. Before their eyes must be the highest possible ideal, even the perfection of God, and with that mission before them they could never say that they had finished growing inlthe Christian life. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Lessons For Children And Lessons From Children.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary was held on Sunday, the preacher being the Rev. W. Reid. The day was beautifully fine and large congregations were present morning, afternoon and evening. Special hymns by the children and choir were a prominent feature of the services. The sermon in the morning was from the text: "Except ye turn and become as a little child ye cannot enter into the Kingdom of Heaven." The preacher, after referring to the incident responsible for this saying. of Christ, proceeded to draw lessons from the child nature, and urged his hearers to strive to copy in their Christian profession humility, unselfishness and purity. The afternoon service was presided over by Mr. W. Russell, the speaker being Councillor R. Henshall. In a bright and cheerful address Mr. Henshall asked the children to cultivate goodness rather than cleverness. He called as witnesses to the fact of goodness being the chief quality of life, Marcus Aurelius, George Herbert, Charles Kingsley and Tennyson, illustrating, by apt quotations from each; how great men of different periods all agree as to its necessity in the formation of high character. In the evening a large congregation heard the preacher from the text in I. Samuel ii. 19: "Moreover his, mother made him a little coat and brought it to him from Year to year when she came up with her husband to offer the yearly sacrifice." The appeal was made for a more intelligent and discriminating nurture of the child, physically and spiritually. Pro- minence was also given to the fact that Han- nah was drawn to the temple by her maternal interest in Samuel, and parents were urged not only to send their children 'to church, but to come themselves.</p>
        <p>• Suitable addresses were given to the children. morning and evening. The' services were heartily enjoyed by all, and will rank as the most successful held at St. John's.</p>
        <p>How the War Started. Crown Prince to Raiser: "Father, who started the war? Was it the Emperor of Austria, or King of Serbia, or King George, or had the King of Bulgaria or the Sultan of Turkey a hand in it r'</p>
        <p>_ _ " No, no, my son, none of them had anything to do with it. A man named Roosevelt came from America to see my great armx, my great navy, my great guns, and my munitions. He gave me a pat on the shoulder and said, Bill, you can conquer the world,' and I, like a fool, believed him."</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
