      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>•:••:•••:•+++•:••:••:•+•:•••:••:••:•4:••:••:••:•••:••:••:••:•••:••••:•,•:•++++0.6.):••:•+++++•:•++.:• •:••:••:••:••:•"•:••••••:•,:•••••:••:••:****,:••:••:**iCio:••:••:•••:•••:••:••:•-•:•+•:••:••:•+•:••:••:••:••:••:* , IN OM AN'S WORL :1•.:1: • 4.</head>
        <p>•:••:•••:•+++•:••:••:•+•:•••:••:••:•4:••:••:••:•••:••:••:••:•••:••••:•,•:•++++0.6.):••:•+++++•:•++.:• •:••:••:••:••:•"•:••••••:•,:•••••:••:••:****,:••:••:**iCio:••:••:•••:•••:••:••:•-•:•+•:••:••:•+•:••:••:••:••:••:* , IN OM AN'S WORL :1•.:1: • 4.</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Skirt and Manual Labour.</p>
        <p>The war seems likely to make a dead latter of the law under which it is an indictable offence for a woman to wear male attire. Women are taking to so many outdoor manual occupations, in which womanly skirts are a drawback, that these garments are being dis- pensed with in many instances. ,In some cases, such as work in stables or cow-byres, even the shortest of skirts are incompatible , with effi- cient work and cleanliness, and breeches become a necessity. There is not, however, the same compulsion for girl commissionaires, some of whom may be seen outside London- establish- ments in full masculine garb, while the police look on unmoved. Experience has shown that it is not an easy matter to .design a uniform for women that is suitable and yet neat and attractive. At first results in this direction were far from satisfactory, but later attempts have been more successful, and to-day one may see women in smart uniforms and yet retain their femininity. • - Practical Garments.</p>
        <p>The blouse jersey and the coat-frock bid fair to rival each other, for both are practical., yet graceful garments, that save the time of their patrons. Intricate indeed were the fastenings of pre-war garments compared with those of the present day. The blouse jersey, for in- stance, is smart and becoming, yet can be worn without an underbodice or shirt; consequently its adjustment is but a matter of a few seconds. When first it appeared it was made of jerseyette, .but it may now be had in crepe de chine, and is being prepared for the hot weather in various makes of linen. Sports- coats, ton, as they were called when they first appeared, are equally practical and now serve every purpose. In woven silk the latest and most becoming styles are worn in the Metro- polis, as well as in the country, and form, with a suitable hat, a charming morning dress, although but a short time ago such a toilette would have, been considered unconventional. Particularly smart are the coats that are smocked at the back and sides, and falling in straight folds in front.</p>
        <p>Simple Knot Coiffures. Nowadays fashions in hairdressing change very frequently, and the sleek and simple coiffure may in/ the near future have to give place to puffs and curls of the Stuart period. That is if the large hat displaces the small toque, which has had such a lengthy reign. With the picturesque high-crowned hat, close hairdressing is absolutely necessary, but with larger hats the larger coiffure will become requisite. The French twist has certainly gone out of favour, and several other styles are now on their trial. One of these goes well with the hat-brims that turn up at the back. The hair for these is brushed from the forehead and rolled into a loose knot, standing out a little below the crown of the head. The fortunate possessors of a perfect profile can 'wear this</p>
        <p>style quite close and uncurled, bui tiaeSe wain are unfortunately in thetpinority, and for the less favoured, little straight or curled fringes, or a break made by a low parting, are perniis- sible A bunch of curls in the Pompadour style, or a comical twist on the top of the head, are also favoured.</p>
        <p>Combs and Earrings. The right and proper position for these curls or knots •is not always easy to find, and yet success depends on the knot being placed at the right angle to suit the face. ' Whatever style may be chosen in order to be in the mode of the moment, the hair must be brushed closely up from the nape of the neck and cover about half of the ears.' Variety in hairdressing is also sought this season in the diversity of the ornaments that adorn the coiffure. Fan shell combs, Spanish in style and therefore high, give a quaint finish to the new knot worn on the top of the head. Carved combs are also ih evidence, as well as pins of bizarre shape set with a little good paste. Combs, other than the Spanish, are placed in the Japanese style at various angles, and the quainter the effect the more fashio4able the coiffure. Other ornaments will be affected by hats; earrings, for instance, will be larger and longer, for the small single 'stone is out of proportion under a big chapeau. The Veil in War-Time.</p>
        <p>The veil is essentially a feminine accessory and is connected with the most important events in a woman's life, giving a mysterious and distinctive air to the bride, and to the young, girl at her confirmation. Frenchwomen wear the veil more elegantly than the women of other countries, but motoring has helped our womenfolk to acquire the art also. Since the war, however, the veil has taken on a new and deeper meaning; it is the recognised symbol of service to the sick and wounded soldiers. The snowy folds of the amateur nurse's veil constitute an honourable badge to-day, for it is the outward. and visible sign that the wearer has joined the great army of recrhits, whose 4Srivilege it is to aid in the work of mercy and of healing. The, Russian Bride.</p>
        <p>Russian girls, like the French, have very Tittle to do with the choosing of their husbands, but in Russia the dot is not of a paramount importance. Roughly speaking, there are four classes of Russian girls, and they do not, as a rule, come much in contact with each other. There are the daughters of the nobility; next come those of the clerical families; below them the daughters of the commercial and trading classes; and lastly the daughters of the pea- sants. In the highest class a girl's education is considered finished when she reaches the age of eighteen. She is then presented to the Emperor, and a suitable husband is sought for her. Peasant girls marry earlier, generally at sixteen or seventeen, but in their case also the \parents make all the arrangements. The marriage of a high-born damsel was formerly attended by many quaint and picturesque cere- monies, and the ancient custom of blessing the wedding is still in force. The bride is led to the altar by the groomsmen, and the groom by the bridesmaids, and throughout the ceremony crowns, provided by the church, are held over the heads of the bridal- couple.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
