      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>END OF THE HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>THE END OF THE HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— It is pleasant to know that you all enjoyed your holidays, and that most of you are glad to be back again at school. Several extremely good letters were sent in this week; and it will be seen from those letters I reproduce how sensibly and usefully you all made use of the time dur- ing which the schools were closed. Although many of the letters were very good indeed, I think the one that was just the best was that sent in by</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson (No. 777). who lives in Lovely-lane, and attends Wycliffe School, and I therefore !award, her the prize. I hope all my nephews and nieces will real all the letters given below, as they show how to be happy by making the best use of the time at one's disposal. The' letter front Niece Edith Pugh, who won the prize a few weeks ago, is particularly interesting, describing as it does her -holiday experiences' at Brecon. This week we will have a Drawing Competftion,</p>
        <p>and I ask my young people to send me a drawing (not in colours) of any subjeet with which they feel themselves most capable of dealing. There are several things I should like you to bear in mind. All letters should reach me at the latest by Wednesday. The printers at "The Examiner" Office get terribly busy by Thursday and Friday—and what the Editor calls "matter" can only be dealt with when it is of great im- portance as news. Our little Corner is not "news," though it is important, and if the matter for it is left till late in the week all the persuasion in the world on my part will not soften the heart of the man who has to set the matter up in type. The only way to get well served at a newspaper office is to come• as early in the week as possible. Last week I received a letter from one of my nieces on Friday morn- ing, but I was quite unable to deal with it. So let me have your letters on Monday or Tuesday, or, at the very latest, on Wednesday in each week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Your loving, ITNCLE JACK.</head>
        <p>Your loving, ITNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>THE WINNING LETTER. By Elsie Johnson (777). 323, Lovely-lane, Warrington', April 30th, 1916 :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Holidays and Back to School.</head>
        <p>Holidays and Back to School.</p>
        <p>"I have enjoyed my holidays immensely.. The week seemed to pass rather too quickly to say I had enjoyed my holidays thoroughly. Mon- day and Tuesday were spent at home, but on Wednesday morning such a pleasant surprise awaited me, and what do you think it was? Why! I was going to Prodsham with both my auntie and cousins for the day. We arrived quite safely, and the best of it was it turned out to be a most glorious day. Having reached the top of the hill with tired legs, we sat down to have , dinner. We , sat down while auntie *went into Mrs. Parker's house,, Mersey View, to purchase tea and various other things. Then when all was ready we had a good and hearty dinner, enough to last us till supper-time. We —the children—had a game of ball, which we bought from the shop on tie hill. The4ime came for going home and we walked leisurely down to the station, waiting for the train. Why the time seemed to pass like the slibt of a gun, and we were soon in the train, puffing away to the old home. That day .was passed very pleasantly and the other days also were passed away by going either long or short walks into the country. I am looking forward to going .back to school to learn what I have to learn that will be of use to me in after-time. On Monday morning I am always up early, so it is of no use making excuses of being late if you can possibly be early, and encourage the head- master to do a good ,day's work and also for him to put his full confidence into the task before him.</p>
        <p>"ELSIE JOHNSON." "P.S.—I hope you have enjoyed your holidays as well as we have."</p>
        <p>Helped Mother.</p>
        <p>! Niece Eva Handley, 17, Collin-street; St. Bar- nabas'. School.—" I have enjoyed ray holiday very- much. I could not go away because there was a baby to mind, so I enjoyed myself by :taking baby out into the country. One or two days I went tp the Park and other days I went all ;ound BuitoiiiiioiA—aiid'Otheilills in thilf I country. This holiday I was glad. and a gave</p>
        <p>me great pleasure to help my mother some way or another. When Monday morning came I was glad to get back to school; I had got tired of staying at home. When I had got to school the teacher said she was glad to see her scholars back again and that she hoped we had enjoyed our holiday." Looked For Primroses and Violets.</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Pugh, 17, Ship-street. Brecon, South Wales, whose Warrington- address-3 iiy• 44, Glazebrook-street,—" I am glad we have' had the opportunity of writing on our holidays- and returning to school. Brecon is surroundbd' by hills called beacon's.; On Good Friday- we went a long walk of about eight miles; we set off looking forward to find primroses. and viblets. We had to climb many, stiles and cross, many fields to get to our destination.. On one side of us was the picturesque River Usk. Gentle- men were fishing, because there are many trout in the river. In the tall trees there were im- mense numbers of birds' nests and the birds were chirpinF away merrily. When we reached our destination we found to our delight a tre- mendous number, of primroses, but not violets, and , the atmosphere was filled with the fra- grance of them. On Friday we went down the promenade and across some fields, and we had such fun crossing ditehes and sometimes falling in; we also climbed some of the hills, but it is hard work getting to the top. We are re- turning to school on Wednesday here, and we are having two • extra ,days 'because the 'May Fair is here, and we are having such glorious weather. Now r think I must close lest I miss the post. Love to Uncle Ned. I hope you haii3 both enjoyed the Eastertide." A .Happy Time.</p>
        <p>Niece Edna Fellows, 44, Bewsey-road.--" Our holidays began last Thursday but one, and we were very glad to get a rest from lessons for a while. All that day I helped my mother with the housework, which I thought was not as troublesome as lessons. I went to bed that night very tired, for I was not used to house- work. When I awoke on the following morn- ing I hurried downstairs, for I wished to see my Easter egg. When I had seen it and played with it for a while I began to realise that I was hungry, and I soon ate my breakfast. This day, being Good Friday, we did nothing in particular, but, all the same, I was ready for bed when the time came. The next day being Saturday there was the shopping to be done. When I had done that I wrote my letter to Uncle Jack; after dinner we went to visit a friend of ours. We went to the Market to- gether and then we had tea. Afterwards I played out until bedtime came. The following day was Sunday, so we went to Sunday school and church. When Monday came we did not have our holiday like other people, but we got the washing done, for we wanted to go to Blackpool on the Tuesday. When we had finished washing we were quite ready for a rest and we were glad when bedtime came. On Tuesday morning we went to Blackpool. We had a very happy time there, too. Every morning and afternoon I was wading in the water. It was a very happy time and I en- joyed it immensely. We were very reluctant to come back to Warrington when the time came; but we had to come. That was Saturday. and the Sunday passed like all other Sundays do. When we went back to school we were glad to see each other again, and in an hour or two we were hard at work and glad ,that we had not forgotten to do our lessons." Fun In The Parks.</p>
        <p>Niece Mabel Wright, 68, Winwick-street.-- " I have had a very happy Easter holiday, and have enjoyed myself by going to the Bank Park. I sat on the grass and ate my lunch and then played with my playmates. I am sure everyone has been pleased with the week, as I have .my- self, with it being so beautiful. I am so glad to go back to school as I like school as much as I like holidays." Niece Dorothy Wright, 68, Winwick-street.— " Our holidays are soon over, and now we have to be ready to do a good week's work and try our best at school until another holiday comes. Well, Uncle Jack, I think we have had some lovely weather this week, and now we should not grumble at it being hot. I have been to the Park nearly every day this week, and it has been splendid. I went to look round in the new Park, and I noticed the different things and the, lovely flowers. Oh! my playmates and I did have some fun Playing on the grass and eating our lunch. Well, at the end of the week I be- gan to think of school, and thought to myself, 'surely everybody is thinking of school,' and I got up on Monday morning ready to do a good week's work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Glad To Be At School Again.</head>
        <p>Glad To Be At School Again.</p>
        <p>Nephew John Webb, 27, Catherine-street.--" I am not sorry to be back at school. Some boys think it is a nuisance. Before the holidays I was away from school with pains in my chest and stomach for a week, and I soon got tired of staying at home with nobody to play with." Niece Jeannie Wright, 10, Church-street.—" It</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>LENTIL COOKERY.</p>
        <p>'Lentils, well chosen and cooked, are the best of the pulse, and one should have a good store of them for winter and spring, when fresh vege- tables are not always at hand. They are usually ' to be had in a good state, and it is well to see they are fresh and free from weevils. They are , freely grown on the Continent, and though they may not be grown in our land they come to our markets freely. Boiled Lentils.—Soak a cupful of lentils in cold Water over night. Drain, boil until tender, and drain. Chop a small onion fine, fry in butter, add a teaspoonful of flour, and mix to a smooth paste. Add a cupful of hot stock and cook until thick, stirring constantly. Re-heat the lentils in the sauce, seasoning lightly. Lentil Croquettes.—Soak over night three cup- fuls of lentils, drain, and cook slowly for an hour in boiling water to cover. Drain, press through a colander, season lightly, add a table- spoonful of minced parsley, two tablespoonfuls of cream, and a teaspoonful of onion juice. Cool, shape into croquettes, dip in egg and crumbs, and fry in deep fat. Serve with tomato sauce. Curried Lentils.—Soak a cupful of red lentils for three or four hours and drain. Slice an onion, fry it in-butter, add the lentils and two cupfuls of boiling water or stock. Simmer for an hour, season lightly, and add a teaspoonful of curry powder and the juice of half a lemon. Simmer for ten minutes and serve in a torder of boiled rice.</p>
        <p>Lentil Rolls.—Mix together half a cupful each of boiled lentils, boiled rice, cooked chopped ham, and cooked chicken or veal. Season to taste. Scald cabbage leaves and wrap small bits, of the mixture in each leaf. Pack closely in buttered saucepan, cover with stock, and add atl bay leaf, an onion stuck with four cloves, and a blade of mace. Cook covered for forty-five minutes. Drain, strain the liquid, and thicken a sufficient quantity of it with flour browned in butter. Take from the fire and add the yolk of one egg beaten with a tablespoonful of tarragon vinegar. Pour the sauce over and serve.</p>
        <p>Lentils with Onions.—Boil half a pound of lentils in salted water until tender, and drain. Pry three sliced onions in butter, add the drained lentils, pepper and salt to season, and enough stock to moisten. Heat thoroughly and serve.</p>
        <p>Lentils and Bacon.—Cook a pint of lentils in water to cover, adding a bay leaf, a blade of mace, and an onion stuffed with three or four cloves. Fry a sliced onion in butter, and add a quarter of a pound of bacon cut into dice. Fry until the bacon is brown and crisp, add the lentils and enough stock to moisten. Season with salt, pepper, and minced parsley, heat thoroughly, and serve. Puree of Lentils.—Soak a quart of lentils for three hours and cook slowly until soft in water to cover, with a bunch of parsley, a carrot, an onion stuck with three cloves, and half a pound of salt pork. Remove the pork and vegetables, drain, press through a sieve, and season with salt, pepper, and minced parsley. Add a little butter and moisten with stock if too thick.</p>
        <p>Stewed Lentils.—Soak a cupful of lentils over night in cold water. Drain and cook for an hour in boiling water. Drain and reheat with a tablespoonful of butter.—From "How to Cook Vegetables."</p>
        <p>Wealth may not bring true happiness, but lots of us would be satisfied with the imitation.</p>
      </div>
