      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>• t 4 • _ 14. e</head>
        <p>• t 4 • _ 14. e</p>
        <p>was at this period put to the strongest possible climatic test, and showed no more shrinkage in the tropical heat of Afghanistan then it did in the coldest climate. It remains firm and hard in all weathers. This is why it las+ so long why it</p>
        <p>does not soften in use and why it is so very economical</p>
        <p>USEFULNESS OF BOYS.</p>
        <p>been done. In the hospital they should remem- ber there were inexperienced young girls. They ought tb think of that. There was already a married nurse in the hospital, and it was not hospital etiquette. On 'a vas being taken, only three members voted for Miss Broadbent's amendment, and, therefore, the recommendation of the House Committee was carried.</p>
        <p>Better In The Workshops Than Hawking In The Streets. His services wasted to the nation by going about with a rag and bone cart, a Warrington boy is;.now, as a consequence of proceedings at the Warrington Juvenile Court, on Wednesday* about to enter a workshop, where he will he useful in the production of munitions. • I</p>
        <p>It came 'about in this way : The boy was charged with hawking without a licence, and the Chief Constable stated that the mother. of the boy had applied for street trading badges, but had been refused. Since then the boy had insisted on going round. "At these times," continued the Chief, "when boys are released from school, it is intended that they should go into works, and not go about in this way." The Chairman (Mr. J. W. IlaTward) said there had never been so many chances for boys as there were to-day. Boys like defendant were urgently needed.</p>
        <p>On the mother promising that6she would get her boy into a workshop immediately, the ease was adjourned until June 7th.</p>
        <p>11 t • ;</p>
      </div>
