      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>DR. JAGGER'S WEDDING.</head>
        <p>DR. JAGGER'S WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Exigencies Of War-Time Cause</p>
        <p>A Little Friction.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS &amp; HOSPITAL ETIQUETTE.</p>
        <p>An unusual, piquant, and, sometiines, amusing discussion followed the announcement to the Board of Guardians on Wednesday, that their medical officer, Lieutenant Jagger, had married their superintendent nurse, Miss L. Emerton, at Earlestown, on Thursday of last week, and are now on their honeymoon. The Lieutenant is shortly to proceed to Egypt with the R.A.M.C. On the Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) reporting that the House Committee had recommended that the best wishes of the Guardians be sent to Lieutenant and Mrs. Jagger, and that Mrs. Jagger be allowed to retain her position, Miss Broadbent said she was very dissatisfied that Mrs. Jagger should be asked to remain, and she thought it should be reconsidered by the House Committee. She moved that the recom- ' mendation be not approved. Mrs. Hall seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Shaw: Did Miss Emerton obtain permission to go on leave after her marriage? The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley): She is allowed to take a holiday. Mr. Shaw: Is there any stipulated time or length of time?±</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley: Yes. She went last Thursday and she has a fortnight. Mr. Shaw: Has anything irregular been done by her going away? Mr. Jolley: No. Engaged As Bachelor And Spinster. Critical yet sympathetic, the Rev. A. M. Mitcliell.remarked that they ought not to forget that Lieutenant and Mrs. Jagger had just taken on the duties of holy matrimony, and they should receive their best wishes, whatever the Board Might think of their particular conduct. But it had occurred to him that the whole pro- ceeding was suggestive of irregularity, and he very much deplored the fact that information was not sent to the Guardians, or to the Chair- man personally, that the event was in contem- plation. They had there people holding the highest offices in the institution, both of whom had been engaged as bachelor and spinster. Therefore, it was really a very serious matter, and was calculated -to lead to)aomplications when they entered into the holy estate, of matrimony, especially in such a surpriising way without any information that such- an event was coming to pass. For the future, he, thought it ought to be clearly understood l that if their officers were imbued with the, matrimonial idea and wanted to marry, they shourd communicate with the Chairman or Gnardians. (Some laughter.) They should not enter into matrimony without permission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Military Reason For Haste.</head>
        <p>Military Reason For Haste.</p>
        <p>The Chairman agreed that in the ordinary way that should be done, but in this case the whole thing was covered by the fact that there were exceptional circumstances, lie had re- ceived notice that the marriage was going to be solemnised. The reason for haste was that the parties only knew two days before that the doctor would have to go abroad. Of course, if anything like this happened in ordinary times the officers' services would be terminated at once, but these were not normal times. It was not a question of setting up a precedent. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I suppose it has beeü. going on for some time, at any rate? The Chairman: The two parties called on me and informed me the day before. , Mr. Allen: I think we all ought to be satis- fied, as the Chairman is. Mr. Flanagan: This is the first offence of these two young people. Let us hope there will be no more. (Laughter.) Mr. George Mather: If superintendent nurses and doctors get courting they must let us know. (Laughter.) Dr. Jagger Saw The Clerk.</p>
        <p>At the suggestion of the Chairman, the Clerk made a statement regarding the premartial arrangements with him. He said there had been nothing in writing at all, but Lieutenant and Mrs. Jagger came specially to see him about the matter. With regard to the holiday, the Guardians granted them a certain allowance each year. When officers left for a holiday, how- ever, he (the Clerk) requested that they should inform him that they were going away, and that proper arrangements had been made for the carrying on of the work. That was the custom, and that had been done in this case.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent said she complained of more than the very discourteous way in which it had</p>
        <p>a</p>
        <p>t</p>
