      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>VISCOUNTESS AND GARDENING . FOR GIRLS. ,</head>
        <p>VISCOUNTESS AND GARDENING . FOR GIRLS. ,</p>
        <p>"The life of a gardening student is a very happy one, and I am often struck with the ready answer that eomee when a visitor asks about it. The answer usually given is, 'I would not change my life for any other.' Early hours, days spent in the open air, congenial companions all at work upon the same inter- ests, tending beautiful flowers and fruit, pick- ing and packing fresh vegetables that go to stlengthen dwellers in towns, physical aetivity which all young people enjoy, these are amongst the pleasures of a gardener's life. Those who only care for the bright glitter of shop win- dows, who prefer smart patent leather shoes and going from one afternoon teaparty to an- other, had best keep away from it, hut active, healthy country girls will revel in the happy enjoyment that it brings." So writes Lady Wolseley in the May issue of the "Woman at Home," and women folk who study the illustrated article cannot fail to be fired with enthusiasm at the brilliant prospect attainable by those who can so easily become efficient for work on the land.</p>
        <p>1; , ;1\</p>
      </div>
