      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</p>
        <p>COMPULSION.,</p>
        <p>In the new Military Service Bill the Gov- ernment stipulate that every man in the country over 18 and under 41 years old, who is not exempted on swcial grounds, shall from the date of the passing of the Bill be deemed to have been enlisted in the regular forces and shall LI subject to military law. Mr. Asquith, in his speech intimating to the House the intentions of the Government,</p>
        <p>gave some striking figures of the expansion of our military and naval forces in response to the demands of the war. When war broke out we had six Regular • and 14 Territorial Divisions and six Overseas Divisions-26 Divisions in all. We have now 42 Regular and 28 Territorial Divisions, a Naval Division, and 12 Divisions from the Dominions-83 Divisions in all. The total military and naval effort of the Empire is represented in a num- ber which exceeds 5,000,000 men. When it is considered that this total has been reached under a voluntary; system, while we have been carrying out special and peculiar responsibili- ties on an enormous scale in providing finan- cial assistance, transport, 'supplies, and munitions for our Allies, the record is cer- tainly not one of which we as a nation have any cause to be ashamed. But we have reached a point at which the existing system and our long-established and cherished volun- tary institutions are no longer sufficient to meet the needs of the future. The Govern- ment has arrived, after an exhaustive inquiry into all the conditions of the problem, at the conclusion, on the one hand, that the nuin- ber of men required in view of the contin- gencies of the summer and autumn campaign, could not be obtained in due timeunder the existing machinery, and on the other hand that this number is actually available without incapacitating us for the discharge of our other responsibilitiesn which are, quite as essential to the succesful prosecution of the war as the maintenance of a fixed number of</p>
        <p>men at the Front;' The practical application</p>
        <p>irf such a conclusion is obvious. It is the duty ' of the nation to find these men, and as the resources of voluntary jnethods are unequal to the task, the Government is obliged to appeal to the nation for general consent to the , adoption of compulsory methods—immediate</p>
        <p>and "all round." It is. not often that Mr. Asquith replies to the unrestrained attacks upon the Govern- ment which are now a daily featilre of political life. - But on Tuesday.he did so in a powerful appeal for national unity, not "because he had any doubt as to the prospects of our cause on tho. sea or in the field, but because of the damaging effect of nivision here at home on our Allies and on neutral countries."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>IRISH TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>• The Irish trouble has come to an end. It wa's not a rebellion in the proper sense. The King has found the proper word with which to describe the affair, for in thanking those *lo had contributed to the "vindication of the law, His Majesty called it the recent lamentable "outbreak." It was an outbreak</p>
        <p>of the most regrettable kind; but, as Mr.</p>
        <p>Birrell, who was forced to resign, rejliped to say, there was no Irish rebellion. The coun- try stood firm in face of a great temptation, and the inference to be drawn is that Mr. Birrell's long and successful and conciliatory rule has made the country substantially loyal, although by a curious paradox it brought about his fall—through the recklessieas of a section upon whom good intentions 'had no corresponding-effect. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
