      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Some newspapers have been- brandishing the fact that at one time catkin of the Irish rebel leaders have enjoyed hospitality in their dis- tricts. "Happily, we in Warrington can make no such boast," a prominent member of the United Irish League said to me. ,</p>
        <p>I hear that the customers of the pub!ic-bolises in town have been "on strike" during some parts of this week against the increase in Ile cost of beer. A meeting or the licenied victuallers is to be held next week; 'nit I. don't anticipate that they will retaliate by a lock-mt.</p>
        <p>So great has been the call at the Town Hall during the last few days for appeal forms ,that the demand has been greater than the supply. A fresh supply is expected. a.</p>
        <p>At the annual police- inspection on Wednes- day. the 6overnment Inspector had the un- usual experience of seeing—in Warrington, at all events—about as many special constables as regular policemen drawn up in the parade room for his critical attention.</p>
        <p>The regulars were a fine body of men and the man without one or more war medals on his breast was an exception, the Inspector was not slow. to notice.</p>
        <p>To the specials he said the country was in- debted to them for having, by coming ,forward in such large numbers, released many useful men in the forces of the country to join the Army.</p>
        <p>He had a special word for Mr. John Upson, who fs a magistrate and 'a special constable, too. All the magistrates at Nottingham, said the Inspector, were specials. As Mr. Upson is the only rebresentative of the local Bench, Warrington would appear to have considerable arrears to make up.</p>
        <p>"War saving" has taken the place of " thrift " in a Government scheme for findiug the sinews of war upon lines which readers of "The Examiner " will recognise as those advocated some months ago by Mr. E. Qsbor-ne.</p>
        <p>Wednesday, the real anniverary of the death of Shakespeare, was recognised informally at some of the local day schools. Alderman Ben- nett, who visited the Secondary School, told the senior classes there that if the British Empire ever did fall; Shakespeare would still live to represent us through the ages, as Homer has saved Greece frbm oblivion.</p>
        <p>It is a comforting reflection. But, as- the worthy Aldeiman believes, and we .all believe, there are agencies in existence that will save the Empire from having to fall back into the last ditch of tradition and history even in such good company as Shakespeare. Tommy' Atkins and Jack Tar are among those agencies. So are the civilians who do not croak.</p>
        <p>Dr. Grimshaw, who lectured in the Parr Hall on Saturday, mentioned that Norman Holbrook, V.C., once showed him his identity disc, and on the obverse side of if was engraved, "No flowers by request."</p>
        <p>This cheerful cynicism is on a par with that of an American newspaper heading. A celebrated divine had declared that the floor of Hen was littered with champagne corks, the buckles of dancing slippers, and cigarette ends. The head- ing to the report of the sermon was, "0 Death. where is thy sting?"</p>
        <p>Mr. Adam Bennett, the Labour organiser, was representing a workman at the Warrington Munitions ,Court on Monday. On the Chairman asking _ him his Christian name, he replied, "Adam—the very first!" Mr. Bennett has a beard, but I did not guess he was so old.</p>
        <p>In the course of Thursday's sitting of the Tribunal, at the Town Hall, Alderman Tinnion remarked to Mr. Jarman, secretary of the Co- operative Society, that one of the shops was not particularly creditable • from. an architectural point ,of view. Mr. Jarman retorted, amid laughter. "None of them is. They were not built for beauty !"</p>
        <p>I have not yet heard the cuckoo this year, but I know those who heard "'the wandering voice" several times last week.</p>
        <p>We are all glad to hear the cuckoo—not for its own worth, but as a sign that summer is' at hand.</p>
        <p>As a, matter of fact, we have as small reason to welcome this visitor as we have to be over- anxious that the Germans should land. For the cuckoo is, in another sphere, and on a. more moderate scale, what the Germans are when they get a chance to reveal their own character.</p>
        <p>Each cuckoo's egg deposited.in one of our song-bird's nests means the destruction of the rightful brood. It also means that the foster- parents are worked to death.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederick J. Stubbs, of Theydon Bois, writing to filo London "Observer," estimates that about 5,000,000 small birds. are destroyed each year in England and Wales by this insidious pest. The cuckoo is, he says, tho worst animal in the world from the moral point of view. It is really a disgrace in the scheme of Nature.</p>
        <p>Clearly, the bird has imposed upon our poets. There is as much difference between the "har- binger of spring" and "an insidious pest" as there is, say, between German Culture and the revealed German nature, -</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</p>
        <p>SIIIESPEIRE TERCENTENARY.</p>
        <p>1616-1916.</p>
        <p>:Adult Schools' Dramatic And Musical Recital.</p>
        <p>A very modest, unambitious, but most praise- worthy effort on the part of a fewof the attenuated remnant of Shakespearian enthusiasts available at'the present. time in W.arrington (owing to ,the call of the war) has resulted in a vex:), creditable celebration of this important event by the mem- bers of the Warrington Adult Schools, which took place .on Wednesday evening at Cairo- street Schoolroom. It took the form of a dramatic and musical recital from the plays, and was attended by a large. and keenly inter- ested audience, the hearty applause bestowed upon the performers being clear evidence of the thorough:enjqinaent which the entertainment afforded. •</p>
        <p>Selections .:from. three of Shakespeare's most famous comedies were kiven, viz.,. the "Forest" scenes-in Act 24f ."-Ati,Y-Cou Like It:" the.char- acters !being distributed as follows: The ban- ished Duke, Mr. W. H. Sherburn; Jaques, Mr. Alfred Perris; Amiens, Mr. W. J. Rowland; Ist Lord, Mr. H. Sherburn; 2nd Lord,' Mr. R. Williams; Orlando, Mr. W. Landon; and Adam, Mr. W: Hankey.. The "Love" scenes from "The Merchant of Venice" formed the chief feature of the per- formance, owing to the part of Portia being sus- tained by Miss Gladys Adams, who, it will be remembered, enacted the character with great distinction. in one of the Shakespearian Players' Commemoration productions at the Royal Court Theatre a few years ago.. She was very ably assisted by Miss Ellison in the part of Nerissa, and the male parts were taken by Mr. W. Lan- don (Bassanio), Mr. W. J. Rowland (Prince of Morocco), and gr. H. M. Ashton (Gratiano). The concluding selections were the droll Dog- berry stones from "Much Ado About Nothing," the several parts being taken by Mr. H. M. Ashton (Dogberry), Mr. A. Perris, (Verges), Mr. W. H. Sherburn (Leonato), Mr. J. Greenwood (Borachio), Mr. R. Williams- (Conrade), Mr. W. Landon (Ist Watch), Mr.' W. Hankey (2nd Watch). Between the dramatic selections were •interspersed .the .following veal musical items, the accompaniments. being .tastefully played by 'Mrs: Rowland :-- f _ _</p>
        <p>"Who is Sylvia?" ,(Schubert), Mr. Rowland; song, "0 Mistress Mine" (Sullivan), Miss Sherburn ;' duet, "When that I was a little :tiny boy" (Wyndham Robinson), by Miss Sher- burn and Mr. Rowland; song, "Orpheus with his lute" (Sullivan), Miss Sherburn. Mr. Rowland also sang in the Forest scenes in "As You Like it" (unaccompanied) "Blow, blow, thou winter wind," and "Under' the greenwood tree." It is unnecessary to attempt the apportioning of commendation where such a general excel- lence was attained. To the ladies and to Mr. lI.M. Ashton Must, be given a Tull meed of praise for the undoubtedly high tone and finish imparted to the performance as a whole by their participation in it.. For the rest we feel assured °the members of the Adult Schools will account themselves Well paid for their labour—willingly .ared delightedly engaged in—by the assurance of their auditois' pleasure and satisfaction. The general suCcess of the little "celebration" v,-as; much enhanced by the presence in the chair Of Mr. G. A. Dunlop, who gave a very interest- ing address on the life and works of Shake- speare as a prelude to the scenes marked out for performance, and during the course of the evening acted as ,chorus to the performers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>SHAKESPEARE THE MAN.</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE THE MAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. A. Dimlop, in his opening address, ex- pressed the indebtedness of the Adult Schools to the help so willingly given by several mem- bers of the old Shakespearian Society, includ- ing Miss G. Adams, Miss Ellison, and Messrs. H. M. Ashton and A. W. Perris, who, by taking part with the Adult School members, came to stiffen and give quality to the performance. He referred to the great pleasure given to his ad- mirers of the somewhat belated honour given this week to Frank R. Benson—(applause)—who has done so much during the last thirty years for the English, stage. Performances of Shake- speare's plays were being given in Germany, where they. looked upon Shakespeare as too good to be an Englishman. He could not help think- ing that if the benign influenee of the literature which Shakespeare introduced to all countries alike had permeated the minds and lives of the different countries We should have been spared the present catastrophe. Most people looked upon Shakespeare as a problem or a miracle, and-, were thereby deterred from taking any seriou.interest in him. They should look upon Shakespeare as a man. He lived a very human life, and, like ourselves, had his development. It as by following that development and the maturing ,of his mind, as shown in his plays, that we got to understand the real man. Mr. Dunlop then jariefly sketched Shake- sixvtre's career. • "In stifdying the development,- growth and achievement of this great mind," concluded the speaker, ".we have after all to admit :that he was e. miracle in so far as all genitts is a miracle. When we remember the finished pro- duct, his trueness to Nature, his versatility, his beauty of, language, his perfect prose, his depth and richness of thought, and his long gallery of characters—if we Could only get all his charac- ters assembled in this room to-night and hear them speak—what a feast it would be for the imagination. When we think of all these things we realise what a great Englishman was William Shakespeare." ?Are understand that Mr. W. Landon has been the prime mover in the desire to commemorate the poet's death, and has managed the whole of. the arrangements, which have resulted so suc- cessfully, and on which he is to be congratu- lated. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>TERCENTENARY OF SHAKESPEARE.</head>
        <p>THE TERCENTENARY OF SHAKESPEARE.</p>
        <p>The soil pf England had been dewed with tears For centuries, and wet with blood-red rain. She watched the changing seasons wax and wane, But patiently, with mingled hopes and fears, She calmly waited through the silent years. Her great •heart for some god-like birth was ' fain Which woul4 atone for all her toil and pain And make a Music fit for angels' ears.</p>
        <p>And from the fair isle's travail, hale and strong, While the young daffodils were all aflame, And the green buds leapt out to greet the morn, And all the birds burst into happy song, - A sudden glory to the dull world came, And smiling April saw our Shakespeare born.</p>
        <p>He was oui England. All his mother's face In. his bright lineaments she proudly found.; Her voice spoke in his, accents, and around The listening globe declared his dwelling-place; Bespoke his kinship with the island race; Whose home from sea to sea was'holy ground, .Whose soil for ever should be more renowned Because he lent if his immortal bays.</p>
        <p>Shakespeare was England,--and'is -England- still; In all her mirth and beauty and high deeds; His tongue a trumpet when she meets her foes; 4 His heart's blood pulses in her peerless veins; He suffers when his noble England bleeds; Of all her blooms he is the perfect rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>The years rolled on, and Spring came, laughing- eyed, - And once more wreathed the dear old land withi flowers; But sunny April is the month of showers, For 'twits. in April-that sweet Shakespeare died Three hundred years ago. Now far and wide Pierce havoc rages, and grim war- devours The shuddering world, and temples fall and tbwers, _ ,And mighty empires perish .in their pride; But Shakespeare lives and 'England, .and again, , As o'er the sleeping streets of Stratford town I saw the red sun, rise this Easter day, Shall shine majestic and in splendour reign, And loving hands shell come the king to " crown, f When the last cannon rusts itself away. ARTHUR BENNETT. Stratford-on-Avon, Raster Sunday. . „.. .„ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>OBITUARY.</head>
        <p>OBITUARY.</p>
        <p>MR. SPENCER WALLHEAD.</p>
        <p>Solicitor's Death After A Prolonged Illness.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spencer Wallhead, head of the firm of Messrs. Spencer Wallhead and Son, solicitors, •Warrington, died at his residence, The Clef', Walton-road, Stockton Heath, on Sunday night, after a prolonged illness, at the age of 68. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallhea:d, who was a native of Lincoln, came to Warrington in 1871, to take up an appointment with Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., solicitors. He afterwards became managing clerk to the late Mr. W. H. Brook, formerly Town Clerk of Warrington, with whom he served his articles. He began to practise as a solicitor on his own account in 1889,', having been admitted in 1884. His son, Mr. Sidney Wallhead, was admitted to partnership in 1897. '</p>
        <p>A Conservative, Churchman, and a devoted angler, Mr. Wallhead did not come much be- fore the public -gaze. He was, however, an ardent Freeinason, and as long ago as 1886 was Provincial Grand Deachn of the Provincial Grand Lodge of West Lancashire. Locally he was a member of the "Gilbert Greenall" Lodge, the "Gilbert Greenall" Chapter, and the "Lodge of Lights," and the oldest surviv- ing Past Master in each. He was a founder of the "Lodge of Rectitude" and the " Maker- field " Lodge. _ _</p>
        <p>The deceased leaves a widow and two sons and three daughters, for whom much sympathy has been expressed.</p>
        <p>, . THE FUNERAL. The funeral, which took place at the War-- rington Cemetery on Wednesday, was largely attended by Masonic, professional and other friends of the aeceased gentleman. The Rev. B. Percival, vicar of St. Peter's, officiated. The chief mourners were :—Mr. Sidney Wall- head, Lieutenant E. H. Arnott, R.N.R., Mr. J. Spencer Craik, Mr. George Smith, Mr. John 3ifainwaring, Mr. James Richardson, Mr. J. A. Percy, and Mr. Harold Young. . Freemasonry was represented by the follow- ing:—</p>
        <p>Bros. E. Rose, P.M., P.P.D.G.. D.C. Sec. 1250; W. H. J. Jenkins, P.M., P.Z.. P.G.R.; Thomas Hill, P.M., 1250; P.Pr.A.G.D.C., P.Z., Chap. 1250; J. Lee, P.Pr.G.W., Cheshire. "Gilbert Greenall " Lodge, 1250.--Bros. H. Maddock, W.M.; H. Collin, J. W. E. Craddock, P.M., P.Z.; R. T. Smith, P.M., P.Z.; H. Isherwood, S.E. 1250 and P.M. 148; J. S. Patten, 'P.M. 1384, 2819, P.Z. 1250, and P.P.G.5.8.; W. H. Stansfield, P.B. 2651. "St. Elphin " Lodge, 3287.—8r05. J. T. Bar- ton, W.M.; Jas. Waldron, P.M., Sec.; T. S. Steel, P.M.</p>
        <p>"Lodge of Rectitude," 3597.—8r05. H. Woods, W.M.; S. Pickton, S.W. "Lodge of Lights," 148.—Bros. T. I. Fletcher, W.M.; H. F. Greenough, P.M., P.Pr.G.S.B.; J. L. Davenport, H. Taylor and T. F. Barker.</p>
        <p>The following solicitors were present V. B. Davies, prAident of the Warrington Law Sotiety (of which deceased was a member), Mr. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), Mr. W. H. J. Jen- kins, Mr. Herbert Woods, Mr. J. Longland, Mr. A. E. White, Mr. W. D. Jeans, jun. (secretary) and Mr. T. S. Steel.</p>
        <p>Councillor J. G. Craik and Messrs. Segar Owen, Jas. Carter, Stanley Carer also attended. Beautiful floral tributes were received from: "His loving wife," "Ada, Sharl and Bay," "Sid and Eply," "Ernest and Ada." "Harry and Annie," "Jeffery and Ruth," Mr. and Mrs. J. Spencer Craik, Miss Lucy Craik, Mr. and Mrs. George Smith, Mrs. Charles Wallhead and family (Lincoln). Miss L. Wallhead (Horsforth), Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Percy (Chorlton-cum-Hardy), Mr. and Mrs. G. C. Jackson (Chorlton-cum- Hardy), Mrs. William Rodgers (Coniston), Mr. and Mrs. John Mainwaring, Mrs. L. Edelsten, Miss V. Ellison, Mrs. H. Ferguson and family, Mr. H. G. Mosely, Mr. and Mrs. fames Richardson, Mrs. W. H. Young and family, Mr. and Mrs. R. T. Smith (Frodshaln). Major and Mrs. W. Owen Smith, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Garnett, Mr. and Mrs. Stanley Carter, "The Lodge of Lights, No. 148; The family of the late Mr. and Mrs. Peter Leigh, "Maid of The Clee."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. F. Greenhough had charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LATE MR. T. J. LEE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. T. J. LEE.</p>
        <p>Funeral Of Highly-Respected Tradesman.</p>
        <p>• In the death of Mr. Thomas Jackson Lee yesterday week, a gentleman who will be much missed, has -passed away. Testimony of his popularity was afforded by the large crowd which assembled on the occasion of his funeral at St. Thomas' Church, at 'Which he was the Vicar's warden until last week, when ill-health prompted his retirement. Mr. Lee, who was 58 years old, had not been well for some time, and a few weeks ago left for Colwyn Bay in the hope of being restored, but while there he became seriously ill, and had to-be brought to his home, Lyndale, Stockton Ikatli, where he passed away as stated. Mr. Lee Was a well-known figure in Warring- ton and district. .In business as a draper in Bridge-street, he was also connected with a num- ber of local institutions, ,being a prominent Freemason, a member of the Old Academy, and an enthusiastic ffolter—in fact, he was one of those who formed the club at Appleton. Mr. Lee was initiated as a Freemason into the "Lodge of Lights," 148 about 21 years ago, was founder, treasurer and P.M. of the ," Lodge of Friendship," 2963, and in 1910 served as Pro- vincial Grand Senior Deacon for West Lanca- shire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AN IMPRESSIVE CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>AN IMPRESSIVE CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>It was natural, therefore, that there should be a large assembly at the funeral, which took place on Tuesday afternoon, prominent amongst these being Freemasons from various lodges. Among the clergy was the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones (vicar of Daresbury) who, following the usual "last rites," by the Rev. James Collins, con- ducted the short Masonic service amidst an im- pressive silence. The other clergyman present was the Rev. E. W. Clarke, curate. Prior to the interment there was a brief ser- vice in the church, but no music was rendered. Solemn simplicity predominated. The principal mourners were : Mr. H. Hedley Taylor (son-in-law), Mr. Edmunds '(brother-in- law), Hawarden ; Mr. Harry Edmunds (nephew), Mr. John Fairhurst, Mr. T. Jackson, Mr. William Fletcher, Whitley; Mr. Thomas Fair- hurst (cousins), Altriaham; Mr. E. J. Hall , and Mr. A. T. Hallaway (executors), Dr. G. W. I Joseph (medical attendant), Mr. Harold Clarke I (Messrs. Lee and Clarke), Mr. James Richard- son, Mr. Josiah Longland, Mr. A. C. Hall, and I Mr. George Hall (Bury). I The Freemasons present were: Bros. W. I Greenhouse, P.M., P.P.G.D., D.C.; John I Hesketh, P.M., P.P.A.G.S., West Lancashire; E. Rose, P.M., 1250, . -P.P.G.A.D.C.•, H. F. Greenough, P.M., P.P.G5.13.•;:" Gilbert Green- ' all " Lodge 1250, F.' Rose, P.M.; Alfred Jolley, S. McDowell; "Lodge of Lights ", 148, F. Howard, 11.. Isherwood, P.M., Thomas Toft, P.M.; 'J. H. Day, P.M;; Samuel Picton, and S. W. 3597; Thomas .1;, Fletcher, Wad.; Charles J. 'Smith, 1.P.M.; J. H. Team, J. Swin- ton,: R.: Shacklady; "Lodge of Friendship" 2963, J. 11. Clarke, 1.G.; H. W. Taylor, A. W. Paul, P.M.; Turner Cooper, J. E. Birtles, A. J. Willett, H. Lawrence; "St. -Elphin's " Lodge 3287, J. Waldron, P.M.; J. H. Hayward, P.M.; J. T. Barton, W.M.; 3597 Herbert Woods, W.M.; and Henry L. Taylor; "Lodge of Charity " 2651, F. A. Milling and W. IL Woodcock, I.P.M. Representing St. Thomas' Church were: Messrs. W. Mclntyre (vicar's warden), James Richardson (people's warden), A. J. Willett, J. H. Fletcher, T. Jackson, A. Worrall, J. Swinton, R. H. Smith, S. McDowell, F. Berry and J. Melbourne (sidesmen). Among others were Mr. R. H. Smith (of the C.E.M.S.) and Mr. Arthur Down and Mr. F. Rose, representing the St. Thomas' School, of which Mr. Lee was a manager. Among others we noticed were Alderman Bennett (Warrington Society), Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle, A. M. Ker, James Carter. Thomas Baxter, Henry' Greenall, A. Bottotaley, E. 3. Sutton and S. Moss. - A large number of beautiful floral tributes were received, these being from :—" Loving wife and daughters," Hedley and Florrie Taylor, Geoffrey, Hubert and Trevor Taylor, Mr. and Mrs. John Taylor and family, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Woodcock and family, Mr. and Mrs. W: Fletcher. Mr. and Mrs. Harold Clarke, Mr. and</p>
        <p>ell-next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>DAYLIGHT SAVIMG.</head>
        <p>DAYLIGHT SAVIMG.</p>
        <p>Government Are Now Prepared To Take Action In The Matter.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,--So at last we are going to have a Day- light Saving Bill I Some years ago, when the late Mr. W. Willett was alive, I spent a con- siderable proportion of my spare time in advocacy of this measure, and it is therefore with considerable satisfaction that I read in the papers that the Cabinet now proposes to take it_up in earnest. _</p>
        <p>This satisfaction, however, is considerably tempered by the reflection that they are only going to do so some eight years after the intro- duction of the original Daylight Saving Bill; for it was in 1908 that the first Bill was submitted to Parliament, and in the interval we have lost individually some 150 hours of .daylight annually. The Select Committee to which this Bill was referred took a large amount of evidence on the subject, and finally, if I remember aright, re- jected it by a majority of one. No one who read the evidence could fail to be impressed by the fact that a good deal of it was irrelevant, and even more of it based on absolute ignorance of what had always been the primary object of Mr. Willett's proposals, namely, to make the working day synchronise more closely with the hours of daylight. Since that time hundreds of Corporations and other public bodies throughout the country have signified their approval of the measure, to say nothing of the efforts of the Associated Chambers of Commerce, in which your own local Chamber played a not unimportant part,, in sending reso- lutions yearly to the President of the Board of Trade, that the whole weight of commercial opinion in the country was in favour of it. _</p>
        <p>How ,often in those days was one met with the reply, "My dear fellow, if you want to get up earlier in the morning, by all means do so, but don't ask me to follow your example." Showing the difficulty of getting the man in the street really to study a proposal of this kind if at first 'sight the idea does not appeal to him. The Delay and the Loss. Now that Germany, Austria and Holland have adopted the. idea, and in France the proposal only awaits the approval of the Senate, our noble politicians have decided that it probably would be to our advantage to have a Daylight Saving Bill as well, although eight years ago, :when the Bill was .before Parliament, it was clearly .shown that the saving in the, cost .of artificial light alone 'would be not far short of two millions per annum, and if two millions are worth saving when we are spending five millions a day, one would . have thought that this economy would have been even more at- tractive in times of normal Budgets. There is no new proposal Of any kind which has not some disadvantages, but in the case of Daylight Saving these are so small compared with the advantages, that it is Ei matter of great regret to me personally that we have had to wait until the year 1916 to see its practical adoption; and although the present measure is believed to apply only to the period of the war, I venture to express the opinion that after an experience of "summer time," as they Call it in Holland, and the delights of longer evenings after work is over, we shall never willingly go ,back to the old arrangement. Yours faithfully, HOLMAN SINGDON. • Bank Quay, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NQ GRAVEYARD.</head>
        <p>NQ GRAVEYARD.</p>
        <p>Latchford Without Burial Grievance</p>
        <p>Discussed At Vestry Meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Gorton, at the Easter vestry meeting for Christ Church, Latchford, which was held on Saturday evening, drew attention to the fact that the inhabitants of Latchford Without have no burial,ground. If their dead were buried at Grappenhall or Warrington, he said, double fees were charged, while the people of Latch- ford had the use of the Warrington Cemetery at ordinary rates. The parishioners of Latch- ford Without had been in communication with the Rector of Grappenhall, whose private opinion was that they were fully entitled to be buried at Grappenhall Church without double fees being incurred. He would like to know if Mr. Briggs had communicated with the Rector of Grappenhall, and if so, what was the result? The Vicar (the Rev. G. C. Briggs) said the question affected about 700 inhabitants. He had been in communication with Mr. Pigot on more than one occasion, and Mr. Pigot had told him, as he had told the Latchford Without Parish Council, that he was quite willing to admit the claim of the inhabitants of Latchford Without to burial in Grappenhall Churchyard, but that he did not know what the legal position was. He wished the Latchford Council to obtain a legal, opinion, asAhis was a civil, rather than an ecclesiastical question. The Latchford 'Without Parish. Council, it was explained, are in communiCation with the Chester Diocesan Registrars upon the question of the right of the inhabitants to burial in Grappenhall Churchyard. Mr. Gorton: I propose that the Vestry write to the Registrars asking their opinion as to our locus standi. The Vicar: That is the business of the Coun- cil and not of this Vestry. Mr. Gorton (who is a member of the Latch- ford Without Parish Council): We are waiting to see what action you are taking in the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Jenkins: That is not the case. Our Council were instructed to communicate with the Registrars, irrespective of any action this meeting might take. The Vicar: The position of the people of Latchford Without is, I should think, unique, as there is no hallowed ground where they may claim to have their bodies laid. We in the borough in Latchford can claim to be buried in the Cemetery. The parishioners of Grap- penhall can claim to be buried at Grappenhall, and the people at Stockton Heath at Stockton Heath, and so on. My own opinion is that the inhabitants of Latchford Without can claim burial at Grappenhall at ordinary fees, but it is a matter which should be definitely' settled, and I am glad to hear that the Council have decided to get a legal opinion on the question. The Vestry approved the action of the Latch- ford Council. RIGHT SPIRIT OF THE PEOPLE. '</p>
        <p>Mr. George Venn (vicar's warden). presented the accounts for the year; which showed that a balance due to the bank of £5 9s. sd. had been reduced to .P.. 3 16s. 3d. The special offertories realised £BO ss. 9d., and the general offertories £l5B 9s. 10d. The offertories, which showed an increase, had averaged £3 10s. 2d. per week.— The accounts were adopted. The Vicar thanked the parishioners for their Easter offering of £26 10s. 3d., and said he was glad to learn from Mr. Venn's statement that in spite of the numerous calls upon the charities of the people, the collections had increased. The number of communicants had been larger than ever, which showed that a right spirit existed among the people, and that they were attending church, and were remembering the bravo boys who were fighting their cause at the Front. The number of soldiers from the parish Witb the Colours was about 250, of whom 70 were communicants.</p>
        <p>The following appointments were made:— Vicars -warden,. Mr. J. W. C. Taylor; people's warden, Mr. F. Shprt. Sidesmen : Messrs. J. Lowe, George AiLenn, D. Banks, John Fairclough, R. T. Pairclough, J. P. Booth, H. Mockett, Joseph Banks, H. Bridge E. W. Simciick, J. Muskett, and J. Evans. Lay delegates: Messrs. R. T.' Fairclough and D. Banks; .auditor, Mr. Longhottom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>OBlTUARY.—Continued.</head>
        <p>OBlTUARY.—Continued.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. J. Hall, Mr. and Mrs. Ira Lamb, Church , Officers at St. Thomas',," Mary and Rose," "From all at the Grange, Antrobus," Mr. and Mrs. McDowell, " All at Over Hill, Hawarden," Mr. and Mrs. Robt. Smith, Frodsham ; Mr. Frank A. Milling, Mr. and Mrs. A. M. lier, Mr. and Mrs. E. L. Robinson, Mr. and Mrs. James Richardson, Mr. and Mrs. J. E. Birtles, "Lodge of Lights," 148; The Worshipful Master and brethren of the "Lodge of Friendship," 2963; Mr. and Mrs. A. T. Hallaway, Mr. and Mrs. W. Greenhouse, the employes of Messrs. Lee and Clarke, Joseph Clarke, "May and Aida," Mrs. Alfred Silcock and "Ida," Mr. and Mrs. 11. F. Greenough, Rifleman D. L. Oakes, Mr. and Mrs. William Roberts, "Elsie and Lynn." Mr. and Mrs. A. W. Paul, Miss Robinson, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Greenall. „. ' Greenouil and '.C.W. Daven- rPort had cluirge of the faneral arrangements.</p>
        <p>Boarded-out Children to be Visited.</p>
        <p>The Board of Guardians have decided .that Mrs. Hall, one of the two .lady members, shall visit the children who have been boarded-out into various districts.</p>
        <p>Cottage_ Homes' Band "Knocks Off."</p>
        <p>It has been decided that the school band of .• the Padgate Cottage homes shall be disbanded until the termination of the war, and that the bandmaster be informed that his services will be temporarily dispensed with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Scarlet Fever Dying.</head>
        <p>Scarlet Fever Dying.</p>
        <p>There were only five new -case-s of scarlet fever reported in Warrington last week, and only 39 local patients remained in hospital. • Other eases in hospital were :—Enteric fever, 2; diphtheria, 8; phthisis, 21.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Picture Papers to Instruct Children._</head>
        <p>Picture Papers to Instruct Children._</p>
        <p>Better to instruct in topical events the chil- dren at the Padgate Cottage Homes, the Board of Guardians on Wednesday decided that they should be served with two illustrated papers daily and one on Sunda</p>
        <p>Eggs and Entertainment at the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>On the initiative of the House Committee, the Board of Guardians, on Wednesday, decided tc tender their' best thanks to the female workers of Messrs. Greening and Sons, Ltd., for their kind gift of eggs to the inmates, and also to the children of. Miss Roscoliar's school for their excellent entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Buffaloes' Successful Sale.</head>
        <p>Buffaloes' Successful Sale.</p>
        <p>The committee of the R.A.0.8. jumble sale, which took place last month, desire to thank all donors and others who contributed towards the success of the undertaking and the R.A.0.8. Band for their services. As a result of the sale, .R4B is. 10d. has been handed over to the Mayor for his fund for local prisoners of war in Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Coincidence in Deaths.</head>
        <p>Coincidence in Deaths.</p>
        <p>During the week 'ended April 29th the deaths in Warrington numbered 26, the precise number recorded in the 'corresponding week last year. A further curious circumstance was that in each case the total of 26 was made up of 16 males and 10 females. Among ~the causes of death were :—Phthisis, 4; pneumonia, 3; heart disease, 3; whooping cough, 2; bronchitis, 2. Six Licences Transferred.</p>
        <p>The borough magistrates on 'Monday granted the following licensing transfers :—Off-licence, 58, Legh-street, from Elizabeth Frances Wat- kins to Joseph Holbrook; off-lieence, 17, Frog- hall-lane, from Louis Edward Jowett (deceased) to Clara Jowett; off-licence, 107, Manchester- road, from Mary Jane Colbourne to Wm. Evans Atherton; off-licence, 38, Hoyle-street, from Elizabeth Tickle to Mary Jane Roylance; Royal Oak Branch, beerhouse, linutsford-road, from George Tryner (deceased) to Jane Tryner ; Shakespeare, beerhouse, Pinner's Brow, from Wm. Lawless to Wm. Derbyshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>At the Council.</head>
        <p>At the Council.</p>
        <p>There was a poor attendance at the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday, and the business, which was of a formal character, was disposed of in ten minutes. The Mayor (Alderman Peter. Peacock) presided, , and was supported by the Deputy Mayor (Dr. Joseph), Aldermen A. Bennett, D. Tinnion, John Evans, James Evans; Councillors G. Dale, J. T. Barton, Firth Lindley, J. G. Craik, W. J. Wilson, J. Chrimes, E. Cunnington, F. T. Lashford, J. Pemberton. S., Gough, H. Roberts, J. Broad- hurst, R. Henshall, J. Wilkinson; and Mr. J. L. Whittle (town clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Guardians Who Do Not Attend Meetings.</head>
        <p>Guardians Who Do Not Attend Meetings.</p>
        <p>Those assiduous members of the Board of Guardians—the Rev. A. M. Mitchell and Mr. W. C. Brooks—gained the consent of the Board on Wednesday to the inclusion, of the attend- ances of individual members in the Year Book. In commending the proposal, the reverend gentleman said the attendance of some of the members was not very creditable, and he thought that if they saw their sins and negli- gence before them they might mend their ways. Mr. W. J. Shaw remarked that they always placed the attendances before the Public at election times—if they were good. (Laughter.) A good attendance' was "another stripe for the election banner:" '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>_Cheese-making in Cheshire.</head>
        <p>_Cheese-making in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>With a view to encou—raging cheese-making in the county, the Cheshire County Council has decided to promote classes of two weeks' dura- tion at centres in the county where such are desired, the services of a competent and ex- perienced person having been obtained for the purpose of cheese-making. The County Council appeal to farmers to co-operate with them in making such classes a success, and any farmer willing for his dairy to be utilised for the class, on stating the same to the Director of Educa- tion, City-road, Chester, will be at once com- municated with, and arrangements made for the holding of the class. • To-morrow's Anniversaries.</p>
        <p>Sunday school anniversary services will be held , to-morrow in the following churches : Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church: preacher, morning and evening, Rev. J. Grange Bennett; afternoon, special musical service. Baptist Church, Latchford: preacher, morning and evening, Mr. S. Albinson; afternoon, special musical service.</p>
        <p>At the. Welsh Church, Crosfield-street, the services will commence to-day, when the preacher will be the Rev. David Jones, Liver- pool; while to-morrow, at 10.30 and 6, the Rev. David Davies, Conway, will be the preacher. The above named services will be in, Welsh, but to-morrow afternoon the Rev. David Davies will conduct an English service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Women Do Well in Tan Yards.</head>
        <p>Women Do Well in Tan Yards.</p>
        <p>The Russian order is still the sole topic of conversation between tanners and factors, but tanners are not jet free to dispose of their stocks until the Government say so. This business is certainly causing a great amount of inconvenience to some of the tanners, writes the Warrington correspondent in the "Leather Trades Review," especially those who are not rblessed with too much warehousing room, and stocks suitable for this order have had to be turned over and over again in order to make room for quantities coming into the ware- houses week by week. The question of .labour also becomes worse every day, and tanners are being put to a very severe test, although I understand the women labour in the various yards is doing splendidly. Buyers have again been plentiful during the holiday week, but have found stocks very low, in fact leather for the ordinary trade is being readily snapped up in view of the shortage there is likely to be in the near future.</p>
        <p>Tribute To Deputy Borough. Surveyor. Mr. M. A. Piercy, the deputy engineer and sur- veyor, who has. won valuable premiums in con nection with towii-planning schemes, was heartily congratulated by Alderman. Bennett at the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday. The Alderman recalled that when Mr. Piercy came to Warrington, one of his recomv mendations was that he had won the second premium in an important town-planning scheme for Huddersfield. Recent events had shown,that that was not a solitary flash, and the capacity he displayed on that. occasion has been empha- sise4. As a result of the first success, he was asked to advise the Council of Ellesmere Fort, and more recently, entirely, in his own time, he took part in a still more import nit tow a-plan- ning compegtion for the city of York. The Council, wolild be pleased to hear that in that scheme also he came out second, and won a premium of £5O. It was highly creditable to Mr. Piercy and indirectly to themselves. They ought to congratulate themselves upon having a deputy surveyor who was an expert on matters ,of that kind. (Hear, hear.) It was specially important in Warrington, because they had a town-planning scheme of their own, and it was a great advantage to have the ability of Mr. Piercy to assist them in making the scheme the very best possible. (Hear, hear.Y—Councillor ' Pemberton endorsed Alderman -Bennett's tribute. He had, he said, conversed with the adjudicator (Professor. Abercrombie, of Liver- pool University) and found that he thought very highly of Mr. Piercy's scheme. As we had a town-planning scheme of. our own there ,was no doubt Mr. Piercy would be a yet great use to us. '(Hotie,lieiati . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Pound Day at Raddon Court.</head>
        <p>Pound Day at Raddon Court.</p>
        <p>W,e4nesday, will be Pound Day at Raddon CAmire 'Red Cross Hospital, when all gifts, espe- cially groceries, eggs and jam, will be gratefully received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Late Mrs. Smethurst.</head>
        <p>The Late Mrs. Smethurst.</p>
        <p>The Mayor announced at Tuesday's Town Council meeting that he had received a letter from Mr. J. H. Smethurst expressing his own and his family's appreciation of the resolution of condolence passed owing to the death of Mrs. Smethurst, the wife of Alderman Smethurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Late Vicar's Son Wounded,:</head>
        <p>Late Vicar's Son Wounded,:</p>
        <p>It is unofficially announced to-day that Lieutenant and Adjutant G. J. L. Slater, of the Worcestershire Regiment, was killed in -action on April 30th. He was a son .of the late 'Rev. Francis Slater, formerly vicar of St. James', Latchford, Warrington. Concert at Golborne Street.</p>
        <p>A concert, organised by Mr. W. Pearce in aid of the Church Fund of the Golborne-street Baptist Church, was given on Thursday even- ing, the Rev. H. Ogden presiding. The concert was of a very enjoyable character. The artistes were: Miss Rigby and Miss Blackburne, and Messrs. Hannan, Starkey and Pearce. Nationalisation of the Drink Trade Advocated.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Branch of the British Women's Temperance Association was held in the Bold-street Church Parlour on Wednesday, when the Rev. Percy meson was the speaker, and gave an excellent and helpful address, in the course of which he strongly advocated the nationalisation of the drink trade. The meeting was well attended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Appreciation of School Mistress's</head>
        <p>Appreciation of School Mistress's</p>
        <p>Services.</p>
        <p>It was reported at the meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday, that the Padgate Cottage Homes Committee had placed on record their high appreciation of the special work per- formed by Mrs. Hi'ditch, schoolmistress, under abnormal conditions; and, further, she be in- formed that at an early date the committee in- tended recommending the Board to recognise these services in a substantial tangible manner. Dr. Barnardo's</p>
        <p>The annual box-opening in connection with the Young Helpers' League has resulted in the following amounts being raised :—Collected in the boxes, £9 Bs. 5d.; subscriptions, £2 135.; annual donation of the Warrington Co-opera- tive Society, £5 55.; total, £l7 6s. sd. A silver badge has been gained by Ruth Garland. The hon. secretary of the Warrington Habitation is Mrs. Woodcock, 62, Whitfield-road, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>• Dr. Grunshaw's Dardanelles Lecture. We are asked by the British Red Cross Society to express the thanks of the promoters to the Mayor for granting the use of the Parr Hall and for his presence at Dr. Grimshaw's lecture on the Dardanelles on Saturday; to the Chair- man (Councillor Henry Roberts) for presiding, to Mrs. W. B. Roberts's orchestra, to Mr. Harold Smith for the use of the lantern and supplying an operator, and to other ladies and gentlemen for helping to make the lecture so successful. A report of the lecture appears elsewhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>LONG SERVICE MEDALS</head>
        <p>LONG SERVICE MEDALS</p>
        <p>Presented At St. John Ambu-</p>
        <p>lance Whist Drive.</p>
        <p>The interior of the Parr Hall last night pre- sented quite a cheery appearance. The occasion was the holding of a whist drive and dance, promoted by the Warrington Division of St. John Ambulance Brigade, in aid of the Pris- oners of War Fund. There were 54 tables in use, and the company included the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) and Mrs. Hy. Greenell. The secretarial arrange- ments were in the capable hands of Mrs. Fisher, assisted by Mrs. Warhurst, and a number of other ladies prominently identified with the nursing division of the Brigade.</p>
        <p>During an interval a number of presenta- tions were made. The Mayor presented Mr. Baldry, the superintendent of the men's divi- sion, with the long service medal, that official having completed twenty years' service, while Corporal J. Lee was the recipient of a similar distinction.</p>
        <p>Mrs.. Henry Greenall handed over a number of certificates, medallions, labels and voucher. 3 to a number of ladies who had successfully passed examinations. The recipients were : First aid certificates: Miss Harper, Miss Wil- liams, Miss Gregg, Miss Barlow, Miss Hough, Miss Hilton, Miss H. Hilton, Miss Michael, Mrs. Kempster, Mrs. Holt, Mrs., Leigh, Mrs. Reid, Mrs. Williamson. Vouchers: Mrs. Berry, Miss Stevens. Medallions: Mrs. Boulton, Mrs. Monks, Miss Burgess, Miss • Rose, Miss Wil- Jiams, Miss Appleton, Miss Dobbins, Miss Malone. , Labels (4th examination) : Miss Ban- ning, Miss Williams. Mrs. Fisher, Mrs. War- hurst (sth examination).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TRIBUTES TO MANY.</head>
        <p>TRIBUTES TO MANY.</p>
        <p>Rev. W. Bracecarnp's Message To St. Paul's Vestry Meeting.</p>
        <p>The feature at St. Paul's Vestry meeting, held last night, under the chairmanship of the Rev. Edward Downham, was a letter sent by the Vicar (the Rev. W. Bracecamp), who is serving as an Army Chaplain in the South- east of England. The reverend gentleman nominated as his warden, Mr. J. H. Smethurst, who was one of the very best, and spoke highly of the people's warden (Mr. A. C. Binks), who was reappointed. It had been a great comfort to him (the Vicar) in his lbng absence to feel that he had such loyal clergy, wardens, sidesmen, choir, and such a body of voluntary workers. When The Boys Come Home.</p>
        <p>Requesting the sidesmen to be' assiduous in the carrying out of their duties, the Vicar pro- ceded: "The boys will come home from the Front changed for the better In every way. Therefore, those at home must make them feel happy and comfortable when they come to church. . . . Things in the war zone are looking better, and I am afraid worse has to come yet. I should like a vote of condolence to be passed to the friends of those who have been called to lay down their lives for their King and country in our parish; also I should like a letter to be sent from the vestry to Colonel George Crosfield for his conspicuous bravery in this struggle, and that the earnest prayers of the vestry be offered that his life may be spared. Colonel Crosfield's Character.</p>
        <p>He is one of the finest characters, whom it has been my privilege to know, and if he and those ,who thought like him had been listened to there would not have been this terrible war." The Vicar's suggestions were acquiesced in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Small Adverse Balance.</head>
        <p>Small Adverse Balance.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. C. Binks (people's warden) presented the accounts, which showed that the Special offertories amounted to XB5 3s. 6d., and the ordinary offertories to £l6O Is. 10d. The ,col- lections for charities totalled £52 Os. 2d. There was an adverse balance of a 13s. 11d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Sidesmen.</head>
        <p>The Sidesmen.</p>
        <p>The sidesmen were re-elected as follows : Messrs. H. Broadhurst, S. Bailey, G. H. Ing- ram, H. Flood, W. Caulk, H. F. Greenough, T. Locker, S. A. H. Mackey, T. Seed, H. Collin, F. Lindley, J. Lawton, James Hough- ton, J. W. Parker, H. Hosker, E. White, R. G. S. Finney, G. Newburn, S. Lawton, T. Arm- strong, J. Wainwright, W. T. Flood, James Fern, W. Loxham, H. L. Clarke, W. Bobbing- ton, W. H. Thompson, W. Woodcock, W. L. Clarke, D. Warburton, J. Hayes, J. E. Good- win, W. McG. Lee, J. R. Deakin, and F. J. Dunn.</p>
        <p>Votes of condolence were alto passed to the relatives of the late Miss Westwell and Mrs - Smethurst. Christ for the Nation. Commending the National Mission, which is to commence soon, the Chairman said the," must have realised that civilisation and philosophy had failed. The Church, too, had failed in this country and 'elsewhere, and they must all get back to Christ to save them. In Christ they would and inspiration, power and hope for tiv: nation, as well as for the individual. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>JOIN THE VOLUNTEER; CORPS.</head>
        <p>JOIN THE VOLUNTEER; CORPS.</p>
        <p>Tribunal Chairman's Advice To Temporarily Exempted Men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SOLDIERS' WIVES' CULINARY HABITS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' WIVES' CULINARY HABITS.</p>
        <p>Presidjng at the Tribunal at the Town Hall, .Yesterday afternoon, Alderman Tinnion made 411 important suggestion to men who received &gt;conditional exemption. The Tribunal, said Alderman Tinnion, re- Sarded_ the matter as one of great importance. There were many "starred" men in Warring- ton, and others were engaged in occupations of .nuch national importance that the Tribunal, after considering their cases, decided to grant :Conditional exemption. They thought it was the duty of men who obtained such exemption to join the Warrington Volunteer Training Corps, or Citizens' Army, and they hoped they would see it in that light. The Tribunal felt that these men should do something to helpl their country. Many of them, but for the im- .Portant positions they held, would willingly lave joined the Army. They could, at any rate, do something for their country by join- ing the Citizens' Army, and they would also De doing themselves good. They never knew what the circumstances might be, and if at llorne future time they were called upon for vice,, it would be of great assistance to the 3nilitary authorities if they had qualified ;themselves by training.</p>
        <p>FORGETFULNESS AND GRUESOMENESS. Mr. J. H. Smethurst, chairman of the Board of Management of the Infirmary, appealed on behalf of two men, one, the porter, and the other, the engineer and electrician. With regard to the porter, Mr. Smethurst said he was the man who in ordinary times was assistant porter, but the senior had been called up to his regiment, and they were trying-to manage with the applicant and a girl. He was married. "As far as the assistant porter at the door is concerned," added Mr. Smethurst, "we have had girls, but -they had not enough nous, and did not always deliver messages to the doctors when sent." In the event of post- mortem examinations taking place the porter had to clean things up, and bury or burn what had been extracted or cut away from bodies. Not every man could do that. At the beginning the man complained that "it turned him up," but now he is "case-hardened." It was suggested that a retired soldier might' be found to do the portering. "it is certainly work for an old Soldier," observed the Chairman (Dr. G. W. Joseph). Three months' exemption was allowed. In the meantime an effort is to be made to find a suit- able.discharged soldier. Conditional exemption .was given to the electrician and engineer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TO JOIN CITIZEN'S ARMY.</head>
        <p>TO JOIN CITIZEN'S ARMY.</p>
        <p>The manager of the Maypole Dairy Corn- 41any's shop at Market Gate was granted con- ,ditional exemption. The appellant said there was only another man in the shop and he was ,called up on May 29th. In giving the decision of the Tribunal, the 'Chairman said there was no •reason why a man like the applicant should not join the Citizens' Army in his spare time. He (the Chairman) 'said this not only in the man's own interests, 'but in the interests of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ONLY SALESMAN LEFT.</head>
        <p>ONLY SALESMAN LEFT.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Sterling and. Co.'s manager of the tridge-street shop, was granted conditional ex- _eznption. He said he was the only man engaged in the sales department. On being asked to join the Citizen's Army, •alVellant said he would be glad to do so.</p>
        <p>TOO YOUNG FOR TOTAL EXEMPTION. A butcher in a large way of business in llorsemarket-street applied for the exemption of his son who, he said, did the slaughtering, buy- ing, and generally helped to manage the busi- iless. He had another man who, he believed, Was in the last group. He had two sons in Australia who had joined the Army there, and another son who had joined a Canadian Regi- raent. One son had been killed.</p>
        <p>Temporary exemption for three months was granted, the Chairman remarking that the man 'Was too young to be totally exempted, but he 'loped he would join the Citizens' Army and train in 'his spare time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ALL I N KHAKI.</head>
        <p>ALL I N KHAKI.</p>
        <p>Mr. James 6ibson, plumber and painter, appealing for his son, a painter, said previous to the war he had six men in his employ, and they were all in khaki now. Six months' ex- omption granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GREENGROCER'S PREDICAMENT.</head>
        <p>GREENGROCER'S PREDICAMENT.</p>
        <p>A young man carrying. on a greengrocery business on his own account, appealed, and he was given two months in which satisfactorily to arrange his business. He was told that he had better face the possibility of having to dispose of his business, to which appellant replied "that it, was very hard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>COMPETITORS OF GERMANY.</head>
        <p>COMPETITORS OF GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Netherwood, on behalf of the United *Velvet Association, applied for the exemption of a tooler, who, 'he said, was' indispensable if 'the company were to compete successfully and maintain its output. He mentioned that a big 'German velvet manufacturing company applied to the German Home Office to have the royalty Paid on one of the company's patents sold to a German firm of machine makers reduced to Practically nothing.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: You are not surprised at this ? Mr. Netherwood : No. Continuing, Mr. Netherwood said the German velvet manufactur- ing company were their biggest competitors, and the German Home Office granted the applica- tion, but in making the concession the •German Rome Office stipulated that 25 more machines should be installed before the end of March next; otherwise the concession would be with- drawn. The Chairman inquired where Mr. Nether- wood had got this information, and Mr. Nether- Wood said he had got the papers in reference to it. It was a Dutch patent agent who acquainted his agent and, of course, they appealed against this.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It is a question of a small scrap of paper. Mr. Netherwood: That is so. The excuse they /flake is that the British are confiscating Ger- Inan patents, and I think there is something in as well. Mr. Netherwood added that his contention was that if the Germans could keep at home increasing a new and important Industry, we at least ought to be able to retain what men we had got. The Tribunal granted exemption till June .20th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WOMEN'S VAGARIES.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S VAGARIES.</p>
        <p>'Give Up Hot Dinners While Husbands</p>
        <p>Are Away.</p>
        <p>'Two remarkable facts respecting .the habits of Irctmen in war-time were divulged to the 'Tribunal at the Town Hall, on Thursday.. One was that since the war began the sale of bread at the Co-operative Stores had gone up 13Y one hundred sacks per week, and is accounted for by the fact that instead of cooking dinners, as they did when their husbands were at home. Women eat more bread because it is easier to Prepare a meal.</p>
        <p>The other was that at the Infirmary. female Porters were tried; but were unsuccessful be- cause they failed to deliver messages promptly and correctly to the doctors. Sometimes they completely forgot. Therefore, a man was neces- sary for the position.</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE STORES' CLAIM. 'The. Tribunal was occupied an hour and a half in hearing the case put forward by the Co-. Stores in respect of a confidential 'Clerk, eight grocery shop managers, five bakers, A motorman, lnanager of the butcher's. shop, a "Warehouseman, and 'four coal carters.' The facts as addressed by Mr. Jarman and Mr. Atkinson, secretary and manager respectively, Were that.they had sixteen branch shops, taking oa an average from £2OO (in the case of the 'Blcallest shop) to £5OO each per week. The total "grocery. trade in the last three months averaged ',.500 per Week. It was impossible to run the `lncps entirely by females. At the beginning of 'cue war they had sixty men over eighteen years -af age in the grocery shops. Thirty-four had '4lready enlisted, and eighteen more had attested, nd would be called up in their groups. That ..left eight, for whom they were appealing. Yesterday afternoon the Chairman said the Tribunal felt that the society would experi- 'ence•no hardship if they (the Tribunal) took 'away a proportion of the branch managers, 'arid that with, a rearrangement of the staff the Ilerviee should be carried on in an efficient Winer. ~ The Tribunal granted conditional exemption to four branch managers (out Of eight), whose aR CA ranged from 40 to 34. A motor driver „rag granted temporary exemption till July 8t The manager of a branch butcher's shop :was allowed exemption till June 30th. ~, Out of five bakers who were appealed for, I.9ur were granted conditional exemption and 'lle other refused. Exemption for one coal carter was refused. Conditional exemption 3:48 granted to three others. The head ware- q_°llsernan, head clerk and the drapery man- Were conditionally exempted. -Ile representatives of the society expressed ' th k s to the Tribunal, and intimated that the ,10ciety would have to do its best under the -7,4Clitastancos. _ . • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>BROTHER IN IRELAND.</head>
        <p>A BROTHER IN IRELAND.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting cases heard—or part-heard—was the adjourned one in which the Tribunal had requested that the brother of an applicant (a marine store dealer), who was said to be in Ireland, should be present. Mr. T. S. Steel, for the applicant, said that on the last adjournment he wrote a straight and direct letter to the doctor, who was attending the brother in Ireland, and explained the whole citcumstances to him, saying that a certificate must either be sent stating that it was impos- sible for him to travel or the young man must be present himself., A certificate, dated March 18th, had been received stating that the brother was suffering from curvature of the spine and debility, and it would be at the risk of his life if he travelled.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel added that he sent another letter to the doctor on Monday, on hearing that the case was to come on that day. Owing to the trouble in Ireland, however, there had been no reply. The Chairman: Was he suffering from curva- ture of the spine when he left here for Ireland? Travelling in a case of that kind would not endanger his life., Mr. Steel: There is debility also. Captain Mathams : Could not we adjourn the case for a military examination in Ireland? This suggestion wa :31 agreed to, and Mr. Steel handed the Captain the absent brother's address.</p>
        <p>SMASHED! t • Mr. T. S. Steel applied on behalf of a yoyaig man who carried on business in Liverpoollas a claims assessor in mdtor smashes. He was agent for Lloyd's and the Motor Union Insur- ance Company, representing these in a district which extended from Holyhead to Carlisle. The claims with which he dealt involved about £25,000 per year. The applicant had only been passed for clerical work in the Army. Refused.</p>
        <p>LUCK 1.</p>
        <p>A boot repairer stated he thought he was unfit for the Army and explained that the reason he had not been to the Drill• Hall to be medically examined was because he had heard of a man who had been going regularly for a month and had not been examined yet. He (the applicant) could not afford to waste his time in that manner.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. T. Hallaway (the Clerk) : If you were lucky you might be going for a year or two and the war would be over. (Laughter.) The case was adjourned for a medical exam- ination, the applicant agreeing to go to the Drill Hall on Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>FARM LABOUR SHORTAGE.</head>
        <p>FARM LABOUR SHORTAGE.</p>
        <p>Rural Tribunal Chairman And</p>
        <p>The Difficulty.</p>
        <p>The shortage of farm labourers .was again emphasised at a meeting of the Warrington and District Tribunal, held on Thursday afternoc\n. Previous to hearing the claims, the Chairman (Mr. G. Mather) said fresh instructions , had been received from headquarters, but as far as he could see there was no fundamental change in the instructions.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Except in respect of age. - Mr. Mather: We have to take each case on its merits. •</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said in a week or two they would have to deal with the new Act, and there would,he trouble about it.</p>
        <p>An Indispensable Sankey Son.</p>
        <p>A Great Sankey farmer applied for the exemp- tion of his son, single, 27, on the ground that he was indispensable to a farm of 115 acres, lie had another son, but he was unable to take part in the mai}agement of the, farm owing to optic ailment. He had no other male on the farm, and the son he was appealing for was teamsman, horseman, stacker, &amp;c. There were 15 milking cows, other cattle and six horses on the farm. Conditional exemption for three. months was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Solicitor's Suggestion.</head>
        <p>A Solicitor's Suggestion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbrook appeared on behalf of a Sankey farmer who appealed for the exemption of his son, aged 20. • Appellant's father farmed 50 acres, of which 40 was ploughed. There was also a milk round in Warrington of some 100 customers served twice daily. Since th appeal was lodged, Mr. Westbrook Said, he had had a talk with the father, and the applicant, and, although there was. good claim for exemp- tion, the needs of the nation were paraniount over private concerns. He had a suggestion to make ' to - tlie Tribunal that time should be allowed appellant so that his father could make arrangements for the aisposal of the business or part of it. There was also the setting of potatoes, getting in the hay and portion of the harvest, and he suggested the man should be given till September Ist. He would then join the Colours on that date.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal granted one month's exemption on the understanding that the young man joined the Colours at the end of that period. Later Mr. Westbrook asked the Tribunal to extend the exemption until July Ist. This was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Suffitient For Small Farm.</head>
        <p>Suffitient For Small Farm.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal refused the appeal of a Raton farmer for his son, aged 20. He farmed 51 acres of land, and employed in all himself, three sons, a man 69, and two boys. The Tribunal was of opinion that fora small farm there- was sufficient labour employed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Building A House For Himself.</head>
        <p>Building A House For Himself.</p>
        <p>A young man who is engaged on building a house for himself, and who had been granted by the Tribunal temporary exemption, applied for .a further extension of six weeks in order to complete his building arrangements. Appellant, who failed to appear, had his application refused. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>"Why Not Say a Girl?"</head>
        <p>"Why Not Say a Girl?"</p>
        <p>A Sankey farmer appealing for his•son, aged 19, a farrier, said his was the only farrier's establishment between Sankey and St. Helens. Mr. Knowles remarked that if more farriers were sent to the Front, horses would soon have to go unshod.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Could not a boy be found to do the work?</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: Why not say a girl? (Laugh- ter.)</p>
        <p>The Tribunal granted the appellant two months' exemption. Farm Labourers At A Premium.</p>
        <p>Commenting on an applicant's appeal for his son, aged 20, as he was unable to get farm labourers, the Chairman said no matter how much advertising was done and how much money was. offered, farm labourers could not be .obtained: _ . , . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</p>
        <p>£1 FOR 15s. 6D.</p>
        <p>Time Coming When £1 Saved Now Will Be Worth £2. WAR SAVINGS CERTIFI- ' CATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chambers, of the National War Savings Committee, explained to a meeting held in the council Chamber, on Thursday evening, under the chairmanship of the Mayor '(Alderman .Peter %Peacock), the• Government sckelue of war savings certificates for the people under which deposits of 15s. 6d. become £1 in five years' time, having in the meantime assisted the Government to win the war. He deprecated strongly the use of the word thrift, a word to which a very wrong and unpopular .meaning had been given. But money. was seriously needed by the country, and if was the clear duty of all non-combatants, as a matter Of patriotism, to save all the money they could for the purpose of placing it at the disposal of the country in its present need. Besides benefiting the national cause, these si;vings were in the best family interest. Prices were so high now that the purchasing power of otl had been materially lowered. A short time after the conclusion of war prices would lose much of their present 50 per cent. increase. These invested fifteen and sixpences would then become ..tl, and those sovereigns would be able to purchase 'as much as £2 would purchase now. Added to these facts the cir- cumstance that there is Government security the value of present saving for future gain was fully established. Our Serious Financial Responsibilities.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chambers explained that the country is spending £1,825,000,000, of which £1,625,000,000 represents the cost :of the war. This money has to be, found by us, because there is prac- tically no other country who can help us, though last year America lent us and France the comparatively rnall sum of £100,000,000. Of the needed money we were raising by taxa- tion this year £500,000,000, two-and-a-half- times as much as we ever raised in taxation before; ,but still .there remained £1,325,000,000 to be raised by loan from tho people of this country, and it was desirable that •it should be raised by all classes of the community. it was unfortunate that very few people were interested in the securities of the Government in this country. In France it was different, for the people invested largely in nation..l securities, which was a healthy means of givin; all an interest in the concerns of the.couniry. He then proceeded to show how all could do their bit towards helping, he would not gay to crush Germany, but towards preventing Ger- many from crushing us. All persons should give up buying unnecessary things'. All ex- penditure involved employment, and the more labour engaged upon the meeting of our de- mands meant less labour upon work upon which the success of the war depends. The Board of Trade reiterated the growing scarcity of labour in all trades connected with war. If e caused people to do unnecessary work we were preventing them from doing their duty to the coantry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Saving In Groups.</head>
        <p>Saving In Groups.</p>
        <p>There was a direet connection between economy and war finance—we could transfer the money to -the State instead of using the spending p.f.% ourseh es. The general scheme ofqhe National War Savingt; Committee sought to set up 1-cal Central t. nininit, whose twt) principal functions were to create an etmos- phere in their area in which the necessities of the situation would be realised, and to en- courage the setting up of local War Savings Associations among different groups of people. These associations could be set up by any group of people, however small, accustomed to come together in ordinary times—trade unions, friendly societies, co-operative societies, wot-ks' people attending the same place of worship, people who worked together in the same shop, Boy Scouts, and Girl Guides. The groups would manage their own affairs in democratic fashion, being supplied free with the forms for saving and transferring money, and the keeping of accounts. There were two methods of saving—by exchequer bonds and by war savings certificates. He advocated espe- cially the formation of associations for saving by means of the second method, war savings certificates, the best offer ever made to the people of a national sectrity. They bore compound interest and rose in value :—At the end of the first year, 15s. 9d.; second year, 16s. 9d.; third year, 17s. 9d.; fourth year, 18s. 9d.; fifth year, ,tl. The simplest form of association was this :-- Thirty-one people agree to pay 6d. a week. The secretary takes the thirty-one sixpences to the Post Office vid buys a certificate with the 15s. 6d. The next week he buys another with the second thirty-one sixpences, and so on. The question of who of the thirty-one should have the first certificate could- be settkd b'- them: selves. There might be one -model association under the auspices of the local committee. The money could be drawn out at any time. Saving In Schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jos. Elwell was sure the working men would make use of this method of saving when they understood it thoroughly. . _</p>
        <p>In answer to the Director Of Education (Mr. J. M. Murray),, Mr. Chambers explained that in some towns school savings banks for the purchase of certi- ficates were very successful. At one school tha children brought in £650 in four weeks, parents having evidently decided to invest after the children had told them what they had been taught on the subject at school. Mr. Stanley Jones said at his own, school children brought as much as ss. a week to be saved by the headmaster for their partnts. If the people were made familiar with this scheme, the parents being reached through the scholars, much good .might be done amongst that class of working people who did not wish to lose control of what they considered "their own money."</p>
        <p>Mr. Bowen, secretary of the Joint Engineer- ing Trades Committee, said he would bring the matter before his members at the next general meeting. , Mr. Joseph Heyes (steam engine makers) said it was a mistakcoto think engineers were making 02.5 a week nowadays; a great many were making little over .02. _ _</p>
        <p>It was re-solved, on the motion of the Director of Education, seconded by the Rev. W. T. Derry, and supported by Mr. C. E. Parker, to take steps to bring the matter before the notice of the public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MASTER.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER.</p>
        <p>Regarded As. Indispensable To Board</p>
        <p>Of Guardians.</p>
        <p>That Mr. Loxham, tile master of the Work- house, is indispensable to that institution, is to be. submitted toothe Tribunal by the Board of Guardians if he is called np for military service. It wits generally agreed at the Guardians' meeting on Wednesday, that Mr. Loxham's duties are highly importnnt',and onerous. Thetqlerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) observed 'that if any'man could -be considered indispensable Mr. Loxham was in that 'category. ,13e§ides, at any tiei 350 soldiers might arrive at the lio§pital,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>FOUGHT AT WATERLOO.</head>
        <p>FOUGHT AT WATERLOO.</p>
        <p>Great-Grandson Congratulated By</p>
        <p>Board W Guaidians.</p>
        <p>The fact that he is a great grandson of one who fought at Waterloo was recalled by Miss Broadbent at the Board Of Guardians on Wed- nesday, in proposing that best wishes should be extended to Mr. Fred Hanipson, superintendent's clerk at the Padgate Cottage Homes on his hav- ing joined the Forces. _ _</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent said they all wished him good luck. He was only lg years of age, and she was sure he would bring credit to the military family to which he belonged. His great-grandfather served at Waterloo, his grandfather ser+ed 21 years, his father had been in the Army, and his only brother was also serving. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell, seconding, said they ought to extend every possible encouragement to Mr. Hampson, for as soon as he became old enough to enlist he did'so. The motion was carried enthusiastically. The young soldier is the second son of Mr. J. 11, J. Hampson, secretary to the Warrington Infirmary.. Another of Yr. Ilampsons sons is Secorid-Lieutenant in the Lancashire Fusiliers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. S. MAYERS.</p>
        <p>Well-known Local D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Killed By Explosion Of Mine.</p>
        <p>Honoured with the D.C.M. a few months ago, Lance-Corporal Samuel Mayers, of the 173rd Company of the Royal Engineers, has not lived long to enjoy the distinction, for news has this week reached his wife at Manchester to the effect that he has been killed in action. The sad news was conveyed in a letter written by a Captain, who only announced the bare facts, but in response to a communication to the War -Office, the following notice, dated May 4th. yesterday reached the dead soldier's mother- -Mrs. Fanny Mayers, 80, Orford-avenue: "Cor- poral Samuel Mayers, D.C.M., Royal Engineers, was killed on April 17th by an explosion from an enemy mine." The deed for which the gallant Lance.. Corporal was rewarded, was performed in October, 1915, when the Germans broke into a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DIED OF *WOUNDS.</head>
        <p>DIED OF *WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. VARLEY.</p>
        <p>Dies In Hospital From Wounds In Side, Chest And Leg.</p>
        <p>• Through the medium of a letter received from an Army Chaplain, Mr. and Mrs. Varley, 4, Edward-street, Bewsey-road, have been informed that their son, Private John Varley, of the Cameron Highlanders, has died from wounds i hospital. The letter, written on May Ist, ran:-- "Your boy was brought into hospital tw•-) or three days ago, badly wounded.. Every care and attention was given to him, but he did not im- prove, and at 8.3 G this morning he died. I gave him the last sacraments upon admittance, and shall bury him to-day. I am sorry that I have such bad news to send you, but I am sure the knowledge that he received the last rites, and that he sacrificed himself in Almighty God's cause will bring you consolation. May God bless and strengthen you in your trouble." An intimation from the hospital, received pre-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>RIFLEMAN J. GRIFFITHS</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>ported missing after an action with the Ger- mans on the 3rd of May last year, has now been assumed to be dead by the military authorities; and an intimation to that effect has been received from Winchester.</p>
        <p>'Rifleman Griffiths, who was in the Rifle Bri- gade, was one of three soldier-brothers—Harry, who was wounded in the Territorial charge last June; and William; who served in the Boer War, going out twice to South Africa, first with the Volunteers and then with the Yeomanry. The present trouble finds him again in arms in support' of his Country. • Rifleman Griffiths, who was 25 years of age, attended the National Schools as a boy, and then worked for Messrs. Rylands Bros. for a spell. At the outbreak of the war he was employed in the rivet department at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s works. His mother last heard from him on April 29th, 1915, a few days before he was reported missing, when be said he was "all right."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>TAX SURPRISE.</head>
        <p>TAX SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>Entertainments' Levy Affects</p>
        <p>Clubs And All Games.</p>
        <p>Those who think the entertainments tax; ,which is to be imposed on the 15th inst., 'only applies to cinema houses, theatres, music halls, -football and cricket grounds, will be greatly surprised to know that it is much more extensive than that, for from a perusal of the official leaflet which is being sent round, it is gathered that club subscriptions, bowling, golf, billiards, 'dancing, music, and whist drives come within , its scope, if they are not indulged in-on behalf ' of charity. Like many Government documents, the clauses are obscure and complicated, but a corre- spondent who has made a study of them con- cludes that the Act applies in all cases where there is payment for admission to an enter- tainment, and, according to the document, the meaning of " entertainment " includes any ex- hibition performance, amusement, game, or sport to which persons are admitted for pay- ment. Games, therefore, include golf, tennis, billiards and bowls. If any of these are carried on at a club and payment is made by a lump sum—Say, a subscription for a season ticket— those members also are Aable, so that a guinea subscription will mean 4s. extra. Their being seven months of the year to gq.,Soyen-tlielfths of 2s. is due to the Commissioners of Revenue.</p>
        <p>..</p>
        <p>Latchford Locks. He joined the Army soon after the outbreak of the war. Another brother is employed on munition work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL JOHN PARSON.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>AN OMINOUS QUESTION.</head>
        <p>AN OMINOUS QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Coun. Roberts And Composition Of War Pensions Committee.</p>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL NOMINEES.</p>
        <p>It will be recollected that a few weeks ago the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) called a con- ference of trade union officials 1 to nominate their representatives on the Naval and War Pensions Committee, which is in the course of formation in Warrington. •Of the six gentlemen nominated, four are prominent members of the Trades Council, these being Messrs. Dukes, Plinston, Seabury, and Roole, the others recom- mended 'being Messrs. Baker and Hodson. These facts must be borne in mind to under, stand qtierie put to the Mayor by Counbillor Henry Roberts at the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday. The General Purposes Committee had resolved "That the Mayor, Alderman Tinnion, and Councillors Chrimes, Henshall, Lindley and Roberts be appointed a sub-committee to con- sider and report on the conditions and allow- ances affecting employes of the Corporation who have or may attest in H.M. Forces "; and "that the draft scheme under the Naval and Military War Pensions, &amp;c., Act, 1915, as prepared and approved by the sub-committee, be approved and submitted to the Statutory Committee." .• . Approval of the minutes baying been proposed And seconded, Councillor Roberts said: "I should like to ask a question, Mr. Mayor, so that the position may be ,perfectly clear: I take it we are simply adopting the scheme submitted by the sub-com- mittee under which the persons to constitute the Pensions Committee will afterwards ,be appointed. The Mayor: That is so. Councillor Roberts: And what I am anxious to get your clear definition of is this: Shall we, as, a Council, have an ample opportunity, after due • notice, of considering the names recom- mended and of objecting to any of them, and refusing the names if. we think fit? The Mayor; Yes, entirely. Councillor Roberts: This is the deciding body as to the personnel of this Pensions Committee F , The Mayor :•That,is so. • - Councillor Roberts: I am much obliged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>IRISH AT GALLIPOLI,</head>
        <p>IRISH AT GALLIPOLI,</p>
        <p>Unexcelled Bravery The Rebels Should Remember.</p>
        <p>SURGEON'S LECTURE IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>"In these days.of dislualty the Irish rebels should remember the rublin and Munster Fusiliers, who exhibited in Gallipoli bravery and courage which are, indeed, the envy of men."</p>
        <p>So remarked Dr. John Grimahaw, a surgeon, in the course of a lecture describing the Dardanelles campaign to, a crowded audience assembled in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening. The-lecture was illustrated by lantern slides. Dr. Grimshaw. went to. the Dardanelles on the troopship Aquitania, and was there a consider- able time. In graphic manner he described what he had seen himself or- heard from men on the spot in particular actions. He was at Lemnos for .a time, and, becoming very deter- mined to see something of the actual scene of the fighting, managed to get on a trawler, despite the advice of officers that he should not risk his life. In the trawler were some of the Lancashire Fusiliers, who were returning to the firing line after three days' rest, following three months in the trenches. Yet he heard no "grousing "; instead, cheerfulness and camaradie prevailed. Pipes were smoked, mouth organs were played, and songs were the order all through the night voyage. This, in spite of the fact that they were returning to a Hell on earth! Exploits of a Politician.</p>
        <p>What the lecturer characterised as the "night of his life" was spent on the trawler off Cape Heles, where he was an awed and hushed spec- tator of war on land and sea, for he could see the flash' and heard the thunder of the Army and' Naval-guns. Frenzied flames and peals of thunder were seen and heard with remarkable frequency. It was terrific.</p>
        <p>_ Regard_ing the 'Gallipoli enterprise generally, Dr. Grimshaw said that never was an army in such a sorry plight, excepting perhaps at Kut. Never had the exploits of a politician landed a British soldier in such a mess. Three words had become notorious—Lemnos, Mudros and Chaos. The dice was loaded against us when we undertook the great gamble. The lecture was delivered in aid of the funds of the British Red Cross Society and the Prisoners of War Fund. Commending these objects, Councillor Henry Roberts, the chair- man, said the war had given opportunities for personal service such as no other institution in our country had afforded. The women of Eng- land and the Empire had risen magnificently to the occasion, and in connection with the British Red Cross Society and its varied allied associations they had done work which would place on record for all time tile splendid charac- tor of the British women of to-day. (Applause.) The Chairman went on to commend the objects of the gathering, especially the provision of a motor ambulance which the local branch is intent on providing for the Front. In regard .to our prisoners of war, he added, we knew from what we had read .that our men were not treated as they ought to be across the North Sea; in fact, their treatment afforded a striking contrast to the way in which the Germans were catered for to-day. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>COL. CROSFIELD, D.S.O.</p>
        <p>Writes A Letter To The Town</p>
        <p>Council.</p>
        <p>STILL A SPORTSMAN.</p>
        <p>At the Town Council on Tuesday, the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) informed the members that he had -received a letter from Colonel G. R. Crosfield thanking the Council and. the towns- people generally for their kind and sympathetic. references to, his serious injury. Colonel Cresfield added that he was getting on as well as could be expected, although progress was slow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>VILLAGE WAR WORKERS.</head>
        <p>VILLAGE WAR WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Still a Sportsman.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Patrick, the secretary of the War- rington and District -Works Bowling ,Associa- tion, has received-,a postcard fry= Colonel Crosfield, their president, stating that he hopes to witness some of their games before the season is over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>AN OUTRAGEOUS SUGESTION.</head>
        <p>AN OUTRAGEOUS SUGESTION.</p>
        <p>Comfortable Jobs For Conscien- tious Objectors. RURAL COUNCIL'S BAN.</p>
        <p>"An outrageous suggestion!" Such was the indignant comment of Mr. Furness, the • chairman of the District Council on Wednesday; on hearing the Clerk read a letter in which it was stated that conscientious objectors might be found berths on their staff.</p>
        <p>The letter was from the Rural District Coun- cils Association, and stated that at the sugges- tion of the Government the committee which had been appointed to consider what services of national importance conscientious objectors coulfl undertake had decided to ask the Rural Councils for replies to the following questions, in order that they might be able to give advice of value to the Tribunals:—</p>
        <p>(1.) Would your Council be willing to engage those having a conscientious objection to military service, providing that they were suitable? (2.) What number* vacancies are there in the staff of each department of the Council, which you desire to fill?</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. J. C. Sutton) thought that neither of the questions applied to that Council, and Mr. J. W. Knowles satirically suggested that as there was some difficulty in collecting nightsoil, conscientious objectors might be given a task in that direction. That was work of national importance. The Clerk: They are only speaking of staffs, not workmen.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: Mr. Knowles' suggestion is a good one. The ditches round Longford will want attending to this year, and conscientious objectors might do the work. The Clerk: The object of this letter is to allow officials to go into the Army, and have their places taken by conscientious objectors. The letter was allowed to lie on the table, the Chairman remarking that he thought the letter was in very bad taste. It was practically ask- ing that conscientious objectors should be found comfortable places, and, to his mind, it wag an outrage on loyal subjects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS. What Stretton And Appleton SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE REGT.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS. What Stretton And Appleton SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE REGT.</p>
        <p>'Warrington Company.</p>
        <p>Headquartere, Drill Hall. ORDERS FOR WEEK ENDING MAY 13th, by W. PETER RYLANDS. Officer Commanding.</p>
        <p>Parades.</p>
        <p>To-day, 3 p.m.—Bank Park. Uniform. Cere- monial and Company Drill. Monday and Thursday, 8 p.m.—Bank Park. Extended Order Drill.</p>
        <p>Saturday next, May 13th, 2.50 p.m.—Victoria- square, Stockton Heath. Field Training and Outpost by inter-Volunteer Composite Company and 4th South Lancashire Cadets at Appleton Park; disperse, 5 p.m. - _ _ _ _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>_ Route March.—Sunday, May 21st, to Dutton Hall; details published in Orders next week. Musketry.—Tuesday, Legh-street Range, frcm 7 pan‘ Fire Brigade Drill.—Thursday, 3 and 7 p.m. Orderly Sergeant for the week :—Section Com- mander C. H. Hoult.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Have Done For The Red -Cross.</head>
        <p>Have Done For The Red -Cross.</p>
        <p>The sum of £74 15s. 2d. was raised by a sale of work in aid of the funds for the working party of the Stre4on and Appleton Branch of the British Red Cross Society, held on Monday in the grounds of Mr. and Mrs. H. Beresford Melville, Broome Cottage, Appleton. Mr. Melville presided, Mrs. Henry Greenall opened the sale, and a large attendance in- cluded the Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross, vicar of Stretton; the Rev. J. Collins, vicar of St. Thomas', Stockton Heath; and the Rev. H. G. Thompson, vicar of Appleton. The stallholders were: Appleton Stall, Mrs. Melville and Mrs. Segar Owen; Stretton Stall, Mrs. Bower and friends; Farmers' Stall, Mrs. Ralph Tickle, Mrs. Brocklehurst, and friends. Tea was provided in the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WORK OF THE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Miss S. •Broadbent, called upon to speak of the work of the Red Cross Society in general, and of Stretton and Appleton Branch in par- ticular, explained the gradual growth in strength and efficiency of the Voluntary Aid Detachments since 1908. When the war came, she said, many joined the detachments which provided amateur nurses and helped to train hospital nurses to work under regular hospital nurses and with them—but in no case instead of them. The hospitals all over the country had appreciated the work which had .been done, and particularly all those in this neighbourhood were being staffed, or partly so, by the members of the V.A.D., which also supplied the cooks and domestic workers. This work was equally valu- able in its way, though perhaps, not quite So interesting. In Cheshire there were 62 Red Cross hospitals with 2,206 beds. Another Work of the society was the enrolment of subscribers —those who wished to help whO had no time to give to training as nurses. After a small por- tion of the money had been used for working expenses at home, the rest was spent in provid- ing necessaries for the wounded; everything the medical corps could not cope with. It supplied everything needed from ambulance units for all our Armies, as well as for the Russian, French and Italian Armies.</p>
        <p>The expenditure of the Red Cross Society was £25,000 a week, and only 10d. in the was spent on administration. They were hoping to send a motor ambulance to the Front very shortly as a gift from the Daresbury Division of Cheshire, with which Warrington was amalgamated, and to supply the necessary £4OO by "Empire Day," May 24. It would be a great satisfaction to them all if they were able to do so. Already a sum of .2160 had been raised, and she had just received another cheque in aid of the scheme. One object of the society was to find women to take the place of men in the military hospitals, so that the latter could join the Forces. That district was fortunate in having Mrs. Bower as its leader. (Applause.) On the out- break of the war the Appleton branch made 50 garments, including shirts, ' dressing-gowns, scarves and body-belts, helmets, socks, mittens, fracture-pillow l; with cases. Funds came to an end. , During part 'of 1915 very little work was done, but in November, 1915, they became a registered working party, raised funds by a con- cert, and began to work again, sending a con- signment of 109 garments to the British Red Cross Society headquarters in February, 1916, another of 367 garments to the same in April. and have almost made a sufficient' number to</p>
        <p>tsend up again shortly. Bekides this they have ent 72 garments this month to Raddon Court Hospital, including 10 pairs of sheets, 22 pillow cases, &amp;c., and are also doing some of the sewing for the same hospital. They have been entirely self-supporting, except for the donation of £l5 from the Matinee Fund. The average number of workers is five each week, or a total of 16 or 18. Mrs. Greenall, in opening the sale, said Red Cross Society work was an outlet for women's energy, and she was pleased to find that when the war broke oui they all "came up to the scratch" as one. She could not possibly imagine what our soldiers would have done without them. She earnestly hoped that the sale would be a huge success, and that the work- ing party would raise all the money it required . to enable it to carry on its good work with still greater efficiency and success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PROMOTIONS.</head>
        <p>PROMOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The following promotions are notified this week in the "London Gazette"</p>
        <p>South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant C. Collingwood to be temporary captain. Second-Lieutenants J. R. Charnley, L. Hen- shall, E. 0. Crosfield and A. J. Monks to be temporary lieutenants. Cheshire Regiment.</p>
        <p>T. M. Broadbent, late Captain Militia, to be Temporary 'Captain, April 16th, 1916. Captain Broadbent served in the South African War and subsequently rctired on account of ill-health. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Group B • Reserves.</head>
        <p>Group B • Reserves.</p>
        <p>No. 1 Section.—All as detailed above, except Fire Brigade Drill. Recruits' Class.—Squad Drill, Monday and Thursday, S p.m. Recruits enrolled any drill night.</p>
        <p>Memoranda. I.—All Saturday and Sunday Parades will be in uniform, unless otherwise stated. 2.—Captain W. Pearson, 4th South Lancashire Cadets, will give a series of six short lectures to officers and men in this district on Monday evenings, commencing May 15th, Drill Hall, 7.15 p.m. Lecture No. 1, Discipline, Company Drill, Words of Command. Any men interestethin- vited to attend. 6th May, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>NOVEL WAR FUND'.</head>
        <p>NOVEL WAR FUND'.</p>
        <p>May Birthdays And Liverpool "Roll Of Honour" Week.. A great effort is being made in Liverpool next week to show practical sympathy for the widows. and orphans of soldiers and sailors belonging to Liverpool regiments who have lost their lives in the war. •</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor and organisers of the "Roll of Honour" week have adopted what is thought to be an excellent suggestion sent to them by a Warringtonian known as the "May Birthday Book," and all those whose birthday occurs in May or who have relatives or friends in the Navy or Army whose birthdays omit in that month should be specially asked to contribute a.; sum of not less than one shilling each towards the fund. The Lord Mayor has decided to open a "May Birthday Book," in which shall be entered the date, names and amounts of contri- butions, which will be a very interesting souvenir of the great effort made in Liverpool. Contributions are, therefore, invited, and should be sent to the "May Birthday Roll of Honour " Week Fund; Town Hall, Liverpool, with full iparticulars, and if a message or motto could be added, it will be an additional attraction. I On the first day the "May Birthday Book " was opened (2nd inst.), the Lord Mayor of Liver- pool received a letter from a gentleman in the city whose birthday falls on the 11th of May, ibut as it was not his first birthday he enclosed 178 shillings to make the proper acknowledgment far years past and gone. The Lord. Mayor• ' " 1 (Alderman Mather) decided to follow this ex- cellent example, and has entered his name in the "Birthday Book," and as he will be 74 on the 13th inst., will subscribe to the partieular section of the fund Is. for each Tear—in all 74 shillings. The Lol4l Mayor hopes that a large number whose birthdays occur 'in May, or who have relatives at the Front who celebate their natal day in this month, will follow this example and so help the sorrowing relatives of those brave men who have lost their lives in the great war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRES.</head>
        <p>THE SOUTH LANCASHIRES.</p>
        <p>Captain Sydney T. Boast And A Gift From Warrington. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk (Mr. J. L. Whittle), has received the following letter from Captain Sydney T. Boast, dated April .26th : "In reference to your letter of the 4th April I beg to inform you that the consignment of cigarettes arrived a few days ago and was duly distributed amongst the men of the Battalion, who all very much appreciate the gift. Will you please convey their sincere thanks to the subscribers to your fund? We have recently moved into our new area, and the Battalion has been in the trenches for several days. The first two days were very wet and the trenches were very uncomfortable, but the spirits of the men were as usual—cheerful. The sector we now occupy is quite new to us, and although there is considerable activity we have not suffered much up to now. I have not any news for you this time, but will write yore again later.--Yours very 'sincerely, SYDNEY Boar."</p>
      </div>
