      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>BUILDING AFTER THE VAR.</head>
        <p>BUILDING AFTER THE VAR.</p>
        <p>Surveyor Foreshadows Develop-</p>
        <p>ment Round Warrington.</p>
        <p>In any development tilat may ,follow the war the Warrington Rural District Council is not going to be backward, for Mr. B. S. Newcombe, the surveyor, at their meeting on Wednesday afternoon, gave it as his opinion that directly upon the conclusion of war it was likely that there would be much movemext in building works within the district. He thought„ there- fore, that it would be advisable to proceed with draft town-planning schemes in anticipation. The process of preparing was so slow and time. wasting, that it would be- seen how necessary some- provision for hastening should be made and draft ideas worked out. The actual formal steps should not be taken, of course, but every preparation should be made as far as time and other duties allowed,</p>
        <p>To Mr. Newcombe's suggestion the Council gave approval. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>TOWN PLANNING CONFERENCE NOT</head>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING CONFERENCE NOT</p>
        <p>ATTENDED.</p>
        <p>;It may here be stated that a month ago the Chairman (Mr. W. Furness) was appointed to represent the Council upon the National Hous- ing and Town Planning Council, which met in London. The Clerk reported on Wednesday, however, that the Local Government Board had -written that there was not sufficient ground for allowing expenses to be charged to toe rates, and, therefore,i the Clerk (Mr. Thos. Sutton) advised Mr. Furness that he had better not go.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>THREE TIMES CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THREE TIMES CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Furness Again To Preside Over Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>For the third year in succession Mr. W. Furness and Mr. P. Longton have been appointed chairman and vice-chairman of the Warrington Rural District Council. This was agreed to at a meeting on Wednes- day, on the proposition of Mr. George Mather, seconded by Mr. J. W. Knowles, and carried enthusiastically. _ - _</p>
        <p>In taking the chair, Mr. W. Furness thanked the Council for the very great honour they had done him in selecting him again. He added that he had not expected to have the pleasure of presiding over them. for another year, be- cause he felt he had done sufficient during the last two years to warrant retirement from the position. However, these were exceptional times and, perhaps, it was as well that they should have some conttinuity of management while the war lasted. (Hear, hear.) After Mr. Longton had also acknowledged the Council's award, Mr. Furness handed cigars to the members and officials, and, in the pleasantest of ways and amid a delightful atixosphere, the business of the Council pro= ceeded.</p>
        <p>Truly Polite. The politest man in Boston, according to "Success," collided violently with another man on the street. The second man was angry. "My dear sir," said the polite one with a bow, "1 don't know which of us is to blame for this encounter. If I ran into you. I beg your par- don; if you ran into me, don't mention it."</p>
        <p>a s==i</p>
        <p>CH 11 ill _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>– STOMACH &amp; LIVER TROUBLES</head>
        <p>- STOMACH &amp; LIVER TROUBLES</p>
        <p>13 INDIGESTION CONSTIPATION BILIOUSNESS HEADACHES</p>
        <p>Restore your dige&lt;ve a organs to working order. by using Mother Seigel's Syrup. and these troubles will be completely banished. Test it to-day. "</p>
        <p>- YIELD TO - ~ MOTHER SEIGEL'S _.</p>
        <p>SYRUP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>I ~ 0 DISTRICT ti -, Ews I. . _ ~_., ..4 ___</head>
        <p>I ~ 0 DISTRICT ti -, Ews I. . _ ~_., ..4 ___</p>
        <p>LIYMNI</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND. FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>The news of last week's Council scene fell like a bombshell, and created quite a sensation in the village.</p>
        <p>The new Chairman is not likely to forget it. This was his first experience as chairman, and one wonders how many similar sensations he will have to encounter. ' ,</p>
        <p>He remarked when taking the chair that it was a thankless job. lam inclined to agree with kim.</p>
        <p>Perhaps Councillor J. H. Holt's hint to economise will have some effect on the harmony of the future meetings.</p>
        <p>Councillor J. H. Holt was in the running for the chair. Better luck next time!</p>
        <p>The retiring Chairman seemed indignant, and secretly confessed to me that never had a simi- lar scene occurred dUring his year of office.</p>
        <p>Some people think that Councillor Cowper takes a very narrow view: Perhaps he does at the Council meetings, but apart from that he is a "decent sort." I know him.</p>
        <p>And I can honestly- say that in our new Chairman we have a man who is safe end reliable, and a ma who will work in the in- terests of those he represents.</p>
        <p>My advice to them all is to shake hands and put personal feelings on one side. Then we are sure to get good results.</p>
        <p>Councillor W. G. Groves, who has represented Booth's Hill Ward for over 22 years, has been overseer of the poor for a matter of about 19 years. Surely a record of which to be proud.</p>
        <p>He speaks in glowing terms of the services rendered by Mr. lefullard, who occupies the office .of assistant overseer.</p>
        <p>Allow me to express my sympathy with the relatives of Sergeant T. W. Field, our local D.C.M., who was so suddenly taken ill on Satur- day. May he have a speedy recovery!</p>
        <p>Lymm people are not without the sense of humour, for large crowds—including the wounded soldiers from Brookfields—visited a circus which pitched its tent in the village on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>The Warrington theatres still retain their old popularity with Lymm patrons. They are always sure of a good show.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most popular this week is Mr. Warburton's house, where "Mary From Tip- perary" is staged with great effect. Lymm people are always keen on anything sensational.</p>
        <p>Nevertheless, I saw quite' a lot of Lynam faces at the Hippodrome on Monday night. The usual smile !</p>
        <p>I see Corporal W. Molesworth is home on furlough. I sincerely hope that he feels as well as he looks.</p>
        <p>Quite a change from life on the Gallipoli Peninsula—wounded. twice, I believe, but made a grand, recovery.</p>
        <p>All's well that ends well !</p>
        <p>It was suggested at the vestry meeting that a prize should be offered for the sidesman who attends best to his duties during this year:</p>
        <p>These officials have not been attending church very regularly, and last Sunday only one in eight was present.</p>
        <p>Someone suggested that there should be women labour in this as in other departments of life;, but it does not appear that in this case the sides- men have gone on active service.</p>
        <p>The Rector wants a permanent curate, but recognises that the circumstances of the times make such an appointment impracticable.</p>
        <p>As in other places, ithe Easter offerings at Lymm remained at a satisfactory figure, in spite of the calla of the war.</p>
        <p>Can a vestry meeting be held elsewhere than In a vestry and yet retain its title? At the Parish Church on Wednesday the meeting took place in a transept owing to the vestry light not being properly screened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Aged Farmer Who Contemplates • Retirement.</p>
        <p>At the Lymm .3filitary Tribunal a: chimney sweep, who applied for total exemption, stated that he left Lymm to work his brother's busi- ness at Cheadle, so as to allow him to join the colours, Applicant was a married man. The Tribunal decided to refer the case to Cheadle, the district in which the business was situated. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Cannot Get Good Women.</head>
        <p>Cannot Get Good Women.</p>
        <p>A farmer applied for the total exemption of his teamsnxan and ploughman, stating that he worked a farm comprised of 200 acres of land, 150 acres of which were arable; 15 horses, and 40 head of cattle. He further stated that as a rule he kept three teamsmen, but at the pre- sent ,he had only two.</p>
        <p>A Member: Row long has he been in your employ?--Since October last.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: How is it you engaged him, knowing that he was of military age, and liable to be called to the colours?—l advertised for a suitable man for six months, but got no appli- cations.</p>
        <p>The Military Representative: What about women labour? Have you ever tried it?—l have never been able to get a good woman. They cannot drive horse§ or plough. The Tribunal decided to grant conditional exemption until October Ist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Farmer's Son's Conditional Exemption.</head>
        <p>Farmer's Son's Conditional Exemption.</p>
        <p>A grey-haired farmer successfully appliedi for exemption of his son until after the autumtr season, stating that he was 71 years of age, and if only they would let him keep things to- i gether he bad decided to retire at the end of the season. His sop, he stated, acted as dairy- man and cowman, and - worked an extensive milk round. A woman would not suit him, as i it took his son all morning to get through 'with the milk round. Another son, who had been before the Appeal Tribunal at Stockport, had to go.</p>
        <p>Tile Tribunal decided to 'grant conditional exemption until the Ist of September. Toolmaker's Unsuccessful Application.</p>
        <p>A toolmaker asked for leave to appeal before the Lymm Tribunal, stating that his case had been heard before the wrong Court. A solicitor, who represented the man, stated that his client at the time of attestation, was a sub-contractor engaged on Government work. making plier-cutters, but since then their plant had been moved and negotiations were pending which would eventually make them full contractors. He further stated that, it was better for the country that his client should be engaged at home on work of national im- portance. They had this week been engaged in making samples for the War Office, and the War Office were satisfied.</p>
        <p>Applicant was a single man and. arried on business with two brothers.</p>
        <p>A Member (addressing applicant): Were you attested?—No.</p>
        <p>The Solicitor At the time of attestation he was a enh,contractor and therefore a hedged man.</p>
        <p>Applicatioa refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>LOCAL D.C.M.</head>
        <p>LOCAL D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Seriously Ill: Relapse In Manchester.</p>
        <p>Sergt. T. W. Field, of the Lancashire Fusiliers (our local D.C.M.), is lying seriously ill in Whit- worth-street Military Hospital, Manchester, 'suffering from paralysis in the legs. This is the report that reached the gallant sergeant's wife, who resides at Newfields, on Saturday evening. Talking to our representative, Mrs. Field in- formed him that her 'husband was engaged in clerical work at Heaton Park, anti had just fin- ished paying the men 'their wages about 3 p.m. on Saturday, when suddenly he became ill and had to be conveyed to Whitworth,street Mili- tary Hospital, where it was found that he was suffering from paralysis in the legs. When Mrs. Field visited him on Saturday evening she found him in a grave condition, suffering great pain, and it was feared that he'wOuld lose the use of his legs, but better news was received later in the week, although his condition is still serious. Sergt. Field, it will be remembered, was out on the Gallipoli Peninsula, when he was awarded the coveted honour. The story of 'his great bravery can be told in a few words. On August 7th, 1915, the Turks had recaptured a portion of a trench, and Sergt. Field, with three comrades, gallantly held on to the centre of the</p>
        <p>used his influence as a member of Parliament to get out of doing his (linty as a soldier. Well, all he would 'say was that in addition to being a soldier he was an Irishman, and when the medical authorities gave him the change people would not find him lacking in will tsp. go out and share with his fellow-Britishers in doing his bit against the ,Huns., (Applause) Major Hamilton went on to refer to the con-- troversy in the Manchester Chamber of Com- merce as to Free Trade, and said that from a Tariff Reformer's point of view, the change was one of the most encouraging that had occurred for some time in England, because the Man- chester Chamber of Commerce had been a 'stronghold of Free Trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>SEIZGT. T. W. FIELD, D.C.M.</head>
        <p>SEIZGT. T. W. FIELD, D.C.M.</p>
        <p>position until a counter attack had been organised, which drove the enemy off. Two of his comrades were killed, and the other wounded, and he himself was wounded in four places. The gallant Sergeant has not yet been presented with the medal, and it is hoped that our municipal authorities will try to make arrangements for this medal to be presented in Lymm, so giving Lymm people a chance to recognise the honour that the gallant Sergeant has bestowed upon the little village.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES JENNINGS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES JENNINGS.</p>
        <p>Tells Of Lymm Lads Who Meet In The</p>
        <p>East.</p>
        <p>Writing ti.) his mother at Moston-street, New- fields, Private James Jennings, of the Man- chester Regiment, states:— "Having got over my shock, I will try and write a few lines. I was sorry to hear about my poor brother, Sam, getting killed, and it upset me very much. The news came as a great shock to me, as no doubt it did to you. I did not receive your letters until after I had received the parcel, and when I opened them and read the news I could have dropped, but thought something was the matter, as I had not received a letter from either you or Lizzie. But shortly afterwards I received four together, also a. parcel, for which I must- thank you•Yery, ranch. I hope you are not upsetting yourself: I know that it is very hard for all of us, but we must try to bear it bravely, for it is all for the best. I must thank you once again for the parcel. It came exactly at the right moment and contained the right stuff. Our 'cig.' supply seemed to have '-dropped oft. I do not think -there was a single cig.' in the tent, so you can imagine the joy when we saw them, and I can assure you that shortly after- wards our tent was more like a smokeroom than anything else. After putting a share away for my pal, Will Taylor (another New. fields soldier), we finished -off with a bit of chocolate, and you should just have seen how we smacked our lips. I think that there were about four parcels in the tent that day, so you can imagine what a 'spread' we had, everything being divided amongst the lot. I was very glad of the soap, I can tell you, for we shell now be able to- enjoy a wash without rubbing ourselves raw. The soap they 'dish' us out with is more like a brick thary anything else. You will be glad to hear that William Taylor and I are ,still together. I see him every day after parade. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Grand "Do."</head>
        <p>A Grand "Do."</p>
        <p>"We had: a grand 'do' about a week ago. It was his 13irthday, so we went down town and kept it up--chicken and eggs for tea! We don't see that every day. We are still in the same place, but there are rumours that we shall be moving any day. It is a very nice place where we are, and are able to go bath- ing. Who do you think we came across the other day? Joe Daniels! It's a treat to meet anyone from Lynam out here. He has been out-on the Peninsula,. and I suppose you, know he was wounded and lucky enough' to get home. But he was not quite luoky enough to stay there. I am expecting a parcel from the land- lady where we were billeted at in Southport, and when you write you can tell her that I am getting a wash pretty often, but net quite five a day. We have ',to be satisfied with about five a month. Remember me to' Mrs. Taylor and tell her that Willie and I are all right now. It is like old times being together again." Private James Jennings is a well-known local lad, and prior to the war was employed at Irlam. The brother Sam referred to is the one that was killed a few weeks ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>MAJOR G. C. HANIILTON, M.P.</head>
        <p>MAJOR G. C. HANIILTON, M.P.</p>
        <p>Why_ The Member For Altrincham</p>
        <p>Division Is Not On Service.</p>
        <p>The annual.meetingof. the Altrincham Divi- sion Conservative Association was heli on'Satur- day, and was addressed by Major G. C. Hamilton, M.P., Mr. H. V. Kilvert, chairman of the Association, presided. • The Chairman referred to personal attacks which, he said, -hed been made upon Major Hamilton. Soon after the outbreak of war, and when the age limit had been raised, Major Hamilton joined the Cheshire Regiment as a lieutenant. Later he accepted a commission in the Queen's Westminster Rifles because it was then confidently expected that that regiment would be sent to the Front. sooner than the Cheshire Regiment. It was a great disappoint- ment to Major Hamilton at the time that the likelihood of being able to take active service in. the field had been somewhat curtailed. There- fore he applied for and was offered a commission as captain in the Grenadier Guards, which he accepted, but, unfortunately', when he came be- fore the medical board he was rejected for active service owing to a disability of which he had been unaware. It was for them to sympathise with Major Hamilton in the position in which he found himself.</p>
        <p>Major Hamilton, M.P., who had a very cordial reception, said that during the last few months he had received some very insulting anonymous letters from various people in the ivision—("Shame "1.---71esi nu eti ng that. bp- caufie he was.a member of 'Parliament he had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>WORD ABOUT THE CAMERON HIGHLANDERS.</head>
        <p>A WORD ABOUT THE CAMERON HIGHLANDERS.</p>
        <p>By PRIVATE ERNEST SHAW.</p>
        <p>"Am I Scotch? ,This is the question I have been asked thousands of times since I returbed to England. Well, t will tell you! lam nqt, I am a native of Lymm, and was bred and born in Newfields, but how is it I joined the Cameron Highlanders in preference to one of ,my county regiments? Well, that I can hardly explain. Perhaps it was the fascination; of the kilt, or the strains of the pipes and drums, or peihaps it was this particular regiment's exploits in history, but anyhow, when the call to arms came I decided that .I should be a Scottish soldier.</p>
        <p>"I am a plier-maker by trade, and a lot of my fellow workmen, who were in the local Territorials, had left Lymm to prepare to do their bit, and naturally I felt a little bit down- hearted, so I and a workmate decided that we would stick it' no longer. Off we went to the recruiting station and joined the Cameron Highlanders. Shortly after arriving at Inver- ness, the battalion's headquarters, I had the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PRIVATE ERNEST SHAW.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST SHAW.</p>
        <p>misfortune to lose my pal, who was discharged as medically unfit.' But this did not damp my spirits, for I soon made other friends, and was surprised to find that several of them came from Warrington and the surrounding dis- tricts, one of whom, I am sorry to say, I saw reported killed in last week's Examiner.' My stay at Inverness did not last long, for 1 had scarcely been there two months when a call was made for volunteers to make a draft to reinforce a battalion that had suffered heavy losses in the early part of the fighting in France, so I volunteered and was eventually seleeted as one of the draft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>My First Christmas At The Front.</head>
        <p>My First Christmas At The Front.</p>
        <p>"It enabled.me to spend the first Christmas ,of the war in France. The following are a few extracts of my diary, and take me over the period of my first month on active service. "December 3rd, 1914.—Landed in France. The King passed through and inspected the Ist Brigade. _ _</p>
        <p>"4th.—Another draft arrived and consisted of 130 officers, N.C.O.'s and men. "4th to 15th.—Resting at a base camp; noth- ing doing. "19th.—Several new officers joined for duty; weather very bad; up to the knees in mud. "20th.—At 5 p.m. moved off under sudden orders and marched to (16 miles).</p>
        <p>"21st.—Arrived at about 3 a.m. after a very trying' march. Reveille 6.30 a.m. 'Marched off at noon, leaving about 40 sick at , went via and —' and attacked the village of —, along with the Scots and Coldstream Guards. Several officers wounded and a captain Missing. "22nd.—At daybreak 'A! and 'B' Companies advanced against German trenches with Cold- stream Guards; relieved at night by Berkshire Regiment. Went in billets south of canal bridge; several casualties. "23rd.—Still in ,billets.</p>
        <p>"24th.—Draft of 164 joined from —, "25th (Christmas Day).—Distribution of Christmao cards from T.M. the King and Queen. "26th.—Freezing hard. , "27th.—Rain and mud.</p>
        <p>"28th.—More rain and still more mud; re- lieved by Black Watch. Whale Battalion marched to (six miles) in a terrific gale of wind and went into billets in a tobacco factory:</p>
        <p>"2h.—Princess Mary's gifts distributed. " 30th.—Battalion enjoyed hot baths. "31st.—Lochiel paid the Battalion a visit. Battalion moved with rest of the Brigade) at 5.15 p.m., and went into billets at for the night (two miles); slept the New Year in. "And that is how I spent My fast Christaxtas of the Great War."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>SAPPER PERCY CLARE.</head>
        <p>SAPPER PERCY CLARE.</p>
        <p>Getting Better And Thinking Of Dear Old Lymm.</p>
        <p>Sapper Percy Clare, the Royal Engineers, who is at present an inmate of a base hospital some- where in France, is on the road to recovery, and ‘hopes to be fit for duty soon. Writing to his mother at Booths Hill, he states: "I am well on the road to recovery, and I sincerely hope that you are all keeping well at home. I shall soon be right for duty again. I have had a good rest, which I can assure you I have enjoyed. As I write this letter I am sitting under a tree by the riverside, and the weather is simply lovely. Just now it is very warm indeed, almost too warm to walk about. As I sit here I can just imagine myself strolling through the fields in dear old Lymm. It is grand to sit in the fields here and hear the rumble of the big guns; quite a change from being among them. Keep on smiling, and remember that there are better days in store." At the commencement of hostilities Sapper P. Clare was in the Cheshire Itegiment, but goon after ',arriving in Francs he volunteered for mining work with the Royal Engineers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>IRREGULAR WORSHIPPERS</head>
        <p>IRREGULAR WORSHIPPERS</p>
        <p>Sidesmen At St. Mary's Set Bad Example To Parishioners.</p>
        <p>SHOULD LADIES BE INTRODUCED? That the sidesmen at Lymm Church do not attend services as well as might be exPteted was the view expressed at the vestry, meeting held on Wednesday night, the Rettorf (the Rev. G. B. Thurston) presiding. When the appointment of sidesmen Was mooted the Rector asked his warden (Mr. F. E Evans) how many he would like, as, at one time, they had a dozen and now they had eight. .Mr. Evans: A dozen did not work very well, and eight do not work any better, but I should leave it at eight and give a prize for the best attendance. .(Laughter.) One present: Why not introduce ladies? —(More laughter.) The Rector observed that the attendance of the sidesmen was not a bit good Last Sunday there were only two people to collect in the evening, there being only one sideman present out of eight. That was not very good, to say the least. He knew very good excuses could be ,given, but he hoped this year the sidestnen would remember tlq laiT;e *things at stake, and,</p>
        <p>as church officers, standing in the forefront, set an example to all parishioners. The following sidesmen were then appointed: Messrs. Milnes, Oliver, Druce, Pearson, Dixon, Ifeathcote, Basil Hartley and Captain Hartley. Those appointed last year were: Messrs. Dixon, Farrar, Heathcote, Pearson, Lecomber, Milnes, Oliver and Captain Hartley. Patriotic Choristers.</p>
        <p>The Rector said he was gratified by his appeal that the places of the claoristers who had enlisted should be taken by old choristers, and instead of having the gentlemen in the choir reduced to three or four they now had seven or eight. That, in his opinion, was very practical patriotism. Insured Against Aircraft.</p>
        <p>It was. announced that not only the church but the chureh buildings had been insured against damage from aircraft .at a cost of just over £2O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Possibility of a Curate.</head>
        <p>Possibility of a Curate.</p>
        <p>The Church Council, added the Rector, had considered the possible provision of a per- manent curate, who was very much needed, but he (the Rector)' felt that as therd was a shortage of clergy, owing to many of them serving as chaplains, this was not the time to make any effort to secure one, though the parish would benefit much by having one. In regard to the forthcoming National Mission, however, if one found a gentleman of experience who was tem- porarily disengaged, he (the speaker) would pro- pose that he might be appointed, at any rate, for nine months or so. The amount of work which had to be done had been increased by the calls during the war, and it was almostraore than one could manage. • Good Balance In Hand.</p>
        <p>In submitting the accounts, Mr. F. E. Evans, the Rector's warden, mentioned that whereas last year they had a balance in hand of £57, they now had £46. Special offertories amounted to £l9B 15s. 6d., and the ordinary to £154 4s. 2d. These offertories had not been as large as usual, but this was accounted for by the fact that there had been so many calls upon people's purses. The Easter offering to the Rector was, happily, about the same as usual, this being £77 3s. 2d., and he had great pleasure in handing him a cheque for that amount, remarking that "It must be very gratifying to the Rector to know that it keeps up year after year." Value of the " Living."</p>
        <p>In returning thanks, the Rector spoke of the kindness of the parishioners towards him, and added that he had never made a secret of the value of the living. For eighteen years it ha 3 averaged a little over £l9O a year, and it was, therefore, very necessary that the parishioners should make a substantial Easter offering. That they had done, and it was very gratifying, especially owing to the great calls which had been made upon them. There was no doubt that should it he necessary to appoint another Rector at any time the feet that the Easter offerings had kept up to such an amount would be a considerable factor in obtaining a gentle- man whom . they would like.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>Nearness To God Needed For Victory.</head>
        <p>Nearness To God Needed For Victory.</p>
        <p>With regard to the forthcoming National Mission, the Rector said it simply meant that if the country were to hold its own it would have to get back to the position from which it had receded &gt; very. far in recent years—he posi- tion of nearness and of dependence upon God. rt had been burned into their hearts and minds that what' was wrong with the world at the present time was that the majesty and power of the Divine Ruler had been forgotten, and that men had taken things into their own hands. With what result , they now had terrible evidence. What would be aimed at by the National Mission would be that every single member of ,the Church should witness in their lives the reality of their belief. Things could not go on as they were: that was a certainty. The enemy they wanted to be preserved from, was not so much the Germans as to save them- selves from themselves. Officers And Votes of Thanks.</p>
        <p>In appointing Mr. F. E. Evans as his warden, the Rector spoke of his unfailing capability and earnestness in the work, and eulogistio words were ,also spoken of Mr. W. M. Beckett, who Was reappointed people's warden. The retiring members of, the Church Council were re-elected as follows :—Messrs. John Coxon, C. James, Milnes, and Captain Hartley. Those present at the vestry meeting were Messrs. F. E. Evans, W. M. Beckett, J. G. Farrar, F. Druce, H. Pearson, W. Cooke, R. Heathcote,.T. F. W. Dixon, and Basil Hartley.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded to the ladies for decorating the church at festivals, the Rev. Horace 'Gray for his services. in helping the Rector, and Mr. W. Cooke (organist) and choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lymni Platoon.</p>
        <p>• Orders for week commencing Saturday, May 6th, 1916.</p>
        <p>_ Saturday, May 6th.—Battalion Drill and Mano3uvres at Withenshawe Park. Train leaves Lymm Station, 2.40 p.m,</p>
        <p>Tuesday.-7 p.m., Shooting. 8 p.m., Platoon Drill.</p>
        <p>Thursday.-7 p.m.. Shooting. 8 p.m., Platoon Drill and Bayonet Exercise. BY order, G. R. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Women's Adult Adult School.—A special whist drive on behalf of comfocts for wounded soldiers in the Whitecross and Thelwall Hospitals was arranged by the members of the above school last week. It was successful in realising £1 158. (20s. for Whitecross and 15s. for Thelwall). The refreshments and prizes ,were provided by the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>E Wedding.—A very pretty wedding took place at Winwick church on Easter Monday. The 'contracting ifarties-were Mr. William Sweetlove, son of the late Mr. William and Mrs. Sweetlove, lof ,Winwick Post Office, and Miss Margaret, Monks, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. G. Monks, Over Back House, Croft. The Rev. Canon Penrhyn officiated, assisted by the Rev. J. T. Owen. The bide was given away by her I father. The biidesmaids were the Misses A. and A. M. Monks, sisters of the bride, and Miss E. Sanderson, cousin; of the bride. Mr. J. Monks, brother of the bride, was best man. gold Prayer b r thea pi: 17; tfk, t as a. n d the The bride was attired in a grey dress and pale blue hat with osprey. She carried an ivory. backed Prayer Book, and worefausgeofludl bracelet, the the gift of the bridegroom. Her travelling dress consisted of a nigger brown coetnme with hat 1 were Youngerbridegroom. to match. The bridesmaids wore cream dresses The eldestand bridesmaid woreaek ead two received, gAacrfsguiegdninneugt number 1 lesrgesto, of carve r s from the bellringers, of which the bridegroom was a member. As the happy couple left the church ,the bells were rung. The reception was held at the home of the bride, OVOT Back House, where about 50 guests assembled, The honeymoon was 3 spent in NOrth Wares..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>MR. S. W. DALLOW.</head>
        <p>MR. S. W. DALLOW.</p>
        <p>Death And Funeral After A Painful Illness.</p>
        <p>We announce with deep regret the death of Mr. S. W. Dallow, of the Station Hotel, Acton Bridge, and formerly of Stockton Heath. He passed away on Monday after a painful illness extending evcr two months. He was very well known in Stockton Heath, which district he left about nine years ago for Acton Bridge. Here he took a great interest in church work, and had made numerous friends. He was a director of the Warrington Permanent Benefit Building Society. The funeral took place at Weaverham Church on Thursday, the Rev'. Father Jones,- of Run- corn, officiating. There were numerous rela- tives and friends present, and these included Mr. E. Dallow and family, Mr. C. W. Daven- port, Messrs. Grounds apd Isherwood (brothers-in-law), Mr. Joseph Nicholas (son-in- law), Mr. A. J. Willett (solicitor, Warrington), and Mr ig Holland; Messrs. J. Wilkinson (sacra- tuy), F. Timperley, T. Partridge, T. Critchley, I-Mbert and Ince (representing the Warring- ton Permanent Benefit Building Society). Many beautiful floral tributes were received from residents and old friends at Stockton Heath and Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachincnt.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing Bth May, 1916, issued by Mr. Frank Warburton, O.C. Monday.—Drill, Headquarters, Guard Mount- ing, 7.45 p.m. _</p>
        <p>Tuesday,—Musketry Practice at Appleton Rifle Range, 5.30 p.m. Saturday.—lnter-manceuvreS, Appleton Park. Fall in Victoria-square, 3 p.m. In view of duties which the Detachment have been requested to take up in case of emergency. the O.C. will be glad to receive application for membership—especially from men over military age. Men of military age are invited to enrol that they may qualify for non-commissioned rank when called up, as outlined in Army Form No. 3256.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Frost Says The Germans</p>
        <p>Mean to Come.</p>
        <p>rrammed with Volunteers in their greenish- grey uniforms, wounded soldiers, and supporters of the Volunteer movement, the Large Room at Grappenhall on Thursday night had the addi- tional charm of being beautifully decorated with flowering plants, flags, and bunting. The main reason of assembly was for the holding of a concert to raise funds for the equipping of the Grappenhall Volunteer Corps, and for the purpose of hearing an important speech by the Commandant, Mr. F. A. Frost. Where to put Germans—if they land.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frost had great gratification in announc- ing that the Government had allotted a,duty to the Grappenhall Platoon individually, in case of an invasion. The Germans meant to land in this country, and the Government thought it most likely that they would do so. If they did land they wOuld not get back, and it would be the duty of the Volunteer Corps to help to put them under ,the sod and protect the women and children. of the country. He was a little dis- appointed with the way in which the men had responded to the call of the Volunteer Training Corps, although theY at Grappenhall had done very well. He had, however, hoped to muster a platoon—GO men—but as yet there were only 40 men in the corps. It was no• use protesting aboUt a man's business, not being able to con- tinue if he were called up, for if the necessity came for men to defend their own land, it would be a case of "down tools and push the Huns out," and not a thought about business. In conclusion, Mr. Frost thanked all who had assisted them that night, the Rev.., H. V. Pigot. for the loan of the room; Messrs. Peter Walker and Son for the beautiful decorations; to the artistes who had given their services that night; and to all who had Solt! tickets. He also thanked Privates Leigh, Smith and Eastment for their help in getting the, room in order, and Messrs. Green.all, Whitley and Co. for the loan of transports. They wanted money, rifles, and equipment, and he hoped the public would con- tinue to take a deep interest in the movement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>, The Concert.</head>
        <p>, The Concert.</p>
        <p>A fine concert was given during the evening, with which all were delighted. Miss Helen Comber opened the programme with a piano- forte solo, and also played another later in the 'evening. Mr. J. B. Poole delighted with "The 13edouin love:song""and " Up from Ztunmerzet," as also did Mr. J. Rabbitt with "The trumpeter" (for which he was encored) and "A song of Britain!' Miss E. M. Norton was recalled, after rendering- "Love's garden of roses," while she particularly delighted the soldiers with her rendering. or "Pill the boys come home." Miss Marie Houghton was heard to great advantage in her rendering of "For you alone" (by special request), "In an old4ashioned town,' "Cuckoo," "Daddy's sweetheart," and "The rosary." Mrs. Robert Garnett recited with fine expression, one of her pieces, "The ladies of St. James'," being very good. Mr. H. M. Ashton supplied the humorous side by means of recita- tions, the old favourite, "Widow Murphy's ad- ventures," being greatly enjoyed. Miss. Helen Comber accompanied for all the soloists except for Miss Marie Houghton, who played herself, and for Mr. J. Rabbitt, for whom his daughter, Miss Lillie Ralibitt, accompanied. The singing of the National Anthem brought a very enjoy. able evening to a close. DETAOHMENT ORDERS. For week ending 13th May, 1916.</p>
        <p>,7, Dparly.,Epxrparcestisc, Competition: , Monday, Bth May.—Musketry, at the range. Tuesday.—Company and squad drill. Fall in at headquarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. Friday.—Musketry, at the range. 7 p.m., ectiasitios . 8 p.m.. n:fiing practice and class firing. 8 p.m., "Daily Ex. press" Competition. Na—The compOtition for thd g Daily Ex.</p>
      </div>
