      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <p>press" Medal closes on this date. Saturday.—Parade (uniform). Orderlies.—Section-Commander C. G. Hutch- ings and Private R. W. Hasted. It is proposed to hold a Battalion camp for 10 days at Whitsuntide. Full particulars can be obtained from, and names should be given to, the Section-Commanders or the C.-Q.-M.-Ser- geant.</p>
        <p>By order,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>F. A. FROST, O.Q.</head>
        <p>F. A. FROST, O.Q.</p>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Burns and Religion.—The monthly devotional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Mon- day evening, presided over by the .Rev. A. 3, Morton. An excellent paper entitled "The Religion of Burns's Poetry" was given by Mr. W. E. Plinston. He pointed out that in spite of its coarseness, Burns's peetry was full of religious teaching. It was a moral and ethical force. Some of the most important lessons he taught were charity and kindness to animals, whilst at the same time he strongly condemned hypocrisy. To illustrate his arguments; Mr. Plinston read selections from Burns's poetical works, which added considerable interest to the subject. The soloist was Miss Mason, who sang Rest thee, sad heart." Miss Cottrell was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>Concert.—A concert organised by the Padgate Wesleyan Temperance Society was held in the Schoolroom on Thursday evening, presided over by the Rev. A. J. Morton. The first part of the programme consisted of a cantata entitled "Britannia's Awakening," which was very ably rendered by members of the Stockton Heath Band of Hope. A feature of the cantata was the excellent singing of Miss Isherwood In old English, Irish, Welsh and Scotch\ songs. The second part was miscellaneous, and a song entitled "The King's Own" was excellently rendered by Master A. Walton. Miss Evelyn Todd, who sang "Grace Darling," and Miss A. Hamer, who gave "Down the Vale," were loudly applauded, and the action songs of the girls and boys were much appreciated. A Lancashire recitation entitled "Bowton's Yard's was given by Miss May Elliott, and Miss Bell created much amusement by her monologue entitled "The Lost Golosh." The concert con- cluded with a humorous sketch entitled "Tea and Scandal," by the; Warrington Temperance Guild Sketch Party. A hearty vote of thanks was passed to the friends for their excellent entertainment. The admission was free, with collection to defray expenses. Presentation to Police-Sergeant Clarke.—A very interesting ceremony took place last week at the County. Police Office, Leigh, when Police- Sergeant Clarke, of Padgate, was presented with a beautiful oak timepiece as a mark of esteem from the officers, sergeants and constables of the Warrington Division, on his retirement from the Lancashire County Constabulary after up- wards of 28 years' service. - • The presentation was made by Mr. 13, Greenough, who spoke very highly of the spirit the Sergeant had always shown in carrying out his onerous and arduous duties, and as he had known him almost from the beginning of his police service he was speaking from practical knowledge. One noteworthy fact was that dur- ing the whole of his service the Sergeant had not a single mark recorded against him, and in wishing him success in whatever sphere of life he intended to enter, they hoped he would live long to enjoy the pension he had so honourably earned.</p>
        <p>Superintenilent Ross also spoke very highly of the merits of the Sergeant, and said how sorry he was to lose such a capable officer. On behalf of' the sergeants and constables, Inspector Bryant (divisional clerk) expressed regret at losing such a good comrade, and wished him every success. A very suitable present of a lady's umbrella was made to Mrs. Clarke, with the good wishes of the Sergeant's late officers and comrades.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>The Lite Mr. T. Phillips's Funeral—The funeral of the late Mr. Thomas Phillips, Orford Green, ballast foreman on the Cheshire Lines Railway, took place at Padgate Church on Saturday afternoon. The service was conducted by the Rev. G. Vale Owen, vicar of Orford. The principal mourners were: Master Percy Phillips (son.), Ada, Amy and Annie Phillips (daughters), Mr. William Phillips (brother), Thomas and Percy Phillips (nephews), Sarah Phillips (niece), Mr. V. Thomas, Mr. and Mrs', F. :Eardley, and Mr. George Walker (C.L.C. engineer). Wreaths were' sent by: His loving children, "William," Miss Carter, Mrs. Wil- son, Mr. Thomas, Mr. and Mrs. Eardley, Mr. and Mrs. Shaw, Mr. Hamblett (Padgate), Mr. and Mrs. Tomlinson, jun., Mrs. Pugh. A beautiful artificial wreath was sent by his workmates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>ULCER FROM KNEE TO ANKLE.</head>
        <p>ULCER FROM KNEE TO ANKLE.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Prevents Amputation And Saves The Limb. HOW a Iliabieg saved his leg from amputation was, revealed to o, London, Press representative, when be had a chat with. I M. E. T. Pring, of 50, Edward-street, Barking- road; Plaistow: Five little sores broke out on ray right lee said Mr. Pring, who has worked for many years on Thames coal barges. "Two 'similar places," he went on, "also came on my left leg. They began to discharge, and grew so large that ml right leg was one continuous ulcer from knee to ankle. All this part of the limb turned quite black, and seemed absolutely putrid. The place was continually discharging. "My right leg got so bad that I really thought I should have to have it amputated. The two ulcers on the left leg were almost as bad. Work WBB out of the question. "I tried several ointments for a long time, but without the least benefit. One day, how- ever, my, son-in-law brought me some Zam-Buk, with which I dressed my legs. This balm drew out the corruption from the diseased parts in a surprising way. Besides cleansing the ulcers, Zam-Buk also soothed the burning pain, and as I kept on with Zam-Buk I could see improve- ment every day. Zam-Buk carried on its heatt ing mission until the holes filled up with &amp;int healthy flesh, over which clear, new skin grew' I stand to-day, free from pain, and completerY cured by Zam-Buk of about the worst ulcerated leg any man could have." Mr. Pring's _wonderful experience win ere hope to other ,sufferers. No other substance possesses the same healing power as Zara-Ilnk, which will cure the most obstinate e,ase a eczema (especially workshop eezetaa); riiigwor'n" piles, poisoned wounds, preVientr Olaf recurrence of the trouble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>SUMMER SPORTS ONCE MORE.</head>
        <p>SUMMER SPORTS ONCE MORE.</p>
        <p>Ladies And Gentlemen Don Flannels Amid</p>
        <p>'Sunshine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>50,000 SOLDIERS VISIT SWIMMING BATHS.</head>
        <p>50,000 SOLDIERS VISIT SWIMMING BATHS.</p>
        <p>The rain of the last day or two has certainly backed up away from the greens, but a succes- sion of days of brilliant sunshine has. made Pleasant the beginning of our summer 'sports. But hasn't the absence of many 01. gpx men- folk made a difference?</p>
        <p>Much more than in the outdoor pasties of winter do the two sexes take an interest in those of summer. Apart from golf -awl hockey there is no winter game in which women gener- ally join. Football, running, and fishing—per- haps they are a little too impatient for this— (one never knows)—are taboo for them, and Ilaosti of their recreation is derived from walks. Men have crowded the football fields at Hood- lane and Wilderspool. Often the ladies present Could have been counted on one.hand, or put in a gig. It takes a man to become enthusiastic over football. Perhaps women will warm to it When foot warmers are installed in the stands. '</p>
        <p>Sunshine charms them into the open and lnparative statistics as to the number of ladies and gentlemen following the sports of tennis and swimming in the town arid district ,Would prove interesting. The courts at Arpley, Latchford, Padgate and Bewsey already swarm With ladies, and to the delights of the game they add the refined social element which is very welcome and interesting to the gentlemen mem- bers. At the Corporation baths they are already taking the plunge in considerable numbers, though, of course, it is rather early in the season for the ladies' bath to be crowded. Some people refrain from swimming until May is out--it is habit ingrained by fear of "the shivers."</p>
        <p>There is little to report about cricket. The Pitch at Az:pley is somewhat unkempt, and tres- passers may be 'prosecuted. No matches are to be played this year. Juniors at a few works or associated with various Sunday schools are just about, keen, but no League controls their activities. Friendly matches are to be the order. Youngsters in'the parks still fight jheir liattlep with three sticks at one end and a jacket at the other, emulating " over-hands " to the danger of the heads of the batsboys.</p>
        <p>The bowling greens are alive with players. No, they are not playing for drinks this year. The law will not let them. Generally, the greens are in good fettle, and we may expect some capital handicaps and matches, which will. however, be mostly in aid of charity. The usual cups will be competed for, however.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>SOLDIERS ENJOY SWIMMING.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS ENJOY SWIMMING.</p>
        <p>Also Boys And Girls From Elementary</p>
        <p>Schools.</p>
        <p>It is an interesting fact that since the beginning of the war almost 50,000 soldiers have been to the Corporation Swimming Baths. This is the popular manager's computation. And, of course, Mr. Barlow is that popular manager. Soldiers—really they were recruits, with the Ring's shilling still burning in their pockets— do not come with such frequency nowadays, oWing to an alteration in the system of de- parture. At first, on men enlisting at, or being drafted to, Orford Barracks, they used to be marched down to the .baths in companies. Nowadays, the men wash at the Barracks and are sent forthwith to their various regiments quartered in divers places. There is no time to call at the baths. Many hundreds have gone into the baths as eivilians and come out Soldiers. On enlisting in the 4th South Lancashire Regiment they have been served with their -khaki and under:. clothing and told at the Prill Hall to pop round to the baths and have a swim before putting them on. They have entered with their uniform under their arms and come out with their mufti in bundles. A most excellent and patriotic change of clothing; although sometimes the puttees did look inelegant—as if, say, they were a size too big.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>SCHOOL CHILDREN'S DIP.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL CHILDREN'S DIP.</p>
        <p>On Monday the school children re-commenced their swimming lessons. Every afternoon from 2 to 4 ollock scholars from 11 years old have emerged from the various elementary schools in batches, and for half an hour enjoyed the cool and invigorating waters of the boys' large bath. All ,the time has not been wasted in floundering about, for the boys have kept Mr. J. Peers busy in instructing them in the art of swimming. The girls attend two mornings a rweek—on Tuesdays and Thursdays—and place themselves in the keeping of Miss Beresford. Forty boys and thirty girls occupy their re- spective baths at one time. Owing to the large number of members who have enlisted,, the Warrington Swimming Club is submitting no programme either of sports or Polo matches this year, but about the middle of September the Sports Committee are to hold their annual gala for the children who have learned to swim this year. The gala will also include events for more experienced though youthful swimmers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>SPORT AT ARPLEY.</head>
        <p>SPORT AT ARPLEY.</p>
        <p>No Cricket, But Bowling and Tennis KING AND QUEEN HAVE THEIR Still Game. WAYS AND MEANS 'MAIN. The Warrington Cricket, Bowling and Lawn Tennis Club has arranged a very quiet season for its members, but there is every reason to believe that the members will enjoy their Pastimes. The cricketing section will be idle, but this will occasion no surprise, considering the num- ber of members who have responded to the countrY's call to arms. They have forsaken the green sward for the gun and bayonet. The earnest wish of all club members is that this brave band of sportsmen will soon return, and be able to. participate in favourite recreation before the autumn dawns.</p>
        <p>The King and Queen i Bowling Club brought off their Ways and Means Main on Saturday. The bowling was keen and some close games were witnessed. The entries numbered 36, and the results were as follows:</p>
        <p>Second Round.—W. Leigh 15, F. Rothwell 7; N. Holloway 15, J. Barnard 13'; -8:-Garnett 15, D. Bromley, 8; J. Moorfield 15, H. Wilde 7;' T. Timperley 15, R. Tomlinson 7; F.' Faulkes 15, W. Sankey 12; W. Johnstone 15, J. Watkins 10; H. Gatley 15, J. Thompson 11. Third Round.—H. Galley 15, F. Faulkes 12; S. Garnett 15, W. Johnstone 10; W. Leigh 15, W. Holloway 12; J. Moorfield 15, T. Timperley 6. Semi-Final.—S. Garnett 15, J. Moorfield 7; W. Leigh 15, H. Gatley 11.</p>
        <p>• This is the second- season there has been no cricket. Yet the club has in no way suffered financially, but is better off than at any time during its career. This is due to the fact that the club had no professional to pay and no Match expenses to meet. In all, some thirty- five members of the cricketing and bowling sections are to-day wearing khaki instead of Wielding King Willow" and sending the wood to touch "Jack." These true and good sports- men are not to be forgotten, for the club has decided that their names shall be inscribed on a roll of honour.</p>
        <p>Final. W. Leigh</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>Bowling As Usual.</head>
        <p>Bowling As Usual.</p>
        <p>Bowling will be in vogue as much as usual. The war has not affected the membership of this section to any extent; but this "free and easy" pastime will be on a- slightly modified basis. During the season there will be only one match—that with the Seaforth Club—which might almost be termed as a "hardy annual" and a fixture to which the members always look keenly forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>Tennis Renaissance.</head>
        <p>A Tennis Renaissance.</p>
        <p>Tennis has become a popular section of the club, 'and the ladies will be able to indulge in many pleasant games to their hearts' content. They will probably miss the presence of many gentlemen devotees of the game, but they will be able to enjoy themselves. The 'membership 18 steadily on the increase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Women of the Farm.</head>
        <p>Women of the Farm.</p>
        <p>Ingenuous Maiden (on being told she is ex- Pected to milk the cow) : "Oh, Mum 1 dursn't without a soldier holdiw her head."— 'Punch."</p>
        <p>Our Economists. Customer: I've called about the cough-mix- tore I bought. The first dose cured me." - Chemist: "The instantaneous effect of that reparation, sir, has been remarked by every- body..., ' Customer: "It's amazing; and as there's only mle dose gone, I thought perhaps you'd change 'what was left for some photographic plates."— " Punch."</p>
        <p>4 • --7:111111C, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>BOWLS.</head>
        <p>BOWLS.</p>
        <p>NA 111RINGTON WORKS AMATEUR ASSOCIArI lON.</p>
        <p>Wni• Fund Handicap.</p>
        <p>DRAW FOR THE FIRST ROUND.</p>
        <p>The draw and handicap for the War Fund Handicap took place on Tuesday night. The players will be required to play again on the same day. The committee have decided not to close the entries for a few weeks. The games to be played this afternoon are as follows:</p>
        <p>H. Hill (G.) 7, T. W. Jackeon (WM.) 5. A. Hayes (P. &amp; K.) 7, W. Eckersley. jun.(W.) 6. A. Shaw (C.) 6, J. Milner, jun. (W.) 6. R. Warburton (C.) 5, A. Lawton (W.) 4. A. Gleave, jun. (C.) 1, T: Owen (F.S.T.) 5. H. Greenough (C.) 6, J. Milner, gen. (W.) 6. H. McMahon (F.S.T.) 5,3. Ashburner (C.) 5. T. Croft .(8.G.) 6, G. Harrop (R.) 4.</p>
        <p>• 2.20. W. Aleock (G.).5. B. Jennings (C.) 5. W. Caddick (R.3t.) 4, H. Potter (R.) 5. It Bighorn (31.11.) 4, R. Tomlinson (C.L.) 5. S. Brooks, (R.M.) 6, James Stnith (W.) 7. P. Tickle (G) 7, C. Atkinson (C.) 6. O. Chamberlain (R.M.) 6, W. Watson (5.H.C.)5. J. Parsons (R.M.) 5, W. Woods ((IN.) 5.' J. Clarke (S.H.C.) 3, W. Savory (W.C.) 5.</p>
