      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>St.Thomas' Beat Rylands's Rec.</p>
        <p>In The Final Tie.</p>
        <p>A large crowd assembled at Wilderspool on Saturday to witness the final tie in the local Intermediate League between St. Thomas' and Rylande's Rec., the former team gaining a well- deserved victory by 13 points to nil. • The Rylands's backs played 'a 'wretched game, and it must have been heart-rending to the for- wards, who got the ball out of the scrimmages nine times out of ten, only to see the men behind bungle the ball in an unaccountable manner. Although the big Rylands's forwards commanded the scrimmages, the smaller lads from Stockton Heath were a force to be reck- oned with when it came to loose work, and they gave 'much assistance to their backs. Rylands's were decidedly off form. They have played better before. Rylands's won the toes and St. Thomas' started against a strong sun. Touch was fpund in the Rylands's quarters, and from the istrim- mage Fitzgerald received, and after a fine, tricky run found- touch near Eylands'g line. St. Thomas' did not stay here for long, for, from the 'scriiimage, they .were penalised for offside. The free-kick came as a great relief to Rylands's were decidedly off. form. They have Line. -</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' were soon back again: however, and when in the vicinity of the Recs.' line were awarded- a free-kick for offside. Lloyd tried for goal, but his shot went wide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>Fine Run and Tackle.</head>
        <p>A Fine Run and Tackle.</p>
        <p>From the re-start, Britton received for Rylands's and essayed to get Hill going, but the latter was pushed into touch before pro- ceeding far. Play was now removed to the other wing, where Rylands's made some pro- gress and got into their opponents' quarters. Here Caldwell received, and, with a fine solo effort, ran past the half-way line before being " grassed." From the scrimmage the St. Thomas' threequarter-backs broke away and were soon hammering at the Rylands's line. The ball became loose and Fitzgerald made a brilliant cue through the defence and seemed a certain scorer, but an equally fine tackle brought him to Mother Earth. St. Thomas' were now penalised. and the Rylands's for- wards, who were following up quickly, looked dangerous, but Fitzgerald relieved finely. Good forward work took St. Thomas' back again. and they were awarded another free-kick, but Lloyd again failed. From the re-start, Rylands's attacked and Archer was applauded for a nice run along touch. Lloyd, however, received, and with a splendid kick found touch in the corner. A scrimmage was formed a few yards from the line, from which the ball became loose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Roll Past and a Pounce.</head>
        <p>A Roll Past and a Pounce.</p>
        <p>A Rylands's player, standing just on the line, appeared to have plenty of time to pickup, but allowed the ball to roll through his hands, and, before he could recover himself, Berry had flung himself upon the sphere and scored. Noden failed at goal. This happened after 25 minutes' play, and from now to half-time give-and-take was the order.</p>
        <p>Half-time: St. Thomas', 1 try (3 points); . • Sylandies; Soon 'after the resumption Archer had to kick dead to save his lines and a five yards' scrimmage was ordered. From this, however, Rylands's forwards came away with a rush and removed play to centre. From the scrimmage they gave their backs a good opportunity, but the passing waa woefully weak. Lloyd was forced to mark when hard pressed, and touch Was found at the centre. Rylands's were often losing ground through getting offside from the scrimmage. By means of good forward work St. Thomas' again broke away, and Lloyd put in a high kick. He followed up at top speed and charged down Archer's punt. Gathering finely', he' sped away, and scoreil ' under the stumps. The • try was converted and St. Thomas' were eight points Up. Rylands's were now a beaten team, and it was not long before the St. Thomas' threequarter-backs joined in a fine round of passing, and Lloyd again went in. Again the goal-kick was successful. As, time was drawing near Rylands's made one or two spasmodic bursts, but St. Thomas' were equally "at home" ,on the defence as on the attack and they staved off all danger. Towards the close. Sutton, of Rylands's, was sent off the field for tripping. Result: St.. Thomas', 2 goals 3 tries (13 points); Rylands's, nil. On. the winning side all played well, but none better than the diminutive Fitzgerald. He Was a host in himself, and both in defence and attack he excelled. He also kicked with fine judgment. On the Rylands's side the forwards played a hard game, as also did Archer at full-back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Teams.</head>
        <p>The Teams.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas'.—Fitzgerald; Marsh, Lloyd, Cald- well and Noden; Berry and Leah; Glen, Hooper, Sankey, Wibberley, Clarke and Rylance. llylands's Recs.—T. Archer; Latimer, Little- wood, Britton and Hill; Savoury and Swanton; Dumbell (captain), Sutton, Barker, Lee, Mulvey and Butler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>Watches for the Players.</head>
        <p>Watches for the Players.</p>
        <p>At the close of the match the Mayor (Alder- man P. Peacock). presented watches to the teams. He coxnplimented both teams on their displays, remarking that although Rylands's had played hard they had met a better team that day. On the proposition of Alderman Tinnion, seconded by Mr. T. Locker, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Mayor.</p>
        <p>iL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <p>111111Warl. PI rAIIII,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>I.)L-4P</head>
        <p>I.)L-4P</p>
        <p>(By permission of the Proprietors of "Punch,</p>
        <p>EXHILARATED VISITOR (leaving club) : fish 's dem liar."</p>
        <p>PLAYERS OF PROMISE.</p>
        <p>Lads In The Intermediate League Who Are 'Winning Spurs.</p>
        <p>In casting back over the matches which have taken place in the local Intermediate Competi- tion during the past season, one cannot help but think of the fine displays given by a num- ber of the teams. There are certainly some smart players among them, and in the near future one or two will be worth a trial in better company. Indeed, one of them—Lloyd, the left centre of the St. Thomas' team—has already played for Rochdale in the Northern Union League on several occasions, and, we are informed, has shown every satisfaction, and should turn out a first-class player. He is being coached arduously by Stan. Young, the well-known Warrington -half-back, and it is evident Lloyd is learning some of the "tricks of the trade" from an old hand. Indeed; those who witnessed him score the tries against Parochial and Rylands's will not readily forget him. How he followed up the ball the moment it had left his foot, and in what a brilliant manner he gathered it after charging down the kick! He possesses a fine turn of speed, and kicks. -strongly and with judgment. Another fine player on the St. Thomas' team is Fitzgerald, the full-back. It is rather a pity that he is so much on the small side, but what he lacks in this direction he makes up for in trickiness, sure catching; and -fine kicking.. His. display last Saturday against .Rylands's was a treat- to watch. lie_ ran the oppOsing forwards Off their feet. What an adept he is at drawing the opposition! Times without number he wOuld dart oft towards the left-wing with about four Rylands's forwards in hot pursuit. Sud- ,denly he would stop, and veering round in an altogether different direction, would leave his pursuers standing. Of players in the other teams, Brown, of the Parochial, is a good centre, and Lingard, of Heathside, a speedy wing man, while Rylands's possess a strong set of forwards. Jones '(Bolton) is a good half-back, his display against Widnes being very good. Several teams have retired from the League since the commencement of the season, through not being able to raise full complements. Referees have always been available, and to them the thanks of the players are due, they having conducted the matches admirably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>, On Saturday, owing to strong counter attrac- tions by the final tie in the Intermediate Rugby Competition, the attendances at Hood,-lane and other Association ettmes were not as large as usual. ,</p>
        <p>On the Hood-lane ground, under ideal condi- tions, a very good game was witnessed in the return fixture with Stalybridge Celtic team. The latter are a very attractive side. The Warringtonians were intent on making amends for their defeat from their opponents the pre- vious Saturday. A few minutes from the kick- off, the home team gained the lead, and so tenaciously did they play on the visitors' defence that they soon improved their position, and from this stage the result never appeared to be in doubt. The home team fully merited their victory, and it was chiefly owing to the good display by Reynolds in the visitors' goal that there was not a greater margin at the call of time.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Medal Com- petition semi-final ties there were two very keenly contested games. At Victoria Park, the opposing teams wore St. Elphin's and Winwick Rovers, and although the spectators were few, the display of both teams was not lacking in quality. The "Saints," with the aid of a strong wind, gained the substantial lead of two goals at the interval. This did not appcar to dishearten the Rovers, and on resuming it was soon eviilent they were going to fight hard, and in a few minutes they opened their account. A spell of even and keen play followed, and eventually the Rovers reasserted themselves and, got the " Saints' " defence in a muddle, and the scores were made equal. The remaining ten minutes of the game was more in favour of the Rovers, and Walters in goal for the ", paints " did not show his customary coolness in dealing with the shots that were sent in. His backs did not retain their form, and 'the Rovers secured the winning goal in a rather easy fashion.</p>
        <p>The other semi-final tie was played on the Atherton Quay ground and a fair number of spectators witnessed the game. Prom the kick- off the game was keenly contested, and each goal was visited without result. Then the Athletic had more of the play, but Knight and his backs defended splendidly, and at the interval neither side had scored. The second half was nearly a repetition of the first, and when it appeared as if a drawn game was very evident, the Athletic inside right, made a grand individual effort and sent in a shot which de- ceived Knight and just passed under the bar. Attempts to equalise were made by the Em- manuel, but these were stoutly challenged by the Athletic defenders, and at the call of time the Athletic ran out winners by the only goal scored. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CONIBINATION. Final Match In This Season's 0, Competition.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CONIBINATION. Final Match In This Season's 0, Competition.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN v. STALYBRIDGE</p>
        <p>CELTIC.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between tho above clubs was played on Saturday on the flood-lane ground, before MOO spectators. The ground was in good condition and a keen game was anticipated. Neither team was as advertised,</p>
        <p>" The feller who caught. that</p>
        <p>Cookson being absent from the home eleven, which led to the inclusion of Aepinall. The following players turned out:— • • Warrington Town: Kite; Leah and Millward Ashworth, Whitfield and Bullen; Connor, Illidge, Aspinall; Taylor and Owen. Stalybridge: Reynolds; Egerton and Lockett; Bradley, Robinson and Francis; Brindle, Duck- worth, Mosley, Beevors and Burtenshaw. The home captain won the toss and defended the bottom goal. The game opened in favour of the home team, Owen receiving a \good pass from Bullen, but he was in an offside position. A brief visit to the home end was followed by an attack on the visitors' goal, when a good return by Lockett sent them back and good play by Millward relieved the situation at the home end. From a free-kick Kite easily- cleared and the leather was quickly returned to the opposite end, where Reynolds, proved to be safe. -Good play-by the visitors' front rank placed the home goal in danger, Mosley being just too Wide with his final effort. A dangerous movement on the. visitors' right was neatly repulsed by Millward, and a race to the visitors' end re- Suited in a corner being conceded. The flag- lick was well placed by Owen, and an exciting, tussle took place in the visitors' goal-mouth. The ball was eventually got away. Then followed a good shot by Whitfield, which was safely cleared by Reynolds,. and in the next minute Ashwoith was- just foe high with a fast shot.. Play was transferred to the home end, and the final 'effort. from. a.. cornetAtiek..w:or just-wide of the uprights. From the goal-kick the home forwards attacked' smartly; and .fro nv a good centre by Owen, Aspinall ghat in towards goal, and following up strongly, he regained the ball from a partial clearance by Reynolds, and opened the score after twenty minutes' play. Encouraged by this success, the home team played very keenly, and returned to the attack, the visitors' goal having a lucky escape from a corner-kick, Robinson clearing from a lot of players. Keeping up the pressure, the home team attacked strongly, Aspinall hitting the up- right with a -low shot, but in the next minute he safely steered the ball past Reynolds for the second goal. After this reverse the visitors re- plied strongly, and Leah was only just in time to clear his lines. Then, owing to some loose exchanges by the home defence, Mosley got through and beat Kite with a fast shot. The visitors now made stroneeforts to equalise, but Millward cleared at the critical moment, and from this the home forwards got clear of the visitors' backs, Illidge finally shooting wide of the 'posts with only Reynolds to beat. A race to the home end saw Kite clear easily, and then Taylor forced a corner off Egerton. This was cleared by Reynolds. Bullen returned with a hot drive, and scored the third goal for the home team. From the centre-kick an attack on the home goal was well cleared by Millward and a few minutes later Taylor scored a faurth goal for the home team. The interval arrived with the score:—Warrington Town, 4; Staly- bridge Celtic, 1. Owing to a late start the refeiee did not allow an interval, and play was quickly resumed. After even exchanges in midfield the home for- wards got to close quarters, Illidge sending in a shot which found Reynolds to be on the alert, and a little later the same player was just too late to accept a fine centre from Owen. A big kick by Egerton put the home goal in danger, and Leah and Millward were hard pressed by the opposing forwards, but managed to clear. This was followed by a short visit to the oppo- site end, the- ball being -quickly returned by Lockett. A fruitless corner kick followed. An- other attack on the home goal 'resulted in Duck- worth heading the bailout of the reach of Kite. Miliward was\,.inanzainent - with an individual effort, causing Reynolds to, handle, and later Robinson caused Kite to clear hurriedly. • A visit to the visitors' goal, led by Connor, saw Reynolds save well from Aspinall, and at the home end Bullen made a timely clearance, and Aspinall was again prominent with strong forc- ing play. Reynolds stepped into the breach again, and scooped the ball away, and the ball being returned by Whitfield, Taylor was just too late to accept a good opening. An accident to Taylor caused a brief stoppage and from the free-kick Kite was called upon to clear a long shot, while at the other end Egerton checked Aspinall when he .was getting dangerous. A spell of mid-field play was fol- lowed by a raid on the visitors' goal, and after Reynolds had made another good clearance Aspinall shot wide. Some long passing, by the visitors' forwards put the home goal in danger, but Leah came to the rescue and then Burten- shaw shot wide from long range. Good passing between Connor and Midge enabled the former to lead an attack on the visitors' goal and from his centre Taylor had not much difficulty in notching the fifth goal. From the centre- kiek Brindle and Duckworth combined splen- didly, but Leah and Millward were equal to the occasion. Next Owen tested Reynolds with a good shot and Beevors replied with a good attempt at the home goal. Play was in mid- field when the- final Whistle blew, the result being :7—Warrington Town, 5; Stalybridge, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>FOOTBALL.—Continued.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.—Continued.</p>
        <p>across the field was not of much assistance to either team. The opening exchanges were in favour of the Athletic and several corner kicks were conceded, but were not improved upon; then even play in midfield followed for a while, and the Athletic advanced again, Broome send- ing in a shot which was safely cleared by Knight. From the goal kick the Institute for- wards changed the venue of attack, and Brime- low hit the woodwork with a good effort. Both teams now settled down to a keen game, the Athletic showing to the best advantage, and Knight made a good clearance with a shot from Gregory. At this stage Crozier, Knight and Parr were prominent in defence for the Insti- tute, and the Athletic half-backs were playing a very, strong game. At halt-time neither team had scored.-</p>
        <p>The second portion opened very keenly .and each goal was quickly visited, but the defence on each side proved equal to the occasion, and the spectators were very generous in their applause for any good play that was shown. Good combination by the Athletic presented Cleaver with a splendid opening, but. he hesi- tated, and Knight dashed out and cleared from his toe. After this lucky escape the Institute transferred play to the opposite end and the Athletic defence was severely tested, Hum- phries making several fine saves. Brimelow was keeping his forwards well together by judicious passing, and William Eaton was only inches wide with a rasping shot. A little later Humphries made a splendid save from a low shot by Meachin. A goal-kick gave relief to the Athletic defence, and their forwards, gain- ing possession, broke away, and Hill, beating three opponents, sent in a lobbing shot, which deceived Knight and just passed under the bar, gaining the first point for the Athletic. After this reverse the Institute made several attempts to get on equal terms, but Humphries, Harri- son and Murty were on the alert and repulsed their efforts. Close upon time the Athletic forwards were smartly checked by Hughes at close quarters. No further goals were scored, and at the call of time the result was :—Widnes Athletic, 1 goal; Emmanuel Institute, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S V. WINWICK ROVERS.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S V. WINWICK ROVERS.</p>
        <p>The semi-final tie between the above clubs was played in Victoria Park, before a poor attendance of spectators. Mr. Herriman, of Widnes, had charge of the game and the fol- lowing players lined out:— St. Elphin's: Walters; Miller and Rawlinson; Worrell, Long and Welsby; Eccleston, Ford, Pritchard, Woodhouse and Hough. Winwick Rovers: Hollies; Riley and Sher- lock; Morse, Sherlock and Simms; Hodgson, Harrison, Gleave, Gee and Anderton. The "Saints" won the toss and played with the wind in their favour. Gleave set the ball rolling for the Rovers and at once made tracks for the St. Elpfiin's goaJ, which was partially repulsed by their backs, but the Rovers quickly returned to the attack, and Gleave, with an open goal, shot wide. The "Saints" now re- versed the order and set up a keen attack on the Rovers' citadel, and several corner-kicks were conceded, Woodhouse converting after twenty minutes' play. Encouraged by this success, the "Saints" continued to have the best of the exchanges, and Hollies, Riley and Sherlock defended stubbornly. Owing to the strong breeze and the keen tackling of the St. Elphin's half-backs, the Rovers' forwards could not make much headway, and the "Saints" forwards. were showing good combination, Eccleston being very speedy and centring accu- rately. Pritchard scored the second goal. After this reverse the Rovers' left wing. caused trouble to the "Saints'" defence, but their efforts were finally repulsed, and at the oppo- site end Hollies dealt successfully with several good shots. Half-time :—St. Elphin's, 2 goals; Winwick Rovers, nil. On resuming the Rovers had the assistance of the wind, and their forwards quickly settled to their game. Gleave reduced their arrears with capital shot. From the centre kick some strenuous play was witnessed, and the Rovers' backs cleared their lines strongly. During an attack by the Rovers the Saints' defence wavered ih their tactics, and Hodgson scored the equalising goal. From now the game was more keenly contested, and eventually the Rovers' defence succeeded in transferring play to the opposite end, where the Saints' defence was subjected to heavy pressure. In the closing stages of the game the Saints' defence was again at fault, and Glmve registered the win- ning goal. Result: Winwick, 3 goals; St. Elphin's, 2 goals.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDAL 'COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Final Tie.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above Competition Com- mittee was held on Tuesday night at the Mer- sey Hotel, Mr. G. Bamber presiding. The Secre- tary gave a report of the result of the semi-final ties and the receipts, which were considered satisfactory. The final tie between the Widnes Athletic and Winwick Rovers will be played to-day on the Atherton Quay ground, Liverpool-road. Officials:—</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. G. Ember. Linesmen: Mr. Hinds, Warrington Central; Mr. Hatton, Sankey Athletic.</p>
      </div>
