        <p>Money.</p>
        <p>Why He Spoke. "Look here, my lady," eXclaimed the thrifty man to his extravagant wife, "you're carrying too much sail." "Why should that worry me?" she retorted. "Because I have • to raise the wind; that's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1916.</p>
        <p>been very careless with her children, and'on one occasion one of her children was seen at the door with lighted .paper in its hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND AND WIFE SENTENCED. Warrington Constable's Rough Time</p>
        <p>In Horsemarket Street.</p>
        <p>• At the Borough Court on Saturday, Patrick Patten, of 10, Cockhedge-lane, was sent to prison for three months on charges of being drunk and disorderly and assaulting P.C. Gibbs, while his wife, Jane Patten, whose address was given as 10, Dolman's-lane, was given one month's imprisonment for assaulting the constable and using obscene language. P.C. Gibbs stated that at 9.40 on Friday night his attention was called to a disturbance in Horsemarket-street, opposite Lyme-street. He proceeded there and found both prisoners struggling with each other. There was a crowd of three to four hundred people around prisoners. The woman used obscene language and when he tried to take her into custody the male prisoner interfered and he had to let her go. The ,man struck him a violent blow in the ; face and a struggle followed on the ground. He- caught hold of witness by the neck and tried to strangle him. Witness was also kicked. The ‘crowd only looked on, ,and with the assist- ance of a 'civilian and P.C. Steel he got" pri- soner to the Police Station. P.C. Steel said he went to the scene of the disturbance because a woman _told him that a man and woman were murdering a police offi- cer. The male 'prisoner told :P.C. Gibbs thp.t he hoped the Germans would kill all his sons at the Front. The Chief Constable said the man had been ten times previously convicted and had been four times before the Court this year. The woman had "five previous convictions, the last being in December last, when she received three months for neglecting her children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>DEBAUCH IN CHOCOLATES.</head>
        <p>DEBAUCH IN CHOCOLATES.</p>
        <p>How A Girl Wasted• Money Stolen From Employer.</p>
        <p>_ A regrettable story was heard at the Juvenile Court on Wednesday, when a ;poung girl was charged with stealing £1 is. 6d. from her employer, Helen Leach, newsagent and tobacco- nist, 135, Bewsey-road. The girl entered the employment of the prosecutrix in December last, and on March Bth was sent with a parcel of tobacco to another shop, the money for 'which was sent back with the girl. She never gave the money up. It was stated that the girl's father was serving in France. The house was in a clean condition, and there was no reason why she should steal. None of the money had been recovered. The girl said she had spent the whole of the money in chocolates and visiting the pictures. The magistrates discharged the girl with a caution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>DANGEROUS PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>DANGEROUS PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>Nlischievous Boys Uncouple Waggons On L. &amp; N.-W. Railway.</p>
        <p>Six youths appeared at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday charged with trespassing on the London and North-Western Railway. A police officer' stated that at 4.30 p.m. on April ROI he was on duty in Wilderspool-road, when he saw defendants on the railway sidings pushing trucks about and uncoupling them. When approached they all ran away, but when apprehended later, admitted the offence. Detective Hewitt, if • the L. Sand N.-W. Rail- way Company, said it was a• very dangerous practice both for the company and the boys themselves, for they might easily have beefi crippled. Fined 7s. 6d. each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>BIRCH FOR STONE-THROWING.</head>
        <p>BIRCH FOR STONE-THROWING.</p>
        <p>At . the Juverrile Police Court on. Wednesday five boys, were .charged with throwing stones to the danger of Persons on the London and North- Western Railway. One was firiecll.os. and the others were ordere.l to be birched. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>"ROUGH RIDES HEREAFTER."</head>
        <p>"ROUGH RIDES HEREAFTER."</p>
        <p>Chief Constable And Cheshire Motor Cyclist.</p>
        <p>The defendant having been called up for ser- vice, the borough magistrates on Tuesday took up a lenient attitude in the case of Wm. Daniels, 2, Woodfield-road, Broadheath, who was sum- moned for riding a motor bicycle in Knutsford- road to the danger of the public, having regard to all the circumstances.</p>
        <p>The police evidence was to the effect that on the morning of the 24th ultimo, defendant was driving a motor cycle along the Knutsford- road at 25 miles an hour. Tie increased the speed when he got into the wide part of the road, and took no notice of the police call for hint to stop. Defendant said he did not travel more than 20 miles per hour, and he did not hear the, police shout him to stop. Ile had always tried to keep within the speed limit. It was pointed out to defendant that there was no, speed limit, speed being regulated by the conditions prevailing on the road at the time. The Chief Constable said he understood de- fendant had been called up and, in 'order to avdid an endorsement on his licence, he suggested that the case should be dismissed on payment of costs. "Defendant might have some rough rides hereafter," added the Chief Constable. The case was dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>AN UGLY WISH.</head>
        <p>AN UGLY WISH.</p>
        <p>Man AVho Said Wife Ought To Be In</p>
        <p>River.</p>
        <p>"I have nothing to do with the drawing of the blinds, My wife ought to .do that, and she has been drinking all day. She ought to be at the bottom of the river."</p>
        <p>SO remarked Michael Cunningham, of 19, Bank-street, to a police officer who, on noticing a light shining from the kitchen of defendant's house, went to ascertain why the blind was not drawn.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, Cunningham was fined 58.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>I CHARGE WITHDRAWN.</head>
        <p>I CHARGE WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>The adjourned charge against Esther Futter, 9; Bridgewater-street, of failing to reduce a light at her house, was, at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, withdrawn, on the applicatiOn of the Chief Constable. The Chief Constable explained that when a police officer recently visited her house it was found to be in a very filthy condition. The defendant was before the Court on April sth, when the case was adjourned to see if an improvement was made. It was now reported that the house was in a much better condition. Hence he asked for the withdrawal of the ease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>NEED FOR FIREGUARDS.</head>
        <p>NEED FOR FIREGUARDS.</p>
        <p>Bench Give Strong Lecture To N Neglectful Mother.</p>
        <p>"You will be fined 10s. You are getting off very easily this morning. If your own con- science isn't troubling you, it ought to be, after your disgraceful behaviour." So remarked Mr. A. J. Jolley, chairmanpf the Bench at the Borough Police Court on Thurs- day, to Mrs. Ellen Ashton, 9, Pierpoint-street, who.was charged with allowing a child under seven years of hge to be in a room with an open firegrate: • ' A detective stated that as a result of child of the defendant aged ten months, recently suc- cumbing at the Infirmary from burns received at home, he visited the house at one o'clock in the afternoon, of April 9th. The defendant was out, and there were • three children in the kitchen, aged eight, four and two years, play- ing about in the room. There was no fireguard or fender. When the defendant returned he asked her if she thought it was right that these children should be playing about with no fire- guard to protect -them. Witness asked her if she could account for the way her child got on fire, and she replied that she left the child tied in •a rocking chair whilst she went out. On her return another of her children Met her, and upon going in she found that the child's flan- nelette night jacket had been burned, and also, the lower part of her body. At the inquest -both- defendant ,and her, husband said they did not see the use of a firtinard: :_She- seemed to have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>ALLEGED SAVAGE ASSAULT.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED SAVAGE ASSAULT.</p>
        <p>Ironworker Charged With Wounding</p>
        <p>His Mother-in-Law.</p>
        <p>Harry Byron, ironworker, 379, Winwick-road, was charged at the Borough Court on Monday 'with • wounding his mother-in-law, Mary Thompson, 373, Winwick-road, by kicking and striking her. The Chief Constable, in asking for ti remand for a week, said on Sunday night Byron went to the house of his mother-in-law and used bad language. He was under the influence of drink, and his mother-in-law remonstrated with him. al6cused kicked and punched her, with the result that she had to be taken to the Infirmary, where she was, found to have two ribs broken. -That day he the Chief Constable) had received information that her condition was serious.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Hewitt gave evidence to arresting accused, who said: "She is always nagging at me. Is she badly hurt?" When charged, EI:CCIIReI. replied: "I didn't kick her. I had no shoes or stockings on." On this evidence accused was remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>MOTORING OFFENCES.</head>
        <p>MOTORING OFFENCES.</p>
        <p>Danger Of Lending A Motor Cycle To</p>
        <p>A Friend.</p>
        <p>A number of motorists appeared before the Bench at the Borough Police Court. on Thurs- day, charged .with committing offences under the Motor Car Acts.</p>
        <p>James Rail, of Kingsley, near Frodsha.m, was fined 201,1. for causing an obstruction in Butter- market-street with his motor car.</p>
        <p>S. L. Forbes, of - London, was charged with driving a motor ear minus a front identification plate, and was fined £5, his licence having been endorsed several times previously.:</p>
        <p>For driving a motor lorry along Sankey-street at a speed dangerous to the public, James Hannaghan, 26, Higher Temple-street, Man- chester (who did not appear), was fined 40s. It was stated that he travelled through both the wide and narrow parts of Sankey-street at a very quick speed, and never sounded his horn. Edward Shuker, of Audlem, who drove a motor waggon and trailer in Parker-street without a licence or identification plate, was fined £2 or 28 days. , William Farrington, 24, Beswick-street, was summoned for driving a motor bicycle to the common-danger of the public.—The Chief Con- stable explained that Farrington was not really the defendant. He lent his bicycle to a friend, along with his licence, and when stopped by the police, the rider of the bicycle gave Farring- ton's name and address. Consequently, the latter had been summoned, but he (the Chief Constable) asked for the withdrawal of tha case, and intimated that the proper person woula be summoned.—This course was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>FAILED TO REPORT.</head>
        <p>FAILED TO REPORT.</p>
        <p>Another Haul At The Workhouse. Having failed to report under the Military Service Act, John Hall, Michael Kelleher, and John Cummings, labourers, of no fixed abodes, were, at the Borough Police Court on Thursday, ordered to be handed over to the military authorities.</p>
        <p>A police officer visited the Workhouse at 9.45 on Thursday morning, and there found the three prisoners, who admitted they were under 41 years of age, and that they had not reported under the Military Service Act. Two of the me'n told the magistrates that they thought they we,re medically unfit, but they were told they would be thoroughly examined by the military authorities.</p>
        <p>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTORS "HANDED</p>
        <p>OVER."</p>
        <p>Ernest Laycock, wiredrawer, engaged in mak- ing wire entanglements, residing at .162, Well- field-street, was, at the 'Borough Police Court on Friday, fined 40s. and ordered to be handed Over to the military authorities on a charge of failing to report himself under the Military Service Act.</p>
        <p>Detective Skelton stated that in consequence of receiving a notice from the military authorities he called at defendant's house and asked him why he had not reported himself. Defendant replied: "It is against my religion." Witness arrested him and brought him to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>Inspector Hodgkiss stated at the local Tribunal the defendant was granted non-com- batant service. He subsequently appealed to the Central Tribunal, where he was ordered full combatant service. Defendant was called upon to report himself, but he returned the notice, stating that he was a Christadelphian. Harold Richard Greenhalgh, clerk, 25, Dixon- street, was charged with a similar offence, and the Court inflicted a similar fine and order as in the case above.</p>
        <p>When Detective Green called at his house he said he was a conscientious objector.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>OUT OF THEIR TIME.</head>
        <p>OUT OF THEIR TIME.</p>
        <p>Young Engineers Who Wanted Full Money At Once.</p>
        <p>EMPLOYER'S CANDID ADMISSION.</p>
        <p>Should engineers on completing their appren- ticeship be paid full wages immediately? This was the important question facing the Warrington Munitions Tribunal on Monday, when two young men, who recently "came out of their time," as they put it, claimed that they should either he paid full money or be allowed to leave, so that they could get what they wanted elsewhere. They explained that on their seven years' apprenticeship ceasing they were given 30s. a week and promised increases of 2s. a week each three months until fifteen months had elapsed. They could, however, have full money immediately if they went elsewhere. Mr. I3ebbington (of Runcorn) who was sitting on the Tribunal as the workmen's representa- tive, asked the employer whether he would like to be held up in the way he was holding up these young men. "No," he admitted. "It is a case of everyone for himself."</p>
        <p>Mr. Varley (of St. Helens) who is on the employer's panel, remarked that if they granted discharge certificates there would be a hundred young men asking for the same thing next week: The cases were adjourned for a month, pend- ing a decision in the Court of Appeal respecting a case where a similar principle is involved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>GOING BACK TO CANADA.</head>
        <p>GOING BACK TO CANADA.</p>
        <p>AVounded Soldier Throws Over His Employers. _</p>
        <p>On being told that his discharge certificate from industrial employment would be _withheld an ex-wounded soldier, of 88, Slater-street, Latchford, at the Munitions Court on Monday, declared emphatically that he was going to go to Canada. Ho waved a passport to emphasise his declaratiOn. -</p>
        <p>Gilkes, it was stated, had aslied for a dis- charge ccrtificate from his employers on the ground that he was Unfit to do wiredrawing, and a doctor sent word that it was too laborious for him. The firm, however, pointed out that he was not wiredrawing, but wireweaving, which was a vastly different thing, and of doing which the applicant was fully capable. • The Chairman (Mr. T. J.aidgwaY),announced that the discharge certificate would be refused. and. then it was that Gilkes said, "Well, I'll go back.to Canada. You can keep my back time. I am away. You can fight for yoUr aountry."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>TIME-LOSER FINED.</head>
        <p>TIME-LOSER FINED.</p>
        <p>An ironworker, named Albert Cadman, of 166, Liverpool-road. was fined 255. at the Munitions Court 'on Monday, forfieglecting-bis work. It was stated that recentry he had lost 50 per cent. of his tithe, -and had lessened the production of war work considerably. He earned about 12s. 6d. per, day. • ' • ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>NURSE CHILD'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>NURSE CHILD'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Trial At The Assizes Ends In Dismissal.</p>
        <p>• JUDGE'S OPINION.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Thursday, before Mr. Justice Low, Ellen Goodwin (43) wife of Charles Goodwin, labourer, of 20, Cockhedge-lane, Warrington, was charged with the manslaughter of Henry Edge, otherwise known as Charles Goodwin, aged 2 years, at Warrington, on the 27th March last. Prisoner pleaded not guilty. Mr. D'Arcy (instructed by Mr. Arthur Browne) was for the prosecution, and Mr. Madden (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) was for the defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>PROSECUTION,</head>
        <p>THE PROSECUTION,</p>
        <p>Mr. D'Arcy said that at 10, Laurel-road, Fair- field, there lived' a certified midwife namel Pierce. In February, 1914,, Mrs. Piero was in communication with the prisoner, who was known as Mrs. Spencer, and at that time young woman named Mary tEdge was living in Mrs. Pierce's house, expecting her confinement. Edge was confined:of' a male child bn Feb- ruary 9th, 1914, and fourteen days afterwards the child was handed over to the prisoner with the sum of £lO for adopting and looking after him. The child was insured in September of that year, and from.that time up to March 22nd, nothing was heard of ,him. .The child died. on March 27th. A post-mortem examination was made, and it was stated the child died from acute bronchial pneumonia, accelerated by starvation. The child was two years and one month old, and should 'have weighed 321 b., but only weighed 1411 b.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>LIVERPOOL NURSE'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL NURSE'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Kate Mary Pierce, midwife, 10, Laurel- road, Fairfield, Liverpool, stated that the pri- soner had been in communication with her under the name of Mrs. Spencer, 10, Naylor- street, Warrington. Witness took the child to Warrington Station and. handed it to Mrs. Spencer along withSlo. Witness had not. regis.: tered the child. •</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Madden, witness said the child was prematurely born and was weak. She thought the child did very well to have lived for two years. _</p>
        <p>By his Lordship: This child would require more care than an ordinary child s—Yes. Did you tell the prisoner that?—Yes. She did not know where the mother of the child lived.</p>
        <p>Inspector Wainwright, Warrington, in cross- examination, said there was nothing to com- plain of in the external condition of the child when he saw it In the prisoner's arms. Dr. Peaeocke said when he Was called to see the child it was suffering from bronchitis. He examined it, and found t was dirty, emaciated and had been neglected. The bed in which all the family slept was dirty and in a state which was injurious to the health of a child. His Lordship: Was the- emaciation due to want of food? . Witness: Either the want of food or insuffi- cient food.</p>
        <p>It was not due to disease?—No, my lord,</p>
        <p>Dr. G. Binns, acting medical Officer at War- rington Union . Hospital, stated that when .he saw the child at that institution it was very emaciated, and was suffering from acute bron- chial pneumonia. It had signs of rickets, and he, formed the opinion that the child had been suffering for sometime. Witness added that the child died from acute bronchial pneumonia, accelerated by starvation, either from deprivation of food er improper feeding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>ACCUSED LOVED CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>ACCUSED LOVED CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The prisoner then gave evidence. She said the child had always been very delicate. He had often been attended to by a doctor, and she gave him milk every day, and other food, whicu he sometimes refused to take.</p>
        <p>Asked by Mr. D'Arcy why she took children to adopt, witness answered, "Because I love children."</p>
        <p>In answer to the judge, witness said that after she insured the child in September, 1914, she had called in the doctor eight or nine times. CHILD WAS MEDICALLY ATTENDED.</p>
        <p>On Friday the defence called Dr. Munson, one of the medical officers at' the Infirmary, who said he attended the child nine times between June 4th, 1914, and. December 19th, 1915. Dur- ing the latter part of his attendance the child was very emaciated. He could not say whether the child had been properly attended to. He found no occasion to complain or remonstrate with the prisoner as to her treatment of the child.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>NOT GUILTY.</head>
        <p>NOT GUILTY.</p>
        <p>Prisoner was found "Not Guilty "' and dis- charged. The Judge, referring to statements made by the prisoner at Warrington, in charging the jury, said that considering the circumstances in all its bearings, so far as these statements were concerned, they did not give the Court much assistance, and as the case had worked out he thought they need not trouble their heads about the statements.</p>
        <p>After the jury had brought in their verdict, his Lordship, addressing Mr. D'Arcy, said: "I 'should like you, if you would, to convey to the Treasury officials that on the evidence the verdict of the jury is perfectly correct, but I think it is a case which deserves some furl ther inquiry, particularly with regard to the establishment from which these children are supposed to have come." Mr. D'Arcy: quite understand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>OUR POLICE FORCE.</head>
        <p>OUR POLICE FORCE.</p>
        <p>Annual iGovernment Inspection By Captain Tomasson.</p>
        <p>The annual ,Government inspection, of the police force of the County Borough of War- rington, took place at the Police Station on Wednesday afternoon. Captain Tomasson, M.V.0., H.M. Inspector of Constabulary, arrived at 1.30 p.m., and was met at the Chief Constable's Office ,by his Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), and other members of the Watch Committee. The whole of the force with the exception of a few men on duty were aisembled at the police headquarters. After , Captiin Tomasson had made a minute inspection Of the clothing and accoutrements, the men fell in for drill, and were put through a number of evolutions by Inspector Hesp, drill instructor, the mov.linents. being carried out with smartness and precision. The Inspecting Officer also inspected the various rooms, offices and cells, and, examined the books and records. in the Chief. Constable's office, and before leaving expressed himself well pleased with all he had seen. Specicis Doing Very Good Work.</p>
        <p>There were on parade 42 member of the Special Constabulary, in charge of Special Inspector Mason, Acting Inspectors J. Moore Murray, and H. Stuart Page, and these officer.; were put through 'a short drill by ti o Drill Instructor. •</p>
        <p>Captain Tomasson, adressing the Special Con- stables, said :—" Gentlemen, I do not propose to keep you any longer, as I am well aware that you have your businesses to attendlo; but before I dismiss you I wish to thank you on behalf of the town, the Chief Constable, and also on be- half of the Home Office for the services you aro rendering in assisting the regular police as Special Constables. Believe me, gentlemen; you are doing a very good Work, and it is onlY4by such as you giving your time as .you have done and are doing that it has been possible to release a large, number of 'specially fitted men Mr ser- vice in H.M. Army. In 'all soinething like 12,000 regular members Of the police force are now serving thar country."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>TOWN V. KING'S,</head>
        <p>TOWN V. KING'S,</p>
        <p>Big Charity Match At Hood Lane ' To-day.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town F.C. are to meet the 15tb. King's (Liverpool) Regiment at Rood-lane. to- day, the proceeds to he devoted to the Mayor's War Fund. The Mayor will kick-on at 3.30. It will be a big match and a big crowd.' Strong teams are to be fielded, the Army being represented by: 'Croft (Huyton Quarry); Young (Bolton Wanderers), Woods (Darwen); Jones (Manchester City). • Rigby (Central.), Abrams (Chelsea),, Pownell (Port. Sunlight) • Kendrick (Warrington). Pilkington (Darwen), (Manebester City). Gaskin (African Royal) Anil Wallace (Bootle).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court.</p>
        <p>"Mary from Tipperary "—the companion play to "It's a long way to Tipperary," which was here last year—has been attracting satisfactory audiences this week. It has.,inyariably been a -sucCess each week .since it was firat staged.,</p>
        <p>The , company presenting it are, indeed, cap- able, and I have had interesting chats with some of them. Mr. Wilfred Lyndon, the well-known actor, who takes the part of the dutiful but vivacious Lieutenant Sterling, has had a rare career. For eighteen years he was with Minnie Palmer in "My Sweetheart," and travelled all over the world with it.</p>
        <p>Miss Palmer, who is now in Canada, made two or three fortunes out of it. Mr. Lyndon did not do so badly either. He mentioned figures which made one feel happy and think of Ex- chequer Bonds.</p>
        <p>Touring the Colonies with a good piece is always profitable. Colonials will pay well for first-class eutertainments, and show you it sharp- shooter if the show offends their anticipations.</p>
        <p>The ways of millionaires are devious but usually interesting. , Mr. Lyndon has spend Jong. time at:lCimberley in the diamond -fields 'o3vned by Mr. Barnato and Mr. J. B. Joel. When not fingering diamonds, these two gentlemen used to helf)Mr. Lyndon to produce Drury-lane dramas. "'They were splendid actors," observed Mr. Lyndon.. I shoujd think Mr. Joel would do well in "The Whip." His name is not dis- sociated from horses.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lyndon took the music halls by storm twenty-fours years ago, when he introduced the quick-change. turn. The additional advantage of being able to talk and sing like a lady aided his entertainment. He has also appeared in pastoral plays with Mrs. Patrick Campbell.</p>
        <p>Miss Grace Emery, who, with excellent under- standing of a generous and just Irish girl, takes the part of Mary, is the wiffr,of Mr: Charles Diekeni3- 4.(Bartlett McGrath). She has been playing leading parts for Air. Lodge-Percy, the author, for several years. Mr. Dickens is a descendant of the well-known Campbell family. His mother played with Irving for years. He is ably maintaining the traditions of his family in the histrionic art.</p>
        <p>The well-known ,actress, Miss Emma Litch- field, is bringing her ,company to the• Royal Court next week witil a play, "Home Once Mote." It has seven scenes, and has not been on the sage very long. It is one of "heart and home" interest.</p>
        <p>Hippodrome. Mr. W. A. JaclEson, the manager, has Wen the recipient of many felicitations for ,intro- ducing "From Pekin toParis." It is a neat revue, with pretentious fo picturesquehess and humour which are amply justified. The lead- ing parts are taken by interesting fyrtistes and the chorus is well on the "young and fresh" side. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Jackson Owen, the principal comedian, luny de'serves the large-sized poster on the hoardings. His humour is of the droll, almost sleepy, kind, but it gets there every time. He was here two years ago, playing a "La Poupee" Act, but, like many of our prominent comedians, answered the 'call of thee revue.</p>
        <p>Alth()ugh Mr. Owen now plays the telephone exchange:, episode with Mr. Arthur Gilbert, he .used to do so with Mr. Matt. Melrose, who has suspended his humorous inclinations on the stage while he runs revues and panto- mimes.</p>
        <p>I should like to congratulate Miss Edith Devonshire on her singing and Miss Lalla Mel- rose (Mrs. Melrose) on her pleasantries and dancing.</p>
        <p>Mr.. J. Cowl, who plays the part of "The. Bruiser in the telephone episode, celebrated his jubil&amp; on the stage last week. I think he holds the record for appearing in the highest number of performances in one week—nineteen, at the Prince Of Wales' Theatre, Glasgow. There were matinees and three performances nightly, starting at 6 o'clock. ' Afterwards there was .a run' on throat lozenges.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aubrey Standing, the operatic tenor singer, has created a splendid impression. He has a. fine presence and believes in linen— "Linen, "clean and plenty of it," as Beau Brummell used to say.</p>
        <p>Mr. Standing, who sang brilliantly in Eng- lish and Italian, has been associated with the Castellano Italian, Moody-Manners, and the O'Mara Opera Companies.</p>
        <p>Those revue stars, Charley Manny and Bob Roberts, top the bill at this house next week, and the others on the programme include The Shevernskys, comedians; Zakaree Ermakov (late of the Russian Secret Service), in Cossacks' warlike sports. The Kinemas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>Star Kinema.</head>
        <p>Star Kinema.</p>
        <p>A film with a fine patriotic flavour, entitled "A Soldier and a Man," was screened on the first three days of the week, and "The Dop Doctor" has drawn crowded houses during the latter half of the week. It will be shown at all three performances to-day (Saturday).</p>
        <p>On .Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday next the principal film is the production of the London Film Company; entitled "Mr. Lyndon at Liberty," featuring the well-known actor, Mr. Charles Rock, and full London company.</p>
        <p>"The Fatal Card," produced by the Famous Players Company from the great dramatic suc- cess by C". Haddon Chambers, will be screened on Thursday, Friday and Saturday. Special attractions to be shown very shortly at the Star include an adaptation of Ouida's charm- ing story, "Two Little Wooden Shoes," featur- ing Vivian Martin; also the ever-popular lady, Mary Pickford, in Madame Butterfly, as "Cho Cho San." Little Mary is absolutely great. Watch for further announcements next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>Picture House.</head>
        <p>The Picture House.</p>
        <p>" A Bride of Nancy Lee," and " Pine's Revenge," two fine dramas, have been presented at the Picture House this week, the former dur- ing the first half and the latter during the second. Both were brimful of excitement, and well in keeping with the first-rate pictures, which we always look forward to.</p>
        <p>For next week "A Model Wife" and "Out of the Ashes"' are the principal items, the former for the first half, and the latter for the second.. ,</p>
        <p>Liverpool Olympia. -The• management of the Olympia, Liverpool, announce that Mr. D. W. Griffith's wonderful film, "The Birth of a Nation," will be screened far the third week. This remarkable film has attracted big audiences, and those who have not already seen it should take the opportunity dur- ing the forthcoming week.</p>
        <p>FOYER.</p>
        <p>NO OUTSIDE INTERFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Guardians' Chairtnan Rebukes Poor Law Association.</p>
        <p>. A rebuke to the Poor taw Officers' Association was quietly administered by Mr. A. J. Jolley, chairman of the, Board of Guardians, at their meeting on Wednesday, The , Association had written pointing out that there had been great increases in the cost of living, and they -were writing to Boards of Guardians in the hope that they would grant extra remuneration so that there should be ho excessive hardships imposed on officials owing to the war. Whereupon, there seemed an iiclination on the part of the Board to let the letter lie on the table, , Mr. Jolley remarking, "Our relations with our officers have been quite satisfactory; and we don't want anyone to come rind tell us these things?' lie added that the Board had considered the cases of. their officials and they had been dealt with., The letter received had no application for them. The letter. was -allowed to lie on the table.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FAHE ALLOWED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>WOOD'S COLLEGE.</head>
        <p>WOOD'S COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>**%:°</p>
        <p>&lt;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>Alderman Bennett And The</head>
        <p>Alderman Bennett And The</p>
        <p>Dignity Of Work.</p>
        <p>The prizes and certificates gained by the students of Wood's College, Midland Bank Chambers, Bridge-street, were distributed on. TneSday evening in the Patten Hall. The Prin- cipal was supported by Mr. W. H. Woodcock (chairman), and Alderman Arthur Bennett, who presented the certificates. Mrs. F. Wilson Wood. was in attendance.</p>
        <p>The Chairman alluded to the expansion of the College, and warmly congratulated both students and staff on the large number of successes. secured during, the last few months. He paid a very high tribute to the success of Mr. F. Wood's unique methods of ithparting specialised commercial education, and reminded the students of the abnormal demand for young ladies to take the place of the men called to. the Colours. In responding to this "call," he considered that they were fulfilling a patriotic. duty. .</p>
        <p>The Principal read a short report, summaris- ing he growth and prOgress of the College since its inception. He rentarked that he largely attributed the success of his students to the splendid material the.staff had to work on. He considered that the College was fortunate in. receiving pupils who. had had such excellent groundwork and preliminary training in the Elementary and Secondary Schools in the War- rington district.</p>
        <p>Alderman Arthur Bennett, after alluding to the excellent work the institution was doing in the district, took for his theme the dignity of work, especially bearing upon the ever widening sphere of women's activities. He Wri shed the young people to ,bear constantly in mind, how- ever, that mere commercial qualifications did not constitute true education. They should aim at physical, mental and spiritual perfection—be- coming good citizens. He referred to the recent tercentenary of Shakespeare—speaking particu- larly in reference to this great poet's almost universal knowledge. of everything in the great world in which we live,Tand to some extent in the realms beyond it. He urged them not to be satisfied with merely an education which would bring them the necessities of life—but to read judiciously and broaden their outlook on life. At the conclusion of .the address Miss M. Woodward gave a short speed demonstration on the Underwood typewriter. Great interest was manifested by the audience in her performance, and at the conclusion she was the recipient of a choice box of chocolates from the students. The prizes and certificates (of which a summary is given below) were then presented by Alderman Bennett. During the evening Mrs. F. Wilson Wood, on behalf of the students, was presented with a beautiful bouquet of •red tulips and foliage adorned with red and green satin ribbon, (College colours). The meeting was brought to a Conclusion by a unanimous vote of thanks to Alderman Bennett and to the Chair- man, proposed by the Principal, and cordially endorsed by the enthusiastic audience. 'Accom- panist' at the piano Was Mr. Harold Shirtcliff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
