        <p>After Waterloo Britain enjoyed a long peace, brpken only by occasional 'Effie wars' affecting the more remote parts of the Empire. One of the most serious of these was in Afghinistan, where a savage rebellion in which many British had been massacred had to be avenged. The punitive expedition sent out did its work most effectively</p>
        <p>is very nice to be back at school once more, after enjoying a pleasant holiday, roaming I through the fields and woods. Work at school seems quite fresh again, and quite easy to do." Niece Lily Scarisbrick, 170, Battersby-lano. " I like holidays very much, but I also like ' going back to school. When we are on our holi- days we can go for many pleasant walks into Ithe country and have picnics in the woods. Then iwe all feel refreshed and ready to begin our school work. We do not mind if the weather is , wet when we are in school, but we always wish for fine weather at holiday times. Holidays are , few and far between, so we must make the best use of them, and go out as much as we can, and when we go back to school we will all enjoy our work and look forward to our next holiday and plan the things we will do when it comes:" • PROUD OF HER PRIZE. "44, Bewsey-road, Warrington. "Dear Uncle Jack,—l do not know how to thank you for your splendid book. I am very proud of it, and I thank you very much indeed. I took it to school on Monday, and my teitcher is also very proud that I should have won such a splendid prize. "With many thanks.—l remain, EDNA FELLOWS, Evelyn-sreet Council School." LIKES BOOKS OF ADVENTURE. Nephew Ernest William Sankey, 99, Kimber- ley-street, writes to thank me for his Prize and says :—" I like books wherp men go roving, because they are full of adventure." • NEW MEMBERS. Kitallerley-streeti. age 785 Joseph O'Brien, 10; Sacred Heart School.</p>
