        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL MAYtR,S, D.C.M. • Killed</p>
        <p>mine in which he was working. Volunteers were called for, and a sergeant having been wounded, Lance-Corporal Mayers volunteered, and placed powder in the mine, with which the enemy were blown out. At the commencement of hostilities, Lance- Corporal Mayers was working as a mine sinker in Bedfordshire, but the call to duty being strong to him, he enlisted in the Bedfordshires, and was eventually drafted to the Front. Sub- sequently he volunteered to go into the mining line of the 173rd Company of the Royal Engineers. • A memorial service will be conducted at the Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday even- ing, when the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) hopes to be present. RIFLEMAN J. GRIFFITHS. Returned As Killed After A Twelve Months' Silence. Rifleman Jam e 9 Griffiths, son of Mr. Thomas Griffiths, 6, Dickenson-street, Orford-lane, re-</p>
