        <p>ANGLING. NO LONGER A JOKE. "I suppose we will soon be hearing the joke about the big fish that got away." "My friend," replied the fisherman, " with food at present prices the fish that gets away is not a joke. It is a calamity." A conscientious objector has stated that he has given up angling on humanitarian grounds. We fear that his fish stories may have caused some fatal attacks of apoplexy among his audiences.—" Punch." WARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S ASSOCIATION. N.A. CENTRE. PROTECTING PIKE AND EELS. The close season does not give much scope for anglers in this district. The ruling body debarring us from taking eel and pike, we are tied only to the one specie, trout. Clubs locally have never advocated that eels and pike should not be taken from streams, where we know they are doing a very fair amount of destruction amongst the coarse fish, and where our members know they can be taken with a fair amount of ease. Take for instance such streams as the IGowy. This fishery is within easy reach of War. rington, and a favourite of local anglers. We ; know pike and eels are‘destroying coarse fish galore, however, we will leave it at that until ir such times as the F.F.P.L. see their way to make amends. • THE GOWY. Mr. F. Frigger holds first place for trout in , the Warrington N.A. from here. We cannot boast of great catches, but still it is the best of our waters to spend a week-end. Chester N.A. have something better to show, but then they are considerably nearer, and can fish the stream after leaving work any evening. There is no doubt the Mill Race is the best spot for the trout angler, as the majority of trout can be traced from this length for years:- There- may be odd ones to be picked up in the holes of the back brook, but not in any such quantity as the Mill stream, or even behind the Mill. TO MEET A TRYING TIME. Club night at Headquarters on Monday next. fembers please note that cards have been issued some weeks ago, and members are not entitled to use their old ones, and must produce their 1916 cards when challenged by any member. Members must also be in benefit before they are eligible to fish in any N.A.,waters. The Society must take these steps to keep the waters, as a considerable number of members are now in His Majesty's, ,Forces, and it is necessary for those who are still at home to keep their sub- scriptions up for the benefit of their fellow members as well as themselves. It is not much we are asking for, as we are only paying for our own sport, and if members will only keep their cards paid up to date we will be able to pull through this trying time without asking for assistance. A. S.</p>
